var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"api/polarization-math/#polarization-math","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"Supported polarization representations are Jones, Stokes, Mueller, coherency-matrix, and Poincaré axis–angle. A narrow conversion API (jones, stokes, mueller, delta_phi, coherency, azimuth_ellipticity) is decoded by multiple dispatch, and * is overloaded so operators act on states — and on one another — across formalisms, returning the natural result type.","title":"PolarizationMath"},{"category":"section","location":"api/polarization-math/#Convention","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"All constructors, conversions, and operators lift through MonteCarloMeasurements.Particles; the sole exception is jones(::MuellerMatrix) (the Cloude coherency-eigenvector method), which nominalizes Particles before its eigendecomposition.\n\nCanonical fully polarized states (H, V, D, A, R, L) and ideal optical elements (linear_polarizer, retarder, qwp, hwp) live in the Canonical submodule; bring them into scope with using Bifrost.PolarizationMath.Canonical.\n\nState JonesVector StokesVector (S1,S2,S3) DeltaPhiState (θ,ϕ) AzimuthEllipticity (ψ,χ)\nHorizontal (1, 0) (+1, 0, 0) (0, ϕ), ϕ irrelevant (0, 0)\nVertical (0, 1) (−1, 0, 0) (π/2, ϕ), ϕ irrelevant (π/2, 0)\n+45° linear (1, 1)/√2 (0, +1, 0) (π/4, 0) (π/4, 0)\n−45° linear (1, −1)/√2 (0, −1, 0) (π/4, π) (−π/4, 0)\nRight circular (1, −i)/√2 (0, 0, +1) (π/4, +π/2) (0, +π/4)\nLeft circular (1, i)/√2 (0, 0, −1) (π/4, −π/2) (0, −π/4)","title":"Convention"},{"category":"section","location":"api/polarization-math/#Validity-checks","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"Each representation enforces what physically constrains it, and nothing more:\n\nStokesVector requires S0 ≥ √(S1²+S2²+S3²) (degree of polarization ≤ 1).\nCoherencyMatrix requires a Hermitian, positive-semidefinite matrix.\nJonesVector is unconstrained: any nonzero complex 2-vector is a valid fully polarized state, and its magnitude carries power, so normalization is not forced.\nJones and Mueller matrices are not required to be unitary — lossy elements such as linear_polarizer are valid by design. Pass the option :is_unitary to a matrix constructor (JonesMatrix(m, :is_unitary), likewise MuellerMatrix and CoherencyMatrix) to test unitarity opt-in: it returns the matrix when unitary and false otherwise.\nActing a MuellerMatrix on a state enforces the Cloude criterion: the 4×4 Hermitian coherency matrix associated with M must be positive semidefinite. This rejects physically unrealizable Mueller matrices while still allowing a polarizer to raise the degree of polarization of unpolarized light.","title":"Validity checks"},{"category":"module","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"PolarizationMath\n\nJones, Stokes, Mueller, coherency, and Poincaré representations of polarization.\n\nConventions\n\nThis is the single statement of Bifrost's optical conventions. The fiber generators (Bifrost.FiberPath), the docs, and the tests cite it rather than restating it.\n\nTime dependence e^{−iωt}. A Jones vector (Ex, Ey) holds the complex amplitudes of the real field Re[(Ex, Ey) e^{i(kz − ωt)}]. Propagating a distance s with propagation constant β multiplies an amplitude by e^{+iβs}, so a slow (higher-β) axis gains phase relative to a fast one.\nHandedness is judged looking toward the source (Born & Wolf, Principles of Optics, 7th ed., §1.4): right-circular light rotates clockwise. Hence R() = [1, −i]/√2 and L() = [1, i]/√2.\nStokes parameters, with S3 = I_R − I_L:\nS0 = |Ex|² + |Ey|²        S1 = |Ex|² − |Ey|²\nS2 = 2 Re(Ex Ey*)         S3 = +2 Im(Ex Ey*)\nRight-circular light is the Poincaré north pole: S3 = +1, ellipticity +π/4.\nField-defined parameters. AzimuthEllipticity and DeltaPhiState are defined by the real field, so their parameters do not depend on the time sign.\nOther references. A reference using e^{+iωt} (pypolar's default, for one) writes every Jones vector and matrix as the complex conjugate of the one here; Stokes and Mueller quantities agree.\n\nEnforced by the T-PHYSICS tests \"handedness from the real field's rotation\" and \"DeltaPhiState canonical values\" (test/test_polarization_math.jl) and by the quarter-wave fiber test in test/test_birefringence_signs.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.PAULI","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"PAULI\n\nStandard Float64 PauliMatrices: PAULI.I, PAULI.X, PAULI.Y, PAULI.Z.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.PAULI"},{"category":"type","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.AzimuthEllipticity","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"AzimuthEllipticity(azimuth, ellipticity)\n\nPure state described by the polarization ellipse: azimuth ψ (orientation of the major axis from horizontal) and ellipticity χ, with the Poincaré mapping longitude 2ψ and latitude 2χ. Right-circular has χ = +π/4.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.AzimuthEllipticity"},{"category":"type","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.CoherencyMatrix","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"CoherencyMatrix((Jxx, Jxy, Jyx, Jyy))\nCoherencyMatrix(m, :is_unitary)\n\n2×2 Hermitian coherency (polarization density) matrix stored row-major, Jₐᵦ = ⟨Eₐ Eᵦ*⟩. The bridge between Jones states and partially polarized Stokes vectors: a rank-1 matrix is a pure state, lower rank is partial.\n\nWith the option :is_unitary the constructor returns the matrix when it is unitary (ρ†ρ = I, which for a Hermitian positive-semidefinite coherency matrix means ρ = I) and false otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.CoherencyMatrix"},{"category":"type","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.DeltaPhiState","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"DeltaPhiState(θ, ϕ)\n\nPure polarization state in the H/V Jones basis with global phase fixed so that the H component is real and non-negative:\n\n|ψ⟩ = cos(θ)|H⟩ + exp(−im*ϕ) sin(θ)|V⟩.\n\nUse this when you want a minimal, physically interpretable parameterization of a pure polarization state. The real parameter θ sets the H/V amplitude ratio, while the real parameter ϕ is the phase lead of the V oscillation over the H oscillation: the real field is (cos θ cos(ωt), sin θ cos(ωt + ϕ)). ϕ is defined by the physical field, not by the Jones amplitudes, so under the e^{−iωt} convention of Bifrost.PolarizationMath ϕ = arg(EH) − arg(EV), and ϕ = +π/2 (V leads H by a quarter period) is right-circular.\n\nθ ∈ [0, π/2] is enforced. ϕ may be any real number.\n\nAt θ = 0 or θ = π/2, ϕ is physically undefined (it multiplies a zero component) but is still stored as given.\n\nThe corresponding normalized Stokes vector is\n\n(S1, S2, S3) =\n    (cos(2θ), sin(2θ)cos(ϕ), sin(2θ)sin(ϕ)),\n\nusing the file-header convention S3 = +2 Im(Ex Ey*). Hence 2θ is the polar angle from the S1 axis on the Poincaré sphere, and ϕ is the azimuth about that axis.\n\nAngle conventions across this module agree for linear states: a DeltaPhiState with ϕ = 0 is the linear state whose physical axis lies at angle θ from horizontal, so DeltaPhiState(θ, 0) equals the state transmitted by Canonical.linear_polarizer(θ) and the fast-axis eigenpolarization of Canonical.retarder(θ, δ). Note the angles are not the same kind of object: here θ is an amplitude angle whose Poincaré polar angle is 2θ, whereas the θ of linear_polarizer/retarder is a physical-space azimuth; the two coincide numerically only on the linear (ϕ = 0) equator. The state phase ϕ is likewise distinct from the retarder retardation δ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.DeltaPhiState"},{"category":"type","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.JonesMatrix","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"JonesMatrix(m11, m12, m21, m22)\nJonesMatrix((m11, m12, m21, m22))\nJonesMatrix(m, :is_unitary)\n\n2×2 complex Jones operator stored row-major.\n\nWith the option :is_unitary the constructor returns the matrix when it is unitary (J†J = I) and false otherwise. Unitarity is opt-in, not enforced, because lossy elements are valid Jones matrices: Canonical.linear_polarizer is a singular, non-unitary projector by design.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.JonesMatrix"},{"category":"type","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.JonesVector","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"JonesVector(Ex, Ey)\nJonesVector((Ex, Ey))\n\nFully polarized state stored as complex field amplitudes (Ex, Ey).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.JonesVector"},{"category":"type","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.MuellerMatrix","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"MuellerMatrix((m11, …, m44))\nMuellerMatrix(m, :is_unitary)\n\n4×4 real Mueller operator stored row-major as a length-16 tuple.\n\nWith the option :is_unitary the constructor returns the matrix when it is unitary — for a real Mueller matrix, orthogonal (MᵀM = I), the image of a lossless retarder — and false otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.MuellerMatrix"},{"category":"type","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.PauliMatrices","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"PauliMatrices{T}\n\nThe four 2×2 Pauli matrices (I, X, Y, Z), each stored row-major as (m11, m12, m21, m22). The constant PAULI holds the standard Float64 set. The Stokes ordering (I, Z, X, Y) used by the Jones↔Mueller and Jones↔Stokes maps is available through the internal accessor used by mueller.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.PauliMatrices"},{"category":"type","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.PolOperator","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"PolOperator\n\nAbstract supertype for polarization operators (JonesMatrix, MuellerMatrix).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.PolOperator"},{"category":"type","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.PolState","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"PolState\n\nAbstract supertype for polarization states (JonesVector, StokesVector, DeltaPhiState, AzimuthEllipticity, CoherencyMatrix).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.PolState"},{"category":"type","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.StokesVector","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"StokesVector(S0, S1, S2, S3)\nStokesVector((S0, S1, S2, S3))\n\nStokes vector (S0, S1, S2, S3). Partial polarization is allowed; the constructor requires S0 ≥ √(S1² + S2² + S3²).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.StokesVector"},{"category":"method","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.axis_angle_to_mueller-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"axis_angle_to_mueller(axis, angle) -> MuellerMatrix\n\nMueller matrix of a pure retarder: a rotation by angle about the unit Poincaré axis axis = (n1, n2, n3). Inverse of mueller_to_axis_angle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.axis_angle_to_mueller"},{"category":"method","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.azimuth-Tuple{Any}","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"azimuth(x)\n\nPolarization-ellipse azimuth ψ = ½ atan(S2, S1) (radians) of a state x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.azimuth"},{"category":"method","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.azimuth_ellipticity-Tuple{AzimuthEllipticity}","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"azimuth_ellipticity(x)\n\nReturn the AzimuthEllipticity of a state x, computed from its Stokes vector via ψ = ½ atan(S2, S1) and χ = ½ asin(S3 / √(S1²+S2²+S3²)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.azimuth_ellipticity"},{"category":"method","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.coherency-Tuple{CoherencyMatrix}","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"coherency(x)\n\nReturn the CoherencyMatrix ρ of a state x: ρ = |E⟩⟨E| for a Jones vector and ρ = ½ Σ Sᵢ σᵢ for a Stokes vector (so stokes(coherency(s)) == s).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.coherency"},{"category":"method","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.delta_phi-Tuple{DeltaPhiState}","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"delta_phi(x)\n\nReturn the DeltaPhiState (θ, ϕ) of a pure state x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.delta_phi"},{"category":"method","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.docp-Tuple{Any}","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"docp(x)\n\nDegree of circular polarization S3 / S0 (signed; positive for right-circular).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.docp"},{"category":"method","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.dolp-Tuple{Any}","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"dolp(x)\n\nDegree of linear polarization √(S1²+S2²) / S0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.dolp"},{"category":"method","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.dop-Tuple{Any}","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"dop(x)\n\nDegree of polarization √(S1²+S2²+S3²) / S0 of a state x (0 unpolarized, 1 fully polarized).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.dop"},{"category":"method","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.ellipticity-Tuple{Any}","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"ellipticity(x)\n\nPolarization-ellipse ellipticity angle χ = ½ asin(S3/‖S‖) (radians) of x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.ellipticity"},{"category":"method","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.jones-Tuple{JonesVector}","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"jones(x)\n\nReturn the JonesVector (for states) or JonesMatrix (for operators) of x. Defined for pure states only: converting a partially polarized StokesVector or non-rank-1 CoherencyMatrix throws an ArgumentError. For a nondepolarizing MuellerMatrix the Jones matrix is reconstructed by the Cloude coherency-eigenvector method.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.jones"},{"category":"method","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.mueller-Tuple{MuellerMatrix}","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"mueller(x)\n\nReturn the MuellerMatrix of an operator. For a JonesMatrix J it is Mᵢⱼ = ½ Tr(σᵢ J σⱼ J†) in the Stokes Pauli basis, valid for non-unitary J (e.g. polarizers).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.mueller"},{"category":"method","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.mueller_to_axis_angle-Tuple{MuellerMatrix}","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"mueller_to_axis_angle(M) -> (axis, angle)\n\nRotation axis (n1, n2, n3) on the Poincaré sphere and rotation angle of a pure-retarder Mueller matrix M. Inverse of axis_angle_to_mueller. Throws an ArgumentError if M is not a proper rotation (depolarizing or diattenuating). Validity checks nominalize Particles; the returned axis and angle carry the full ensemble. At θ = 0 (the identity rotation) the axis is undefined and its components come back NaN; angle is still exactly 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.mueller_to_axis_angle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.polarized_part-Tuple{Any}","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"polarized_part(x)\n\nThe fully polarized StokesVector in the decomposition of x into a fully polarized plus an unpolarized part.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.polarized_part"},{"category":"method","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.polarized_power-Tuple{Any}","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"polarized_power(x)\n\nPower carried by the fully polarized part, dop(x) · S0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.polarized_power"},{"category":"method","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.stokes-Tuple{StokesVector}","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"stokes(x)\n\nReturn the StokesVector of any state x, with S3 = +2 Im(Ex Ey*) (see Bifrost.PolarizationMath for the conventions).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.stokes"},{"category":"method","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.total_power-Tuple{Any}","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"total_power(x)\n\nTotal optical power S0 of a state x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.total_power"},{"category":"method","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.unpolarized_part-Tuple{Any}","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"unpolarized_part(x)\n\nThe unpolarized StokesVector (S0 − p, 0, 0, 0) in the decomposition of x, where p is the polarized power.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.unpolarized_part"},{"category":"method","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.unpolarized_power-Tuple{Any}","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"unpolarized_power(x)\n\nPower carried by the unpolarized part, (1 − dop(x)) · S0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.unpolarized_power"},{"category":"function","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.unpolarized_stokes_vector","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"unpolarized_stokes_vector(power = 1)\n\nFully unpolarized StokesVector (power, 0, 0, 0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.unpolarized_stokes_vector"},{"category":"module","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.Canonical","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"Canonical\n\nCanonical fully polarized states (H, V, D, A, R, L) and ideal optical elements (linear_polarizer, retarder, qwp, hwp) as JonesVectors and JonesMatrixes. Bring them into scope with using Bifrost.PolarizationMath.Canonical.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.Canonical"},{"category":"method","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.Canonical.A-Tuple{}","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"A()\n\nAnti-diagonal (−45°) linear polarization, [1, −1]/√2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.Canonical.A"},{"category":"method","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.Canonical.D-Tuple{}","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"D()\n\nDiagonal (+45°) linear polarization, [1, 1]/√2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.Canonical.D"},{"category":"method","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.Canonical.H-Tuple{}","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"H()\n\nHorizontal linear polarization, [1, 0].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.Canonical.H"},{"category":"method","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.Canonical.L-Tuple{}","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"L()\n\nLeft-circular polarization, [1, i]/√2 (Stokes S3 = −1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.Canonical.L"},{"category":"method","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.Canonical.R-Tuple{}","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"R()\n\nRight-circular polarization, [1, −i]/√2 (Stokes S3 = +1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.Canonical.R"},{"category":"method","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.Canonical.V-Tuple{}","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"V()\n\nVertical linear polarization, [0, 1].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.Canonical.V"},{"category":"method","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.Canonical.hwp-Tuple{Real}","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"hwp(θ)\n\nHalf-wave plate (retardation π) with fast axis at angle θ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.Canonical.hwp"},{"category":"method","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.Canonical.linear_polarizer-Tuple{Real}","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"linear_polarizer(θ)\n\nJones matrix of an ideal linear polarizer with transmission axis at angle θ from horizontal: the projector [cos²θ  cosθ sinθ; cosθ sinθ  sin²θ]. The axis angle matches the linear-state convention of Bifrost.PolarizationMath.DeltaPhiState: the transmitted state is DeltaPhiState(θ, 0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.Canonical.linear_polarizer"},{"category":"method","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.Canonical.qwp-Tuple{Real}","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"qwp(θ)\n\nQuarter-wave plate (retardation π/2) with fast axis at angle θ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.Canonical.qwp"},{"category":"method","location":"api/polarization-math/#Bifrost.PolarizationMath.Canonical.retarder-Tuple{Real, Real}","page":"PolarizationMath","text":"retarder(θ, δ)\n\nJones matrix of a linear retarder with fast axis at angle θ from horizontal and retardation δ (the slow axis lags the fast axis by δ: in the e^{−iωt} convention it gains phase e^{+iδ/2} and the fast axis e^{−iδ/2}). The global phase is fixed so zero retardation is the identity. The fast-axis angle matches the linear-state convention of Bifrost.PolarizationMath.DeltaPhiState: DeltaPhiState(θ, 0) is the fast-axis eigenpolarization. The retardation δ is an element property, not the state phase ϕ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PolarizationMath.Canonical.retarder"},{"category":"section","location":"api/plots/#plots","page":"Plots","text":"Opt-in developer diagnostics, loaded with using Bifrost.Plots. These record data for a human to inspect and render nothing themselves. For figures, load Plots.jl alongside Bifrost (plot(fiber), plot(dgd(results))) or PlotlyBase for inspect(path).","title":"Plots"},{"category":"type","location":"api/plots/#Bifrost.Plots.StepRecord","page":"Plots","text":"StepRecord\n\nOne trial step of the adaptive step-doubling controller: the position it was attempted from, the step size tried, the phase-insensitive error between the full step and two half steps, the tolerance that error was judged against, and whether the step was accepted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Plots.StepRecord"},{"category":"method","location":"api/plots/#Bifrost.Plots.collect_adaptive_steps-Tuple{Any, Float64, Float64, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Plots","text":"collect_adaptive_steps(K, s0, s1, J0; kwargs...) -> (J_final, records)\n\nPropagate J0 from s0 to s1 under generator K, recording every trial step — accepted and rejected — as a StepRecord.\n\nMirrors the production step controller (same error metric, same safety and growth/shrink factors) without modifying it, so the returned J_final matches an ordinary propagate_interval! over the same interval while records exposes the step history the solver otherwise discards.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Plots.collect_adaptive_steps"},{"category":"section","location":"api/path-geometry/#path-geometry-api","page":"PathGeometry","text":"Three-dimensional path authoring and differential geometry: a PathBuilder of straight, bend, catenary and helix segments and jumpto! connectors with spin! runs, built into a Path for queries, material spin and twist phases, sampling, and global path diagnostics. The transverse frame exposed by bishop_e1/bishop_e2/bishop_frame is the parallel-transported (Bishop) pair. This module also carries the per-segment metadata vocabulary (Nickname, MetaAdd, MetaMul). The Theory section covers the integral geometric quantities (turning angle, torsion, material twist) and the frame convention.","title":"PathGeometry"},{"category":"type","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.TwistRate","page":"PathGeometry","text":"TwistRate\n\nType of a per-segment mechanical-twist rate specification: nothing (no twist) or a Real constant (rad/m, including an MCM Particles). Mechanical twist is a constant rate per segment; approximate a varying profile with consecutive constant-twist segments.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.TwistRate"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._ARC_BOUNDARY_TOL","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_ARC_BOUNDARY_TOL\n\nAbsolute slack (metres) within which an arc length sitting at a segment boundary is attributed to the segment on its near side.\n\nDeliberately tight, and for the opposite reason to _ARC_DOMAIN_TOL: at an interior boundary there is a competing interpretation — the next segment — and a caller who probes s_joint + δ means the far side of the joint. A boundary tolerance as loose as the domain tolerance would silently steal those queries and answer with the previous segment's geometry (a discontinuous quantity such as the bend axis then jumps by a finite angle, not an epsilon). It need only swamp the ~1e-16 relative round-off that the differing sums above introduce, which at metre scale it does by ~1e4.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._ARC_BOUNDARY_TOL"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._ARC_DOMAIN_TOL","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_ARC_DOMAIN_TOL\n\nAbsolute slack (metres) allowed either side of a path's arc-length domain [0, L] before a query is rejected as off the path. Arc lengths reach a routing site through independent running sums — the segment table's entry offsets u0, a caller's own sum of segment lengths, and the ensemble remap's u_j = u0_j + τ·(u - u0_c) — whose groupings differ, so a coordinate meant for an endpoint can arrive a few ulps outside it.\n\nDeliberately loose: past the end of the fiber there is no competing interpretation of the query, so admitting a coordinate a nanometre out costs nothing, and the alternative is rejecting a caller who reached s_end by a different sum. Contrast _ARC_BOUNDARY_TOL, which must stay tight for the opposite reason. Absolute, not relative: fibers here run 1e-2–1e3 m.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._ARC_DOMAIN_TOL"},{"category":"type","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.AbstractMeta","page":"PathGeometry","text":"AbstractMeta\n\nSupertype for per-segment annotations carried in a segment's meta::Vector{AbstractMeta} bag.\n\npath-geometry.jl is deliberately ignorant of what the bag contains; downstream layers define their own AbstractMeta subtypes (see path-geometry-meta.jl) and decide how to act on them. The geometry layer does not interpret any AbstractMeta directly (spin is authored with spin!, not meta — see the file-header \"Spin\" section).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.AbstractMeta"},{"category":"type","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.AbstractPathSegment","page":"PathGeometry","text":"AbstractPathSegment\n\nSupertype for all local-frame path segment primitives (straight, bend, catenary, helix, and the resolved cubic connector).\n\nEach concrete segment defines its geometry in its own local frame, starting at the origin with tangent along local +z. The build placement loop rotates and chains these local descriptions into the global frame.\n\nImplementation\n\nA concrete segment must implement the following, where local arc length is s ∈ [0, arc_length(seg)]. The return element type T follows the segment's own type parameter (Float64 for deterministic segments, Particles for MCM-valued fields):\n\nMethod Returns\narc_length(seg) T\ncurvature(seg, s) T (κ, 1/m)\ngeometric_torsion(seg, s) T (τ_geom, rad/m)\nposition_local(seg, s) length-3 AbstractVector{T}\ntangent_local(seg, s) unit length-3 AbstractVector{T}\n_curvature_vector_local(seg, s) length-3 AbstractVector{T} (k⃗ = dT/ds)\n_parallel_transport_local(seg, v, s) length-3 AbstractVector{T}\n_normal_local(seg, s) unit length-3 AbstractVector{T} (internal construction axes)\n_binormal_local(seg, s) unit length-3 AbstractVector{T} (internal construction axes)\nend_position_local(seg) length-3 AbstractVector{T}\n_end_frame_local(seg) (T, N, B), each length-3 AbstractVector{T}\n\n_normal_local/_binormal_local/_end_frame_local are internal geometric construction axes (they orient axis_angle, the placement chaining, and the connector boundary data). The public transverse frame returned by bishop_e1/bishop_e2 is the parallel-transported (Bishop) frame built from _parallel_transport_local.\n\nJumpTo is unresolved: it resolves at build time into a CubicConnector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.AbstractPathSegment"},{"category":"type","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.BendSegment","page":"PathGeometry","text":"BendSegment(radius, angle, axis_angle)\n\nCircular arc of radius radius (m) sweeping angle (rad) in the plane whose inward normal is at axis_angle (rad) from the local N-axis.\n\nIn the local frame (local z = incoming tangent, local x = incoming normal, local y = incoming binormal), the inward normal direction is:     n̂ = cos(axisangle)·x̂ + sin(axisangle)·ŷ\n\nCurvature κ = 1 / radius.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.BendSegment"},{"category":"type","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.BuildSiteError","page":"PathGeometry","text":"BuildSiteError(index, segment, stage, cause)\n\nA failure while building one authored segment of a PathBuilder, naming the segment. index is its position among the builder's segments (its row in the built Path), segment the authored segment, stage what was being done to it, and cause the exception raised there. stage is one of\n\n:field_meta — applying its field-level MetaAdd/MetaMul (build);\n:placement — resolving it where it is placed, e.g. a jumpto! connector solve (build);\n:strain — scaling it by its :T_K/:tension strain (the fiber layer's Fiber).\n\nshowerror prints BuildSiteError: while building segment 3 (BendSegment \"paddle-1\"), applying field meta: followed by the cause's own message; see segment_label.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.BuildSiteError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.CatenarySegment","page":"PathGeometry","text":"CatenarySegment(a, length, axis_angle)\n\nA catenary curve in the plane whose horizontal direction is at axis_angle from the local N-axis.  a (m) is the catenary parameter (a = T₀/(ρg), the ratio of horizontal tension to weight per unit length).\n\nThe segment starts with T(0) = ẑ (framework invariant).  The curvature is maximal (κ = 1/a) at the arc-length midpoint s = length/2 (the vertex), giving a symmetric curvature profile; the vertex tangent is not ẑ.  The fiber deflects in the −n̂ direction (where n̂ = [cos(axisangle), sin(axisangle), 0]) as s increases.  Curvature: κ(s) = a / (a² + (s − length/2)²).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.CatenarySegment"},{"category":"type","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.CubicConnector","page":"PathGeometry","text":"CubicConnector <: AbstractPathSegment\n\nResolved form of a JumpTo segment: a cubic Hermite curve\n\nr(ξ) = a₀ + a₁ ξ + a₂ ξ² + a₃ ξ³,   ξ ∈ [0,1]\n\nin 3D that matches position and tangent direction at both endpoints (G1 continuity); endpoint curvature is left free. Implements the AbstractPathSegment local-geometry interface. All connector geometry is deterministic Float64 — connectors are only ever solved from scalar boundary data.\n\nFields\n\na: 6 × 3 coefficient matrix; row i stores the coefficient of ξⁱ⁻¹. The curve is cubic, so the quartic and quintic rows (5 and 6) are always zero.\nlambda: chosen handle scale.\ns_table: arc-length lookup, where s_table[i] is the arc length from u = 0 to u = (i-1)/(n-1).\ne1_table: n × 3 parallel-transport lookup; row i holds the local components of the transported e1 at u = (i-1)/(n-1), with e1_table[1, :] = x̂ (the entry transverse axis). Built by discrete double-reflection transport along the same u-grid as s_table.\ntwist: mechanical-twist rate (rad/m) carried from the originating JumpTo (see TwistRate).\nmeta: per-segment annotation bag (see AbstractMeta).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.CubicConnector"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.CubicConnector-Tuple{AbstractMatrix{<:Real}, Real, AbstractVector{<:Real}}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"CubicConnector(a, lambda, s_table; twist = nothing, meta = AbstractMeta[])\n\nConstruct a CubicConnector from a precomputed coefficient matrix a, handle scale lambda, and arc-length table s_table. The parallel-transport e1_table is computed here by discrete double-reflection over the s_table u-grid.\n\ntwist is the connector's mechanical-twist rate (see TwistRate). Callers normally obtain a/s_table from the internal connector builders rather than calling this directly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.CubicConnector"},{"category":"type","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.HelixSegment","page":"PathGeometry","text":"HelixSegment(radius, pitch, turns, axis_angle)\n\nA helix whose entry tangent is ẑ (the incoming sliding-frame tangent), ensuring continuity with the prior segment.  axis_angle (rad) selects which transverse direction n̂ = cos(axisangle)·x̂ + sin(axisangle)·ŷ the helix curves toward.\n\nThe helix axis â = (h·ẑ + R·n̂) / ℓ' is tilted from the transverse plane by arctan(h/R) toward ẑ, where h = pitch/(2π) and ℓ' = √(R²+h²).  This tilt is the geometric consequence of demanding tangent(0) = ẑ: a zero-pitch helix reduces to a circular arc (BendSegment) in the n̂ direction.\n\nκ(s) = R / (R² + h²)          (constant)\nτ_geom(s) = h / (R² + h²)     (constant)\narc_length = turns · 2π · √(R² + h²)\n\nLocal-frame basis vectors:     n̂  = [cos(axisangle), sin(axisangle), 0]     (toward helix axis)     r̂₀ = [-sin(axisangle), cos(axisangle), 0]    (outward radial at s=0)     ê_φ = (R·ẑ - h·n̂) / ℓ'                        (tangential at s=0, ⊥ axis)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.HelixSegment"},{"category":"type","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.JumpTo","page":"PathGeometry","text":"JumpTo <: AbstractPathSegment\n\nConnector to a global target, resolved at build time into a cubic G1 Hermite CubicConnector.\n\nThe connector leaves the current position along the current tangent and lands at the global point. incoming_tangent is the global tangent direction at point; if nothing, the chord direction is used. The connector matches position and tangent at both ends (G1); endpoint curvature is left free. Both point and incoming_tangent are in the global frame, so a connector's target does not move when the segments before it change shape.\n\nmin_bend_radius (metres, nothing = unconstrained) sets a lower bound on the connector's radius of curvature: the handle scale λ grows beyond the chord default (with bisection refinement) until κ ≤ 1/Rmin. `targetlength(metres, nothing = free) fixes the connector's arc length instead, while it still lands atpoint`; with both set, λ is chosen by arc length and the radius limit is validated afterwards.\n\nUnlike every other segment, a JumpTo with target_length = nothing and no strain meta of its own has no authored nominal length: its arc length is whatever the boundary-value solve needs to span the gap between wherever upstream placement actually lands and the fixed point. That is deliberate — the connector exists to absorb upstream geometry (bend radius, thermal expansion, tension) without the downstream target drifting, so its length is expected to change as upstream segments do. A connector that should instead hold a fixed physical length states one, either directly via target_length or by carrying its own :T_K/:tension meta, which re-solves it to that length scaled by its own strain factor.\n\ntwist is the connector's mechanical-twist rate (see TwistRate), carried onto the resolved connector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.JumpTo"},{"category":"type","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.MetaAdd","page":"PathGeometry","text":"MetaAdd(symbol, distribution)\n\nAttach an additive meta annotation to symbol.\n\nConsumers combine matching entries as baseline * product(mul) + sum(add); MetaAdd contributes to the additive sum. The payload may be any object the consumer knows how to apply, including a plain scalar, Particles, or another distribution-like value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.MetaAdd"},{"category":"type","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.MetaMul","page":"PathGeometry","text":"MetaMul(symbol, distribution)\n\nAttach a multiplicative meta annotation to symbol.\n\nConsumers combine matching entries as baseline * product(mul) + sum(add); MetaMul contributes a direct scale factor to the multiplicative product. The payload may be any object the consumer knows how to apply, including a plain scalar, Particles, or another distribution-like value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.MetaMul"},{"category":"type","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.Nickname","page":"PathGeometry","text":"Nickname(label)\n\nAttach a human-readable label to a path segment or a whole path.\n\nPlotting and demo code use this metadata for display labels. The geometry builder stores it in the meta bag and otherwise leaves interpretation to consuming layers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.Nickname"},{"category":"type","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.Path","page":"PathGeometry","text":"Path\n\nBuilt form of a PathBuilder: its placed segments and the per-segment table that the geometry queries, the generators, the ensemble and breakpoints all read. Returned by build(::PathBuilder).\n\nThe columns are parallel arrays with one row per authored segment, in build order, zero-length segments included, never reordered or filtered after build: row c is authored segment c, and that index addresses the same authored segment in every lane scalarized from one model (see fiber/fiber-ensemble.jl).\n\nseg — the PlacedSegment, connectors resolved.\nu0, L0 — the segment's entry arc length and its (strained) arc length, L0[c] == arc_length(seg[c].segment). The finite rows tile [0, s_end] gap-free; a zero-length row is never routed to.\ntwist_entry — accumulated mechanical-twist phase at the segment entry.\nspin_run — the row's spin run, numbered 1, 2, … along the path; consecutive rows share a run.\nspin_rate — the run's authored rate: nothing, a Float64, or a Function of run-local material arc length.\nspin_phi0 — accumulated spin phase at the run start.\nspin_material0, spin_dmds — the material (unstrained) coordinate at the row entry, measured from the run start, and the slope dm/ds = 1/F for the row's glass strain factor F (1.0 unstrained). The spin phase at local arc length sl is spin_phi0 + ∫₀ᵐ rate with m = spin_material0 + spin_dmds·sl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.Path"},{"category":"type","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.PathBuilder","page":"PathGeometry","text":"PathBuilder(; point = (0.0, 0.0, 0.0), outgoing_tangent = (0.0, 0.0, 1.0),\n              meta = AbstractMeta[]) -> PathBuilder\n\nCreate an empty path that starts at the global point heading along the global outgoing_tangent. Append segments with straight!, bend!, helix!, catenary! and the jumpto! connector, and open material-spin runs with spin!; the path ends at the exit of its last segment. meta annotates the whole path (e.g. a Nickname).\n\nA PathBuilder is both the authoring target and the authored spec: build places it into a Path, Fiber(pb; cross_section, T_ref_K) binds it to a cross section, and FiberModel(pb; cross_section, T_ref_K) copies it into a model. Authored values may be MCM Particles in a model; build itself is scalar-only.\n\nExample\n\npb = PathBuilder()\nstraight!(pb; length = 0.5)\nspin!(pb; rate = 2π)\nbend!(pb; radius = 0.05, angle = π / 2)\npath = build(pb)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.PathBuilder"},{"category":"type","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.PathSample","page":"PathGeometry","text":"PathSample\n\nDense samples of a built Path over [s_start, s_end].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.PathSample"},{"category":"type","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.PlacedSegment","page":"PathGeometry","text":"PlacedSegment\n\nA segment together with its placement in the global frame: one row of a Path, whose u0 column holds the segment's entry arc length.\n\nRecords the segment, its global start origin, the 3×3 placement_frame whose columns are [N, B, T] and which transforms local vectors to global as v_g = placement_frame * v_l, bishop_e1 — the global transported (Bishop) e1 at the segment's entry, advanced segment-to-segment by _parallel_transport_local in the build placement loop — and bishop_e1_local, that entry e1 in the construction frame (placement_frame' * bishop_e1), which every transported-frame query starts from. Static arrays, so a query allocates nothing.\n\nplacement_frame is the chained geometric construction frame, distinct from the transported frame carried by bishop_e1 (the two coincide only at the path start). It is not the public bishop_frame(path, s) API. The construction frame is kept because the path-authoring DSL depends on it:\n\naxis_angle is interpreted relative to the incoming local N/B construction axes;\nbend!, helix! and catenary! placement rely on those axes;\nconnector boundary data are expressed in that local construction frame;\nkeeping this machinery means positions and tangents remain bit-identical to the old build logic.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.PlacedSegment"},{"category":"type","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.Sample","page":"PathGeometry","text":"Sample\n\nOne evaluated point on a path: arc-length coordinate s plus all frame quantities (position, tangent/bishope1/bishope2, curvature, geometric torsion, spin rate).\n\nbishop_e1/bishop_e2 are the parallel-transported (Bishop) pair — continuous along the whole path, zero twist about the tangent; see bishop_e1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.Sample"},{"category":"type","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.StraightSegment","page":"PathGeometry","text":"StraightSegment{T,W} <: AbstractPathSegment\n\nStraight line segment of signed, nonzero length along the local tangent; W is the type of its mechanical-twist rate (see TwistRate).\n\nA negative length walks backward along the local tangent: arc_length is |length|, position advances as sign(length)·s, and the end-frame tangent/normal carry the same sign so downstream segments inherit a consistent, right-handed frame matching the direction of motion. Using sign (not a conditional) keeps this branch-free and MCM/Particles-compatible — sign broadcasts elementwise over a Particles length. length == 0 is rejected at construction (issue #344): sign(0.0) == 0.0 would otherwise give the segment a zero tangent and a zero end frame, placing every downstream segment in NaN frames.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.StraightSegment"},{"category":"type","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._RowTable","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_RowTable(p::Path)\n\nThe placed segments of p grouped by concrete type: groups is a tuple of vectors, one per type present, and row c is groups[kind[c]][slot[c]]. Path.seg must hold rows of different segment types, so reading a row from it leaves the type to a dynamic dispatch; _with_row instead picks the group by its tuple index, which the compiler unrolls into one branch per type, so the row's concrete methods are reached without one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._RowTable"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Base.position-Tuple{Path, Real}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"position(path, s) -> Vector\n\nReturn the global-frame position of a built path at arc length s.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.position"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._apply_field_meta-Tuple{AbstractPathSegment}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_apply_field_meta(seg) -> AbstractPathSegment\n\nApply every MetaAdd/MetaMul whose symbol names one of seg's own fields to that field via meta_combine, returning a reconstructed segment. Meta whose symbol is not a field of seg is ignored (carried through unchanged), so foreign annotations are inert here. Segments without meta are returned unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._apply_field_meta"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._assert_scalar_spec-Tuple{PathBuilder}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_assert_scalar_spec(pb::PathBuilder)\n\nThrow unless every value in the authored path — segment fields, meta payloads, start state, spin and twist rates — is deterministic. Geometry construction is scalar-only: an ensemble (Particles) value must be resolved to one scalar realization before any placement, frame, or connector math runs. Uncertain models are executed through FiberModel / propagate_fiber, which realizes every particle as its own scalar build and integrates all lanes in one ensemble solve; a single realization can be materialized with scalarize(model, i).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._assert_scalar_spec"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._author_coords-Tuple{Any}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_author_coords(t) -> NTuple{3, Real}\n\nNormalize an authored 3-tuple (point or tangent) via _author_normalized_real.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._author_coords"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._author_normalized_real-Tuple{Real}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_author_normalized_real(x) -> Real\n\nNormalize one authored value: a deterministic input coerces to Float64, a Particles input is kept as authored. This runs at the authoring boundary, not the geometry-build boundary — Particles <: Real, so retaining it preserves the uncertain model until scalarize(model, i) selects one aligned realization.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._author_normalized_real"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._bend_axis_at-Tuple{PlacedSegment, Any}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_bend_axis_at(ps::PlacedSegment, s_local) -> (cos2φ, sin2φ)\n\nBend-axis orientation (curvature direction) in the transported frame at physical local arc length s_local, as the normalization-free double-angle pair; it matches _bend_axis_in_bishop_frame queried through a Path. Conditional-free for MCM; callers guard κ ≠ 0, so k2 > 0 here.\n\nA consumer whose birefringence magnitude carries a power of κ should prefer _bend_axis_vector_at and form the product itself, so the κ cancels rather than being divided out and multiplied back.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._bend_axis_at"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._bend_axis_vector_at-Tuple{PlacedSegment, Any}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_bend_axis_vector_at(ps::PlacedSegment, s_local) -> (kx, ky)\n\nTransverse components (k_x, k_y) = κ·(cos φ, sin φ) of the curvature vector k⃗ = dT̂/ds (1/m) in the transported frame, at physical local arc length s_local. The frame is the segment's stored entry bishop_e1 transported to s_local, which is the path's frame, continuous along the whole path by construction.\n\nThis is the un-normalized form of _bend_axis_at, and the one the birefringence terms consume. The curvature vector is transverse to the tangent, so k_x² + k_y² = κ² identically — which lets a consumer whose magnitude is polynomial in κ cancel that κ² against this pair's normalization instead of dividing by it. Polynomial in the frame components: no division, no Inf sentinel, exactly zero where the path is straight, and finite through the inflection of a connector (where κ = 0 with the segment far from straight). Conditional-free, so MCM Particles propagate through s_local.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._bend_axis_vector_at"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._bifrost_meta_token-Tuple{IO, Any}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_bifrost_meta_token(io, seg) -> Union{Nothing,String}\n\nRender seg's non-Nickname meta for its own compact show (the nickname itself is always shown inline by the caller, unwrapped to a bare string — see each segment's Base.show). When io carries a :bifrost_meta_registry (set by a container's full display while itemizing many segments — see _BifrostMetaRegistry), returns a short \"[meta1]\"-style reference into that shared lookup table instead of repeating the same rendered text once per segment that carries it. Returns nothing when the segment carries no meta beyond (optionally) its Nickname.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._bifrost_meta_token"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._binormal_local-Tuple{StraightSegment, Any}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_binormal_local(seg::AbstractPathSegment, s) -> AbstractVector{T}\n\nReturn the unit binormal at local arc length s, in the local frame. See AbstractPathSegment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._binormal_local"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._bishop_basis_at-Tuple{PlacedSegment, Any}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_bishop_basis_at(ps::PlacedSegment, s_local) -> NamedTuple\n\nTransported (Bishop) basis (tangent, bishop_e1, bishop_e2) at physical local arc length s_local within placed segment ps. The segment-entry frame ps.bishop_e1 is advanced by _parallel_transport_local; conditional-free, so MCM Particles propagate through s_local.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._bishop_basis_at"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._budget_scalar-Tuple{Real}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_budget_scalar(x) -> Float64\n\nReduce a value to a plain Float64 for sample-budgeting.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._budget_scalar"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._build_cubic_connector-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_build_cubic_connector(p1_local, t_hat_out;\n                       min_bend_radius, target_path_length,\n                       n_table, meta) → CubicConnector\n\nConstruct a CubicConnector from a JumpTo resolve. The connector is a cubic Hermite matching position and tangent at both endpoints (G1); endpoint curvature is left free.\n\nt_hat_in is the local-frame ẑ = (0,0,1) and t_hat_out is the outgoing tangent in the same local frame.\n\nIf min_bend_radius is set, performs an exponential-bracket / bisection search over the handle scale λ until the sampled peak curvature falls below 1/min_bend_radius.\n\nIf target_path_length is set, instead searches λ until the connector's arc length matches the target — a JumpTo's target_length, which a consuming layer may stamp while the endpoint stays a lab-frame invariant.\n\nThe two constraints can be combined: when both are set, λ is chosen by the arc-length search and the result is validated against min_bend_radius.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._build_cubic_connector"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._collect_segment_boundaries-Tuple{Path, Float64, Float64}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_collect_segment_boundaries(path, s1f, s2f) → NamedTuple\n\nPositions (seg_bx, seg_by, seg_bz) of every effective arc-length join between authored segments of path that falls within [s1f, s2f].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._collect_segment_boundaries"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._curvature_vector_local-Tuple{StraightSegment, Any}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_curvature_vector_local(seg::AbstractPathSegment, s) -> AbstractVector{T}\n\nReturn the curvature vector k⃗ = dT̂/ds (1/m) at local arc length s, in the segment's local frame. Unlike a unit Frenet normal it needs no k⃗/|k⃗| normalization, so it is well-defined (zero) on straight regions and continuous through inflections. See AbstractPathSegment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._curvature_vector_local"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._end_frame_local-Tuple{StraightSegment}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_end_frame_local(seg::AbstractPathSegment) -> (T, N, B)\n\nReturn the (tangent, normal, binormal) frame at the segment's end, in its local frame. See AbstractPathSegment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._end_frame_local"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._eval_rate-Tuple{Nothing, Integer}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_eval_rate(rate, s) -> value\n\nEvaluate a twist rate at local arc length s: nothing → 0, a Real constant → itself. Branch-free per type so a Particles constant propagates without coercion; the nothing zero matches the element type of s, so a Particles-valued s (a physical local position under segment-local propagation) yields a Particles zero rather than coercing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._eval_rate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._expand-Tuple{PathSample}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_expand(ps::PathSample) → NamedTuple\n\nUnpack a PathSample into flat named arrays: s, x, y, z, tx, ty, tz, e1x, e1y, e1z, e2x, e2y, e2z, kappa, tau_geom, tau_spin.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._expand"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._frame_mul-Tuple{StaticArraysCore.SMatrix{3, 3, Float64, 9}, AbstractVector}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_frame_mul(A, v) -> SVector{3}\n\nThe 3×3 product A * v, each component accumulated over the columns in order with fused multiply-adds: fma(A[i,3], v[3], fma(A[i,2], v[2], A[i,1] * v[1])). That is the rounding of the BLAS gemv the placement loop uses on x86-64, so a static frame reproduces the stored references bit for bit. A static product's muladd would not: the compiler fuses it or not depending on where the call is inlined.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._frame_mul"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._helix_basis-Tuple{HelixSegment}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_helix_basis(seg::HelixSegment)\n\nReturn the helix geometric basis (R, h, ℓ′, â, r̂₀, ê_φ): radius, axial advance per radian, arc length per radian, axis unit vector, outward radial at s = 0, and tangential direction at s = 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._helix_basis"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._helix_h-Tuple{HelixSegment}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_helix_h(seg::HelixSegment)\n\nReturn the helix axial advance per radian, h = pitch / 2π.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._helix_h"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._initial_frame_from_tangent-Tuple{Tuple{Real, Real, Real}}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_initial_frame_from_tangent(T_tuple) -> (T, N, B)\n\nDerive an orthonormal start frame from a tangent direction.\n\nPicks N orthogonal to T via Gram-Schmidt against the world axis least aligned with T (guaranteeing a non-degenerate cross product), then B = T × N. Throws if the tangent is zero.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._initial_frame_from_tangent"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._integrate_rate-Tuple{Nothing, Float64, Float64}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_integrate_rate(rate, a, b; rtol=1e-8, atol=0.0)\n\nIntegrate a spin rate over the run-local material interval [a, b].\n\nConstant rates take the analytic branch; Function rates use QuadGK adaptive Gauss–Kronrod, which subdivides automatically for oscillatory integrands; a nothing rate (no spin) integrates to 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._integrate_rate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._length_fields-Tuple{JumpTo}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_length_fields(::JumpTo) -> ()\n\nDeclared empty: a connector's arc length is an output of the connector solve, not an authored field, so there is nothing for an isotropic factor to multiply. The fiber layer strains a connector by re-solving it to a stamped target_length instead (see _scale_length_fields).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._length_fields"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._length_fields-Tuple{StraightSegment}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_length_fields(seg) -> Tuple{Vararg{Symbol}}\n\nThe length-dimensioned fields of seg that scale with an isotropic factor τ. Dimensionless fields (angles, turns) are omitted. The inverse-length twist rate is handled separately by _scale_length_fields (divided, not multiplied). Each concrete AbstractPathSegment must declare this; the fallback errors so a newly added segment type cannot be silently mis-scaled.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._length_fields"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._local_to_global-Tuple{PlacedSegment, AbstractVector}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_local_to_global(ps::PlacedSegment, v_local) -> AbstractVector\n\nRotate a local-frame vector into global coordinates via the placed segment's construction frame (v_g = placement_frame * v_l, by _frame_mul).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._local_to_global"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._meta_without-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:AbstractMeta}, Symbol}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_meta_without(meta_or_seg, sym::Symbol) -> Vector{AbstractMeta}\n\nReturn a fresh meta vector (copied from a meta vector or a segment's meta) with every MetaAdd(sym, …) and MetaMul(sym, …) removed. Other meta (including MCM entries for different symbols) is preserved in order. Generic: it names no specific symbol, so a consuming layer can use it to strip an annotation it has just interpreted without the geometry layer knowing what that symbol means.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._meta_without"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._normal_local-Tuple{StraightSegment, Any}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_normal_local(seg::AbstractPathSegment, s) -> AbstractVector{T}\n\nReturn the unit normal at local arc length s, in the local frame. See AbstractPathSegment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._normal_local"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._parallel_transport_local-Tuple{StraightSegment, AbstractVector, Any}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_parallel_transport_local(seg::AbstractPathSegment, v, s) -> AbstractVector\n\nParallel-transport the transverse vector v from the segment's entry (s = 0) to local arc length s, in local coordinates: the unique rotation history with zero twist about the tangent (⟨de/ds, T̂ × e⟩ = 0). v must be perpendicular to the entry tangent (local ẑ); the result is perpendicular to the tangent at s. Closed forms per segment, conditional-free on uncertain values, so MCM Particles propagate. See AbstractPathSegment.\n\nA negative-length StraightSegment reverses the tangent; transport across a tangent reversal is ill-defined, and the identity is used there (matching the sign-carrying frame chaining of build).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._parallel_transport_local"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._path_local_s-Tuple{Path, Integer, Any}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_path_local_s(p, c, u) -> s_local\n\nPhysical segment-local arc length of reference coordinate u inside the already-routed finite row c: s_local = u - u0[c], saturated into the segment's own domain [0, L0[c]] (on a built path L0[c] is the physical segment length).\n\nThe saturation is what makes _ARC_BOUNDARY_TOL safe at a row edge: a u within that slack past the edge still routes to this row, which without the clamp would evaluate the segment formulas just outside their extent — the segment queries carry no domain guard of their own (curvature(::CatenarySegment, s) simply evaluates its formula at whatever s it is handed). The point queries on a Path route through it too (issue #319).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._path_local_s"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._phase_partial-Tuple{Nothing, Any}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_phase_partial(rate, x) -> value\n\nIntegrate a twist/spin rate from 0 to physical position x. A constant Real rate gives rate*x; nothing gives zero(x). A Function rate — material spin only, since mechanical twist is a constant rate (see TwistRate) — falls back to adaptive quadrature.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._phase_partial"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._qc_build_e1_table-Tuple{Matrix{Float64}, Int64}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_qc_build_e1_table(a, n) -> Matrix{Float64}\n\nBuild the n × 3 transported-e1 lookup for a cubic with coefficients a: row i is the parallel transport of the entry x̂ to u = (i-1)/(n-1), accumulated by double-reflection steps between consecutive nodes. Exact for straight and zero-length connectors (identity transport); O(h²)-accurate in the node spacing otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._qc_build_e1_table"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._qc_double_reflect-NTuple{5, AbstractVector}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_qc_double_reflect(v, x0, t0, x1, t1) -> Vector\n\nOne double-reflection parallel-transport step (Wang–Jüttler–Zheng–Liu rotation-minimizing frame, doi:10.1145/1330511.1330513): transport the transverse vector v from the point/tangent pair (x0, t0) to (x1, t1). Degenerate steps (coincident points, parallel reflection data) skip the affected reflection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._qc_double_reflect"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._qc_tangent_at_u-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, Real}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_qc_tangent_at_u(a, u) -> Vector\n\nUnit tangent of the cubic at parameter u (not arc length). Zero-speed points (degenerate/zero-length connectors) map to the local ẑ, mirroring tangent_local.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._qc_tangent_at_u"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._resolve_at_placement-Tuple{AbstractPathSegment, AbstractVector, AbstractMatrix}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_resolve_at_placement(seg, pos, frame_mat) -> AbstractPathSegment\n\nResolve seg at the position pos and construction frame frame_mat where build places it. A segment resolved as authored passes through unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._resolve_at_placement"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._resolve_at_placement-Tuple{JumpTo, AbstractVector, AbstractMatrix}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_resolve_at_placement(seg::JumpTo, pos, frame_mat) -> CubicConnector\n\nResolve a JumpTo at the position pos and construction frame frame_mat (columns [N, B, T]) where it is placed: the global target and incoming tangent are expressed in that local frame and handed to the connector solve, with target_length as its arc-length constraint. Throws an ArgumentError when the target coincides with pos (chord ≤ 1e-12 m), where the cubic solve has no direction to leave along.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._resolve_at_placement"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._rotate_about_unit_axis-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector, Any}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_rotate_about_unit_axis(v, axis, angle) -> AbstractVector\n\nRodrigues rotation of v about the unit vector axis by angle (rad). Conditional-free closed form, so MCM Particles propagate through all three arguments.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._rotate_about_unit_axis"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._route_cell_index-Tuple{Path, Any}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_route_cell_index(p, u) -> Int\n\nRow c of p's segment table whose reference interval [u0[c], u0[c]+L0[c]] contains u, skipping zero-length rows (a zero-length row has an empty interval and must never be routed to — the ensemble would divide 0/0 through τ). The index addresses the same authored segment across index-aligned per-lane tables (see fiber/fiber-ensemble.jl).\n\nu must lie on the tiled domain to within _ARC_DOMAIN_TOL; off-domain is a caller error, not a clamp, since the routed segment's formulas would otherwise be extrapolated silently (issue #319). Separately, _ARC_BOUNDARY_TOL keeps a u a hair past a finite row's edge — the ensemble remap recomputes it as u_j = u0_j + τ·(u - u0_c), which can round just over — on the near side of that edge rather than jumping to the next segment's physics; _path_local_s then saturates the position it implies.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._route_cell_index"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._safe_normalize-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_safe_normalize(v) -> AbstractVector\n\nReturn v scaled to unit length. Assumes v is non-zero.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._safe_normalize"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._scale_length_fields-Tuple{AbstractPathSegment, Any, Any}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_scale_length_fields(seg, factor, new_meta) -> AbstractPathSegment\n\nReturn a copy of seg with every length-dimensioned field (see _length_fields) multiplied by factor, the inverse-length twist rate divided by factor (conserving total turns; see _scale_inverse_twist_rate), all other fields preserved, and meta replaced by new_meta. Broadcasting (.* factor) covers both scalar and tuple-valued length fields. Domain-agnostic: the caller chooses factor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._scale_length_fields"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._segment_is_straight-Tuple{AbstractPathSegment}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_segment_is_straight(seg::AbstractPathSegment) -> Bool\n\nWhether seg has identically zero curvature over its whole domain, as a property of its type rather than of a sampled value.\n\nA curvature-driven term can skip the transported-frame work on such a segment. The saving is the point: the test resolves at dispatch, so unlike a runtime iszero(κ) it introduces no branch on a differentiable (or Particles-valued) quantity, and it never fires at an isolated κ = 0 — the inflection of a connector — where curvature vanishes but the bend axis is still turning.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._segment_is_straight"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._segment_nickname_intervals-Tuple{Path}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_segment_nickname_intervals(path) → Vector{NamedTuple}\n\nEvery placed segment of path, in global arc-length order, as (s_lo, s_hi, nickname). nickname is \"\" for a segment carrying no Nickname meta. The intervals cover [0, s_end(path)] without gaps, so every arc length maps to exactly one entry.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._segment_nickname_intervals"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._segment_point_budget-Tuple{AbstractPathSegment, Float64, Path, Vararg{Float64, 4}}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_segment_point_budget(seg, seg_s_start, b, s_lo, s_hi, fidelity, total_len) -> Int\n\nReturn the number of sample points to allocate to the placed segment seg of the built path b, entered at arc length seg_s_start, over the clamped window [s_lo, s_hi], scaled by fidelity and the largest of four budgets: the segment's geometric turning angle, its material-spin angle, its own mechanical-twist angle, and its arc length as a fraction of total_len (the path's total arc length).\n\nThe length budget is what gives a straight, untwisted, unspun segment — zero turning angle, zero spin, zero twist, otherwise floored to just its two endpoints — a run of interior points as well, so a sampled centerline carries per-point data along its whole length rather than only at its ends. A StraightSegment raises that floor to 5 regardless of length fraction, since a short straight (e.g. a lead-in/lead-out relative to a long curved segment) would otherwise still round down to its two endpoints.\n\nThe twist budget matters even though twist_phase itself is exact (mechanical twist is a per-segment constant, integrated in closed form, never adaptively quadratured): a fast-twisting segment with negligible curvature and spin would otherwise still round down to the shared floor, leaving too few points to resolve how fast the birefringence axis is rotating — the plotted angle at each sample stays exact, but consumers that interpolate or snap between samples (e.g. inspect's cursor slider) then see that exact angle jump by several radians between adjacent points.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._segment_point_budget"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._segment_total_angle-Tuple{BendSegment}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_segment_total_angle(seg) -> Float64\n\nReturn the total turning angle (radians) swept by a segment, used to budget adaptive sample density. Defaults to 0.0 for segments with no net turning.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._segment_total_angle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._segment_twist-Tuple{AbstractPathSegment}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_segment_twist(seg::AbstractPathSegment) -> TwistRate\n\nReturn the segment's mechanical-twist rate spec, or nothing if it has none.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._segment_twist"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._validate_twist_rate-Tuple{Union{Nothing, Real}}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_validate_twist_rate(twist)\n\nThrow if twist is function-valued. Mechanical twist is a constant rate (rad/m) per segment (see TwistRate); approximate a varying profile with consecutive constant-twist segments.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._validate_twist_rate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry._with_row-Tuple{Any, Bifrost.PathGeometry._RowTable, Integer, Vararg{Any}}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"_with_row(f, rows::_RowTable, c, args...) -> f(ps, args...)\n\nf applied to row c's placed segment ps, concretely typed, and args.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry._with_row"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.arc_length-Tuple{Path}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"arc_length(path) -> Float64\narc_length(path, s1, s2) -> Float64\n\nReturn a built path's total arc length, or the arc length between s1 and s2 (which is simply s2 - s1 in the global arc-length parameterization).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.arc_length"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.arc_length-Tuple{StraightSegment}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"arc_length(seg::AbstractPathSegment) -> T\n\nReturn the segment's total arc length (the local-frame interface method; see AbstractPathSegment).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.arc_length"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.bend!-Tuple{PathBuilder}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"bend!(pb::PathBuilder; radius, angle, axis_angle=0.0, twist=nothing,\n      meta=AbstractMeta[]) -> pb\n\nAppend a BendSegment (circular arc) to pb. axis_angle orients the bend plane in the transverse frame.\n\nSee also straight!, helix!, catenary!, jumpto!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.bend!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.bishop_e1-Tuple{Path, Real}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"bishop_e1(path, s) -> Vector\n\nReturn the global-frame transverse unit vector e1 of the parallel-transported (Bishop / relatively-parallel) frame of a built path at arc length s.\n\nThe transported frame has zero twist about the tangent (⟨de1/ds, e2⟩ = 0): it is continuous along the whole path — through inflections, straight regions, and segment joints where the curvature direction jumps. It is anchored at s = 0 by the static lab-frame convention of _initial_frame_from_tangent. It is not the Frenet–Serret normal; the curvature direction is exposed separately by curvature_vector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.bishop_e1"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.bishop_e2-Tuple{Path, Real}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"bishop_e2(path, s) -> Vector\n\nReturn the global-frame transverse unit vector e2 = T̂ × e1 of the parallel-transported (Bishop) frame of a built path at arc length s. See bishop_e1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.bishop_e2"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.bishop_frame-Tuple{Path, Real}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"bishop_frame(path, s) -> NamedTuple\n\nReturn all differential-geometry quantities of a built path at arc length s as a NamedTuple: position, tangent, bishop_e1, bishop_e2 (the parallel-transported pair — see bishop_e1), curvature_vector, curvature, geometric_torsion, and spin_rate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.bishop_frame"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.bounding_box-Tuple{Path}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"bounding_box(path; n=512) -> (; lo, hi)\n\nReturn the axis-aligned bounding box (lo, hi) of a built path, sampled at n points along its arc length.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.bounding_box"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.breakpoints-Tuple{Path}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"breakpoints(path) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nReturn the normalized arc-length segment breakpoints of a built path, read straight from the segment table, whose running offsets are the same floats the generators route on (see path_segment_breakpoints; issue #225). Spin is smooth within a segment, and a spin run starts at a segment boundary, so spin adds no breakpoint of its own. The propagator never steps across one of these.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.breakpoints"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.build-Tuple{PathBuilder}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"build(pb::PathBuilder; material_factors = nothing) -> Path\n\nPlace pb into the global frame and return its Path. Each segment's field-level MetaAdd/MetaMul whose symbol names one of that segment's own fields (:length, :radius, …) is applied first via _apply_field_meta — always, so authored meta is never silently dropped; meta this layer does not recognize is carried through untouched. Each jumpto! connector resolves into its CubicConnector where it is placed (see _resolve_at_placement). One placement loop runs from the start to the last segment, chaining the construction frame and transporting the Bishop frame, which is anchored at the start by _initial_frame_from_tangent and continuous along the whole path; the table rows are filled in the same pass.\n\nmaterial_factors holds one glass strain factor F = L_physical/L_material per segment, as a consuming layer applied them (the fiber's :T_K/:tension); it sets the spin material columns, so each segment's spin turns are conserved under strain. nothing means unstrained.\n\nThrows an ArgumentError when pb carries MCM Particles (geometry is scalar-only; see _assert_scalar_spec), holds no segment, or material_factors does not hold one entry per segment. A failure while applying a segment's field meta or placing it is rethrown as a BuildSiteError naming that segment, with the original exception as its cause.\n\nExtended help\n\nEvery segment defines its geometry in its own local frame, where it always starts at the origin heading along local +z. Each segment type answers two questions in local coordinates:\n\nend_position_local(seg) — where does this segment end, relative to its own start?\n_end_frame_local(seg) — what is the (T, N, B) frame at its end, in its own local axes?\n\nThe placement loop rotates those local answers into global coordinates and chains them (the \"frame propagation\" steps below). The placement_frame matrix (columns [N, B, T]) is the rotation from local to global, so placement_frame * v_local converts any local vector to global.\n\nTwo distinct frames are propagated through the loop:\n\nthe chained [N, B, T] construction frame (placement_frame), which interprets each segment's axis_angle and the connector boundary data — it absorbs each segment's local end frame and is internal to placement;\nthe transported (Bishop) frame e1 (with e2 = T × e1), advanced by each segment's closed-form parallel transport (_parallel_transport_local) and anchored at the path start by _initial_frame_from_tangent. This is the frame returned by bishop_e1/bishop_e2/bishop_frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.build"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.cartesian_distance-Tuple{Path, Real, Real}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"cartesian_distance(path, s1, s2) -> Float64\n\nReturn the straight-line (chord) distance between the points at arc lengths s1 and s2 on a built path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.cartesian_distance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.catenary!-Tuple{PathBuilder}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"catenary!(pb::PathBuilder; a, length, axis_angle=0.0, twist=nothing,\n          meta=AbstractMeta[]) -> pb\n\nAppend a CatenarySegment to pb. a is the catenary parameter and axis_angle orients the curve in the transverse frame.\n\nSee also straight!, bend!, helix!, jumpto!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.catenary!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.curvature-Tuple{Path, Real}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"curvature(path, s) -> Float64\n\nReturn the curvature κ (1/m) of a built path at arc length s.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.curvature"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.curvature-Tuple{StraightSegment, Any}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"curvature(seg::AbstractPathSegment, s) -> T\n\nReturn the curvature κ (1/m) at local arc length s. See AbstractPathSegment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.curvature"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.curvature_in_bishop_frame-Tuple{Path, Real}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"curvature_in_bishop_frame(path, s) -> NamedTuple\n\nResolve the curvature vector of a built path at arc length s into the transported (Bishop) frame. Returns (kx, ky, k2), where kx/ky are the components of curvature_vector(path, s) along bishop_e1/bishop_e2 and k2 = kx^2 + ky^2. The magnitude k2 lets diagnostics recover the bend radius 1 / sqrt(k2).\n\nThis deliberately avoids the Frenet normal: it stays well behaved on nearly straight sections (where the Frenet normal is undefined), and curvature-direction jumps at segment joints are handled by path breakpoints rather than here. Conditional-free, so MCM Particles propagate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.curvature_in_bishop_frame"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.curvature_vector-Tuple{Path, Real}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"curvature_vector(path, s) -> Vector\n\nReturn the global-frame curvature vector k⃗ = dT̂/ds (1/m) of a built path at arc length s: magnitude curvature(path, s), direction the local center of curvature. Zero on straight regions and continuous through inflections — unlike a unit Frenet normal it requires no normalization. Its transported-frame components are exposed by curvature_in_bishop_frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.curvature_vector"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.end_point-Tuple{Path}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"end_point(path) -> Vector\n\nReturn the global-frame position at a built path's end (s = arc_length(path)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.end_point"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.end_position_local-Tuple{StraightSegment}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"end_position_local(seg::AbstractPathSegment) -> AbstractVector{T}\n\nReturn the segment's endpoint position in its local frame. See AbstractPathSegment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.end_position_local"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.end_tangent-Tuple{Path}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"end_tangent(path) -> Vector\n\nReturn the global-frame unit tangent at a built path's end (s = arc_length(path)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.end_tangent"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.geometric_torsion-Tuple{Path, Real}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"geometric_torsion(path, s) -> Float64\n\nReturn the geometric torsion τ (rad/m) of a built path at arc length s.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.geometric_torsion"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.geometric_torsion-Tuple{StraightSegment, Any}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"geometric_torsion(seg::AbstractPathSegment, s) -> T\n\nReturn the geometric torsion τ (rad/m) at local arc length s. See AbstractPathSegment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.geometric_torsion"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.helix!-Tuple{PathBuilder}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"helix!(pb::PathBuilder; radius, pitch, turns, axis_angle=0.0, twist=nothing,\n       meta=AbstractMeta[]) -> pb\n\nAppend a HelixSegment to pb. axis_angle selects the transverse direction the helix curves toward.\n\nSee also straight!, bend!, catenary!, jumpto!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.helix!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.jumpto!-Tuple{PathBuilder}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"jumpto!(pb::PathBuilder; point, incoming_tangent=nothing, min_bend_radius=nothing,\n        target_length=nothing, twist=nothing, meta=AbstractMeta[]) -> pb\n\nAppend a JumpTo connector that bends from the current position and tangent to the global target point, resolved into a cubic G1 connector at build time. incoming_tangent is the global tangent at point (chord direction when nothing). Segments may follow a connector, and a path may hold any number of them.\n\nmin_bend_radius bounds the connector's curvature; target_length fixes its arc length while it still lands at point. Left unset, the connector has no nominal length of its own and freely re-solves to span the gap — see JumpTo for why that's deliberate. Meta the geometry layer does not recognize is carried onto the resolved connector for a consuming layer.\n\nSee also the segment builders straight!, bend!, helix!, catenary!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.jumpto!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.local_segment-Tuple{Path, Real}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"local_segment(path, s) -> AbstractPathSegment\n\nReturn the AbstractPathSegment containing arc length s, retaining its meta. A point-like query sibling of curvature/twist_rate/position; consuming layers use it to recover per-segment annotations at query time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.local_segment"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.meta_combine-Tuple{Any, AbstractVector{<:AbstractMeta}, Symbol}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"meta_combine(baseline, meta_or_seg, sym::Symbol) → perturbed\n\nCombine every MetaMul(sym, d) and MetaAdd(sym, d) in a meta vector (or on a segment, via its meta) and apply them to baseline in a uniform order:\n\nperturbed = baseline * Π(d_mul) + Σ d_add\n\nAll multiplicative factors are applied first (as direct scale factors), then all additive offsets are summed on top. Non-matching-symbol entries are ignored. When no matching entries are present, baseline is returned unchanged (same object, no arithmetic performed).\n\nAccepts either a meta vector (e.g. a PathBuilder's meta) or any segment with a meta field. Intended as the shared composition helper that consumers call so every layer applies MCM perturbations the same way.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.meta_combine"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.normalize_breakpoints-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Real}}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"normalize_breakpoints(breakpoints) -> Vector\n\nReturn the breakpoints sorted and deduplicated.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.normalize_breakpoints"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.path_length-Tuple{Path}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"path_length(path) -> Float64\n\nReturn a built path's total arc length. Alias for arc_length.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.path_length"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.path_segment_breakpoints-Tuple{Path}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"path_segment_breakpoints(path) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nReturn the normalized arc-length breakpoints at every segment boundary (including the path endpoints) of a built path.\n\nRead from the segment table — the single walk that tiles the path, so the solver's interval endpoints and the table's reference intervals are the same floats by construction (issue #225: two independently maintained walks could disagree in the last ulps, which only the routing tolerance absorbed). Only the finite-length rows carry a breakpoint; a zero-length segment's boundary coincides with a neighbour's (and so dedups away).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.path_segment_breakpoints"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.position_local-Tuple{StraightSegment, Any}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"position_local(seg::AbstractPathSegment, s) -> AbstractVector{T}\n\nReturn the position at local arc length s, in the segment's local frame. See AbstractPathSegment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.position_local"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.s_end-Tuple{Path}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"s_end(p::Path) -> Float64\n\nReturn the path's total arc length, the far edge u0[end] + L0[end] of its last row.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.s_end"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.sample-Tuple{Path, Any}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"sample(path, s_values) -> Vector{NamedTuple}\n\nReturn the bishop_frame of a built path at each arc length in s_values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.sample"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.sample_path-Tuple{Path, Real, Real}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"sample_path(path, s1, s2; fidelity = 1.0) → PathSample\n\nAdaptive sampling of a built path over [s1, s2]: each segment gets the sample budget of _segment_point_budget.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.sample_path"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.sample_uniform-Tuple{Path}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"sample_uniform(path; n=256) -> Vector{NamedTuple}\n\nReturn n frames of a built path uniformly spaced over its arc length.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.sample_uniform"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.segment_label-Tuple{AbstractPathSegment, Integer}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"segment_label(seg, index) -> String\n\nName authored segment index for a message: segment 3 (BendSegment \"paddle-1\"), or segment 3 (BendSegment) when seg carries no Nickname.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.segment_label"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.segment_meta-Tuple{AbstractPathSegment}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"segment_meta(seg::AbstractPathSegment) -> Vector{AbstractMeta}\n\nReturn the segment's meta annotation bag, or an empty vector if it has none.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.segment_meta"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.segment_nickname-Tuple{Any}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"segment_nickname(seg) → Union{Nothing,String}\n\nReturn the first Nickname label attached to seg via its meta vector, or nothing if none is present.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.segment_nickname"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.spin!-Tuple{PathBuilder}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"spin!(pb::PathBuilder; rate) -> pb\n\nOpen a material-spin run covering every segment appended after this call, until the next spin!. rate (rad/m) is nothing (no spin), a constant Real (an MCM Particles inside a FiberModel), or a Function rate(m) of run-local material arc length, with m = 0 at the run start. Segments authored before the first spin! form a run without spin.\n\nThe spin phase is continuous across runs: each run starts at the phase the previous run ended with. The rate is written in material (unstrained) coordinate, so :T_K or :tension strain conserves the turns over each segment (see spin_rate).\n\nThrows an ArgumentError for a Symbol rate — a run continues until the next spin!, so there is nothing to inherit — and when the previous spin! has no segment after it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.spin!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.spin_phase-Tuple{Path, Real}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"spin_phase(path, s) -> value\n\nAccumulated spin phase (rad) of a built path at arc length s: the phase at the start of the spin run holding s, continuous along the path, plus ∫ ξ over the run's material coordinate from the run start to s.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.spin_phase"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.spin_rate-Tuple{Path, Real}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"spin_rate(path, s) -> Float64\n\nPhysical spin rate (rad/m) of a built path at arc length s: 0 where the spin run holding s has no spin, else τ_authored(m(s))·m'(s) for the run's authored rate (a constant, or a Function of run-local material arc length) at the material coordinate m(s).\n\nSpin is material spin: the preform is rotated as the fiber is drawn, so the pattern is frozen into the glass. Thermal (:T_K) or tension (:tension) strain stretches that glass but creates and destroys no turns, so ∫ τ ds is invariant over each piece of glass. The governing transformation is the 1-form rule\n\nτ_physical(s)·ds = τ_authored(m(s))·dm   ⟺   τ_physical(s) = τ_authored(m(s))·m'(s)\n\nWith a per-segment strain factor F_k = L_physical/L_material the map m is piecewise linear — m' = 1/F_k on segment k, the table's spin_dmds — so evaluating the authored rate at m reproduces that rule exactly, without resolving the rate into a piecewise-constant Function (which would land an adaptive quadgk over a jump discontinuity in the solver's inner loop). Unstrained, m is the run-local arc length and m' = 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.spin_rate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.start_point-Tuple{Path}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"start_point(path) -> Vector\n\nReturn the global-frame position at a built path's start (s = 0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.start_point"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.start_tangent-Tuple{Path}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"start_tangent(path) -> Vector\n\nReturn the global-frame unit tangent at a built path's start (s = 0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.start_tangent"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.straight!-Tuple{PathBuilder}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"straight!(pb::PathBuilder; length, twist=nothing, meta=AbstractMeta[]) -> pb\n\nAppend a StraightSegment of signed length to pb.\n\nSee also bend!, helix!, catenary!, jumpto!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.straight!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.tangent-Tuple{Path, Real}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"tangent(path, s) -> Vector\n\nReturn the global-frame unit tangent of a built path at arc length s.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.tangent"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.tangent_local-Tuple{StraightSegment, Any}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"tangent_local(seg::AbstractPathSegment, s) -> AbstractVector{T}\n\nReturn the unit tangent at local arc length s, in the local frame. See AbstractPathSegment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.tangent_local"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.total_spin-Tuple{Path}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"total_spin(path; s_start = 0.0, s_end = s_end(path), rtol = 1e-8, atol = 0.0) → Float64\n\nIntegrated spin  τ(s)  ds over [s_start, s_end], run by run: each spin run overlapping the window contributes its authored rate integrated between the material coordinates of the window's ends within that run (the change of variables that conserves turns under strain; see spin_rate). Both endpoints must lie in [0, s_end(path)].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.total_spin"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.total_torsion-Tuple{Path}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"total_torsion(path) -> Float64\n\nReturn the integral of geometric torsion over a built path (in radians).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.total_torsion"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.total_turning_angle-Tuple{Path}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"total_turning_angle(path) -> Float64\n\nReturn the integral of curvature over a built path (total bending angle, in radians).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.total_turning_angle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.twist_phase-Tuple{Path, Real}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"twist_phase(path, s) -> value\n\nAccumulated mechanical-twist phase ∫₀ˢ τ_m (rad) of a built path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.twist_phase"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.twist_rate-Tuple{AbstractPathSegment, Any}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"twist_rate(seg::AbstractPathSegment, s) -> value\n\nReturn the mechanical-twist rate (rad/m) at segment-local arc length s. Part of the AbstractPathSegment interface, beside curvature and geometric_torsion; defaults to evaluating the segment's twist field (zero for an untwisted segment).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.twist_rate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.twist_rate-Tuple{Path, Real}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"twist_rate(path, s) -> value\n\nMechanical-twist rate (rad/m) of a built path at arc length s, read from the segment containing s.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.twist_rate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-geometry/#Bifrost.PathGeometry.writhe-Tuple{Path}","page":"PathGeometry","text":"writhe(path; n = 256) → Float64\n\nWrithe of a built path: numerical double integral on n samples.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathGeometry.writhe"},{"category":"section","location":"api/nonlinear/#nonlinear","page":"Nonlinear","text":"Stimulated Raman and Brillouin scattering: modal gain coefficients and accumulated noise-power spectral density along a fiber, selected by kind (:raman or :brillouin).","title":"Nonlinear"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/nonlinear/#Bifrost.Nonlinear.RAMAN_FRACTION","page":"Nonlinear","text":"Fractional Raman contribution f_R of fused silica (dimensionless).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Nonlinear.RAMAN_FRACTION"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/nonlinear/#Bifrost.Nonlinear.RAMAN_PEAK_OMEGA","page":"Nonlinear","text":"Angular frequency of the dominant silica Raman peak (rad/s, 13.2 THz).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Nonlinear.RAMAN_PEAK_OMEGA"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/nonlinear/#Bifrost.Nonlinear.RAMAN_THRESHOLD_G","page":"Nonlinear","text":"Smith (1972) stimulated-Raman threshold gain parameter G_th ≈ 16.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Nonlinear.RAMAN_THRESHOLD_G"},{"category":"type","location":"api/nonlinear/#Bifrost.Nonlinear.BlowWood","page":"Nonlinear","text":"Blow & Wood (1989) two-parameter damped-oscillator model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Nonlinear.BlowWood"},{"category":"type","location":"api/nonlinear/#Bifrost.Nonlinear.HollenbeckCantrell","page":"Nonlinear","text":"Hollenbeck & Cantrell (2002) 13-mode Gaussian-damped oscillator model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Nonlinear.HollenbeckCantrell"},{"category":"type","location":"api/nonlinear/#Bifrost.Nonlinear.NonlinearGainSpectrum","page":"Nonlinear","text":"NonlinearGainSpectrum\n\nResult of a swept-offset nl_gain call: the stimulated modal gain coefficient (W⁻¹m⁻¹) of nonlinear process kind sampled over a grid of frequency offsets Δf at a fixed pump frequency.\n\nFields\n\nkind::Symbol — :raman or :brillouin.\nf_Hz::Float64 — fixed pump optical frequency (Hz).\noffset_Hz::AbstractVector — swept offset grid Δf (Hz, positive = Stokes).\ngain::AbstractVector — modal gain at each offset (W⁻¹m⁻¹); its entries carry Particles when T_K is uncertain.\ndirection::Symbol — Stokes propagation direction (:forward or :back).\ncross_section::FiberCrossSection — the cross section nl_gain was called with, retained for plot annotation and reproduction. nl_gain is a cross-section quantity (no fibre length or pump power enters), so there is no enclosing Fiber to record here — see NonlinearIntensitySpectrum for the fibre-level counterpart.\nparams::NamedTuple — the keyword arguments forwarded to nl_gain (e.g. T_K, model, fR), retained for reproduction and plot annotation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Nonlinear.NonlinearGainSpectrum"},{"category":"type","location":"api/nonlinear/#Bifrost.Nonlinear.NonlinearIntensitySpectrum","page":"Nonlinear","text":"NonlinearIntensitySpectrum\n\nResult of a swept-offset nl_intensity call: the single-sided noise power spectral density relative to the pump carrier (dBc/Hz) of nonlinear process kind, sampled over a grid of frequency offsets Δf.\n\nFields\n\nkind::Symbol — :raman or :brillouin.\nf_Hz::Float64 — fixed pump optical frequency (Hz).\noffset_Hz::AbstractVector — swept offset grid Δf (Hz, positive = Stokes).\npsd::AbstractVector — noise PSD at each offset (dBc/Hz); its entries carry Particles when T_K is uncertain.\ndirection::Symbol — Stokes propagation direction (:forward or :back).\nfiber::Fiber — the fibre nl_intensity was called with, retained for plot annotation and reproduction, with path replaced by nothing (the compiled geometry is the memory-heavy part and plays no role in the spectrum).\ns::Float64 — observation position along the fibre (m).\nintensity::Float64 — pump intensity I (W/m²).\nparams::NamedTuple — the keyword arguments forwarded to nl_intensity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Nonlinear.NonlinearIntensitySpectrum"},{"category":"type","location":"api/nonlinear/#Bifrost.Nonlinear.RamanConfig","page":"Nonlinear","text":"RamanConfig(; model=BlowWood(), fR=RAMAN_FRACTION)\nRamanConfig(model, fR=RAMAN_FRACTION)\n\nBundle of the response-model singleton (or :bw / :tabulated / :hc) and the fractional Raman contribution fR ∈ [0, 1] that together specify a silica Raman response for _g_R.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Nonlinear.RamanConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"api/nonlinear/#Bifrost.Nonlinear.RamanResponseModel","page":"Nonlinear","text":"abstract type RamanResponseModel\n\nTag type selecting the silica Raman response model. Concrete subtypes BlowWood, Tabulated, and HollenbeckCantrell dispatch im_h_R and _g_R at the type level.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Nonlinear.RamanResponseModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/nonlinear/#Bifrost.Nonlinear.Tabulated","page":"Nonlinear","text":"Lin & Agrawal (2006) 50-point tabulated spectrum, cubic-spline interpolated.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Nonlinear.Tabulated"},{"category":"function","location":"api/nonlinear/#Bifrost.Nonlinear._effective_length_backward","page":"Nonlinear","text":"_effective_length_backward(L, α_pump, α_signal=α_pump) -> m\n\nCounter-propagating effective length L_eff = (1 − e^{−(α_p+α_s)L}) / (α_p+α_s) for the backward (Brillouin-Stokes) geometry. Power attenuation coefficients are in 1/m.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Nonlinear._effective_length_backward"},{"category":"method","location":"api/nonlinear/#Bifrost.Nonlinear._effective_length_forward-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Nonlinear","text":"_effective_length_forward(L, α) -> m\n\nCo-propagating effective length L_eff = (1 − e^{−αL}) / α, tending to L as α → 0. α is the power attenuation coefficient (1/m); used for the co-propagating (Raman-Stokes) geometry.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Nonlinear._effective_length_forward"},{"category":"method","location":"api/nonlinear/#Bifrost.Nonlinear._fiber_without_path-Tuple{Fiber}","page":"Nonlinear","text":"_fiber_without_path(fiber) -> Fiber\n\nCopy of fiber with path replaced by nothing. path (the compiled geometry) is the memory-heavy part of a Fiber; the spectrum structs retain only the lightweight fields (cross_section, T_ref_K, s_start, s_end) needed to label a plot or reproduce a call, not the geometry itself.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Nonlinear._fiber_without_path"},{"category":"method","location":"api/nonlinear/#Bifrost.Nonlinear._g_R-Tuple{RamanResponseModel, Any, Any}","page":"Nonlinear","text":"_g_R(model::RamanResponseModel, Ω, γ; fR=RAMAN_FRACTION) -> W⁻¹m⁻¹\n_g_R(Ω, γ; config=RamanConfig()) -> W⁻¹m⁻¹\n\nRaman modal gain coefficient 2·γ·f_R·Im[h̃_R(Ω)]. Ω is the angular-frequency shift from the pump (rad/s), γ the fibre nonlinear coefficient (W⁻¹m⁻¹). Stokes (Ω > 0) gives positive gain; anti-Stokes (Ω < 0) negative.\n\nThe gain is on an absolute scale: Im[h̃_R] is Kramers-Kronig-normalised to Re[h̃_R(0)] = 1, and fR ∈ [0, 1] is the physical fractional Raman contribution to the Kerr response (RAMAN_FRACTION = 0.18 for silica; Agrawal NLFO 6e), not a free scaling factor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Nonlinear._g_R"},{"category":"method","location":"api/nonlinear/#Bifrost.Nonlinear._nl_channel_integral-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Nonlinear","text":"_nl_channel_integral(ratio_at, Δf_lo, Δf_hi; n_points=201) -> dBc\n\nBand-integrated noise-to-pump ratio 10·log10(∫ ratio dν) over [Δf_lo, Δf_hi], where ratio_at(Δf) is the per-Hz noise ratio. Returns dBc (not dBc/Hz).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Nonlinear._nl_channel_integral"},{"category":"method","location":"api/nonlinear/#Bifrost.Nonlinear._nl_noise_ratio-NTuple{6, Any}","page":"Nonlinear","text":"_nl_noise_ratio(g_modal, f, Δf, T_K, L_eff, P_pump; quantized, pump_depletion) -> 1/Hz\n\nSingle-sided spontaneous-scattering noise power spectral density relative to the pump power, S_noise(Δf)/P_pump. g_modal is the modal gain (W⁻¹m⁻¹), L_eff the effective length (m), P_pump the pump power (W). With quantized, the Stokes band carries (n_th+1) and anti-Stokes n_th; without it, n_th alone. The pump_depletion correction accounts for depletion by this process only, not simultaneous depletion by co- and counter-propagating conversion.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Nonlinear._nl_noise_ratio"},{"category":"method","location":"api/nonlinear/#Bifrost.Nonlinear._nonlinear_coefficient-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Nonlinear","text":"_nonlinear_coefficient(cs, λ, T_K) -> W⁻¹m⁻¹\n\nFibre nonlinear coefficient γ = (2π/λ)·n₂/A_eff from the core Kerr index n₂ = core_kerr_coefficient and the effective mode area mode_area_eff. λ is the vacuum wavelength (m), T_K the temperature (K).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Nonlinear._nonlinear_coefficient"},{"category":"method","location":"api/nonlinear/#Bifrost.Nonlinear._raman_response_freq_bw-Tuple{Any}","page":"Nonlinear","text":"Complex Blow-Wood frequency response h̃_R(Ω) (analytic Fourier transform).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Nonlinear._raman_response_freq_bw"},{"category":"method","location":"api/nonlinear/#Bifrost.Nonlinear._simpson-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector}","page":"Nonlinear","text":"Composite Simpson integral of y over a possibly non-uniform grid x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Nonlinear._simpson"},{"category":"method","location":"api/nonlinear/#Bifrost.Nonlinear._trapz-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector}","page":"Nonlinear","text":"Trapezoidal integral of y over a possibly non-uniform grid x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Nonlinear._trapz"},{"category":"method","location":"api/nonlinear/#Bifrost.Nonlinear.brillouin_gain_lorentzian-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"Nonlinear","text":"brillouin_gain_lorentzian(Ω, Ω_B, g_peak, Δν_FWHM) -> m/W\n\nLorentzian Brillouin gain spectrum g_peak·(Γ/2)² / ((Ω−Ω_B)² + (Γ/2)²), peaked at Ω_B = 2π·ν_B with FWHM Δν_FWHM (Hz). Ω is the angular-frequency offset (rad/s).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Nonlinear.brillouin_gain_lorentzian"},{"category":"method","location":"api/nonlinear/#Bifrost.Nonlinear.brillouin_threshold-Tuple{Fiber, Any, Any}","page":"Nonlinear","text":"brillouin_threshold(fiber, f, s; T_K=fiber.T_ref_K, α=nothing) -> W\n\nSmith (1972) SBS power threshold P_th = G_th / (g_B,peak · L_eff^back), with g_B,peak the peak modal Brillouin gain and L_eff^back the backward effective length over s (m). Only backward SBS on SilicaGermaniaGlass fibres is modelled. doi:10.1364/AO.11.002489.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Nonlinear.brillouin_threshold"},{"category":"method","location":"api/nonlinear/#Bifrost.Nonlinear.im_h_R-Tuple{BlowWood, Any}","page":"Nonlinear","text":"im_h_R(model::RamanResponseModel, Ω) -> Real\n\nImaginary part of the silica Raman frequency response Im[h̃_R(Ω)], antisymmetric in the angular-frequency offset Ω (rad/s). Dispatched per model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Nonlinear.im_h_R"},{"category":"method","location":"api/nonlinear/#Bifrost.Nonlinear.nl_gain-Tuple{Symbol, FiberCrossSection, Any, AbstractVector, Symbol}","page":"Nonlinear","text":"nl_gain(kind, cs, f, Δf::AbstractVector, dir; kwargs...) -> NonlinearGainSpectrum\n\nSwept-offset form of nl_gain: broadcast the scalar gain across the offset grid Δf (Hz) at fixed pump frequency f (Hz) and bundle the result with its parameters in a NonlinearGainSpectrum. Per-kind keywords are forwarded unchanged. A Bifrost extension provides a Plots.jl recipe so the spectrum can be passed directly to plot/plot!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Nonlinear.nl_gain"},{"category":"method","location":"api/nonlinear/#Bifrost.Nonlinear.nl_gain-Tuple{Symbol, FiberCrossSection, Any, Any, Symbol}","page":"Nonlinear","text":"nl_gain(kind, cs, f, Δf, dir; kwargs...) -> W⁻¹m⁻¹\n\nStimulated modal gain coefficient of nonlinear process kind (:raman or :brillouin) at pump optical frequency f (Hz), frequency offset Δf (Hz, positive = Stokes), and Stokes propagation direction dir (:forward or :back). Returns the gain per unit length per unit pump power, W⁻¹m⁻¹. Concrete methods live in raman.jl and brillouin.jl; see those for per-kind keywords.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Nonlinear.nl_gain"},{"category":"method","location":"api/nonlinear/#Bifrost.Nonlinear.nl_gain-Tuple{Val{:brillouin}, FiberCrossSection, Any, Any, Any}","page":"Nonlinear","text":"nl_gain(:brillouin, cs, f, Δf, dir; T_K=297.15) -> W⁻¹m⁻¹\n\nBackward stimulated-Brillouin modal gain coefficient at pump optical frequency f (Hz) and frequency offset Δf (Hz): the Lorentzian material gain brillouin_gain_lorentzian, peaked at the Bragg shift brillouin_freq_shift, divided by the effective mode area. Only backward SBS is modelled — dir must be :back (:forward throws UnsupportedPropertyError) and the core/cladding must be SilicaGermaniaGlass. Uncertain T_K lifts through Particles.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Nonlinear.nl_gain"},{"category":"method","location":"api/nonlinear/#Bifrost.Nonlinear.nl_gain-Tuple{Val{:raman}, FiberCrossSection, Any, Any, Any}","page":"Nonlinear","text":"nl_gain(:raman, cs, f, Δf, dir; T_K=297.15, model=:bw, fR=RAMAN_FRACTION) -> W⁻¹m⁻¹\n\nStimulated-Raman modal gain coefficient at pump optical frequency f (Hz) and frequency offset Δf (Hz, positive = Stokes): 2·γ·f_R·Im[h̃_R(2π·Δf)] with γ the fibre nonlinear coefficient. model selects the response (:bw/:tabulated/:hc or a RamanResponseModel). The Raman gain coefficient is direction-symmetric, so dir (:forward/:back) is validated but does not change the value. Requires a core with a defined Kerr index n₂ (silica / SiGe); a fluorine-doped core surfaces the material layer's UnsupportedPropertyError. Uncertain T_K lifts through Particles.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Nonlinear.nl_gain"},{"category":"method","location":"api/nonlinear/#Bifrost.Nonlinear.nl_intensity-Tuple{Symbol, Fiber, Any, AbstractVector, Symbol, Any, Any}","page":"Nonlinear","text":"nl_intensity(kind, fiber, f, Δf::AbstractVector, dir, s, I; kwargs...)\n    -> NonlinearIntensitySpectrum\n\nSwept-offset form of nl_intensity: broadcast the scalar noise PSD across the offset grid Δf (Hz) at fixed pump frequency f (Hz), observation position s (m), and pump intensity I (W/m²), and bundle the result in a NonlinearIntensitySpectrum. Per-kind keywords are forwarded unchanged. A Bifrost extension provides a Plots.jl recipe for direct plot/plot!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Nonlinear.nl_intensity"},{"category":"method","location":"api/nonlinear/#Bifrost.Nonlinear.nl_intensity-Tuple{Symbol, Fiber, Any, Any, Symbol, Any, Any}","page":"Nonlinear","text":"nl_intensity(kind, fiber, f, Δf, dir, s, I; kwargs...) -> dBc/Hz\n\nSingle-sided noise power spectral density (relative to the pump carrier, dBc/Hz) of nonlinear process kind at frequency offset Δf (Hz), observed at position s (m) for a pump of intensity I (W/m²) injected at s = 0 and propagating so the Stokes wave travels in dir. Concrete methods live in raman.jl and brillouin.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Nonlinear.nl_intensity"},{"category":"method","location":"api/nonlinear/#Bifrost.Nonlinear.nl_intensity-Tuple{Val{:brillouin}, Fiber, Vararg{Any, 5}}","page":"Nonlinear","text":"nl_intensity(:brillouin, fiber, f, Δf, dir, s, I; T_K=fiber.T_ref_K,\n             quantized=true, α=nothing, channel=nothing) -> dBc/Hz\n\nSingle-sided backward spontaneous-Brillouin noise PSD relative to the pump carrier at offset Δf (Hz), accumulated over length s (m) for pump intensity I (W/m²), using the backward effective length. Only backward SBS is modelled (dir must be :back). Loss defaults to smf28_attenuation; override with α (1/m). Uncertain T_K lifts through Particles.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Nonlinear.nl_intensity"},{"category":"method","location":"api/nonlinear/#Bifrost.Nonlinear.nl_intensity-Tuple{Val{:raman}, Fiber, Vararg{Any, 5}}","page":"Nonlinear","text":"nl_intensity(:raman, fiber, f, Δf, dir, s, I; T_K=fiber.T_ref_K, model=:bw,\n             fR=RAMAN_FRACTION, quantized=true, pump_depletion=false, α=nothing) -> dBc/Hz\n\nSingle-sided spontaneous-Raman noise PSD relative to the pump carrier at offset Δf (Hz), accumulated over length s (m) for pump intensity I (W/m²). dir selects the co- (:forward) or counter-propagating (:back) effective length. Loss defaults to smf28_attenuation — the linear (background) pump attenuation that sets L_eff, not the nonlinear conversion, so there is no double counting; override with α (1/m). Uncertain T_K lifts through Particles.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Nonlinear.nl_intensity"},{"category":"method","location":"api/nonlinear/#Bifrost.Nonlinear.raman_threshold-Tuple{Fiber, Any, Any}","page":"Nonlinear","text":"raman_threshold(fiber, f, s; T_K=fiber.T_ref_K, α=nothing, model=:bw) -> W\n\nSmith (1972) stimulated-Raman power threshold P_th = G_th / (g_R,peak · L_eff), with g_R,peak the modal Raman gain at the 13.2 THz peak and L_eff the forward effective length over s (m). doi:10.1364/AO.11.002489.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Nonlinear.raman_threshold"},{"category":"method","location":"api/nonlinear/#Bifrost.Nonlinear.thermal_phonon_number-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Nonlinear","text":"thermal_phonon_number(Ω, T_K) -> dimensionless\n\nBose-Einstein occupancy n_th(Ω, T) = 1 / (exp(ħ|Ω| / k_B T) − 1) of the phonon band at angular-frequency offset Ω (rad/s) and temperature T_K (K). At the Brillouin band (Ω ≈ 2π·11 GHz, 300 K) n_th ≈ 560; at the Raman peak (2π·13.2 THz) n_th ≈ 0.1. Uncertain T_K lifts through Particles (no per-particle branch — large ħ|Ω|/k_BT sends expm1 to Inf and n_th to 0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Nonlinear.thermal_phonon_number"},{"category":"section","location":"api/constants/#constants","page":"Constants","text":"Physical constants used throughout the codebase (speed of light, vacuum permittivity, etc.), re-exported under the u namespace at the Bifrost top level.","title":"Constants"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/constants/#Bifrost.Constants.u","page":"Constants","text":"u\n\nSingleton bundle of fundamental physical constants (CODATA 2022, SI units). Access fields directly:\n\njulia> using Bifrost\n\njulia> u.c\n2.99792458e8\n\nSee NaturalConstants for the available fields (c, ħ, kB).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Constants.u"},{"category":"type","location":"api/constants/#Bifrost.Constants.NaturalConstants","page":"Constants","text":"NaturalConstants\n\nBundle of fundamental physical constants in SI units, stored as Float64.\n\nFields\n\nc: speed of light in vacuum, m/s\nħ: reduced Planck constant, J·s\nkB: Boltzmann constant, J/K\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Constants.NaturalConstants"},{"category":"section","location":"api/utils/#utils","page":"Utils","text":"Layer-agnostic validation vocabulary — range checking and a \"not modeled\" exception — reused across the material, cross-section, and fiber layers.","title":"Utils"},{"category":"type","location":"api/utils/#Bifrost.Utils.UnsupportedPropertyError","page":"Utils","text":"UnsupportedPropertyError(property, owner)\n\nThrown when property (e.g. \"cte\") is intentionally not modeled for owner (a human-readable name such as \"fluorine-doped silica\").\n\nThis is distinct from a MethodError: the property is a deliberate omission from the model — either physically absent or not yet implemented — not an accidental dispatch failure. Callers may catch it by type to degrade gracefully rather than string-matching an error message.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Utils.UnsupportedPropertyError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/utils/#Bifrost.Utils.ValidRange","page":"Utils","text":"ValidRange(lo, hi, name)\n\nClosed interval [lo, hi] bounding the domain over which a physical model is valid, for a quantity labelled name (used only in error messages).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Utils.ValidRange"},{"category":"method","location":"api/utils/#Bifrost.Utils._check_range-Tuple{Any, ValidRange}","page":"Utils","text":"_check_range(value, range::ValidRange) -> value\n_check_range(values::NamedTuple, ranges::NamedTuple) -> nothing\n\nValidate value against range. The original value is returned unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.Utils._check_range"},{"category":"section","location":"path-geometry/#path-geometry.jl-—-concepts-and-integral-quantities","page":"Path geometry","text":"","title":"path-geometry.jl — concepts and integral quantities"},{"category":"section","location":"path-geometry/#The-sliding-(construction)-frame","page":"Path geometry","text":"Every segment is expressed in a local coordinate frame whose z-axis is the incoming tangent direction.  When segments are chained, the exit frame of one segment becomes the entry frame of the next, so tangent continuity is guaranteed by construction.  There is no need to specify absolute orientations; only the relative geometry of each segment matters.  This chained frame is internal to build: it interprets each segment's axis_angle and the connector boundary data, and is not exposed by the query API.","title":"The sliding (construction) frame"},{"category":"section","location":"path-geometry/#The-transported-(Bishop)-frame","page":"Path geometry","text":"The transverse frame returned by bishop_e1/bishop_e2/bishop_frame is the parallel-transported (Bishop) pair (e1, e2 = T̂ × e1):\n\nde1/ds = −(k⃗ · e1) T̂,        k⃗ ≡ dT̂/ds\n\nIt has zero twist about the tangent, is continuous along the whole path — through straights, inflections, and joints where the curvature direction jumps — and is the gauge in which the fiber layer expresses every birefringence axis.  It is anchored at s = 0 by a static lab-frame rule (Gram–Schmidt of the world axis least aligned with the launch tangent), and build transports it through every segment, connectors included, in one placement loop — so its continuity holds by construction.  See The transverse frame and the birefringence gauge for the physics.\n\nScalar fields along the path:\n\nCurvature κ(s): the magnitude of dT̂/ds, in 1/m — how fast the tangent direction turns.  A straight segment has κ = 0.  A circular bend of radius R has κ = 1/R.  This is the quantity integrated by total_turning_angle.  The full curvature vector k⃗(s) (global coordinates, zero on straights) is exposed by curvature_vector(path, s); the bend birefringence axis is its projection onto (e1, e2).\nGeometric torsion τgeom(s): the rate at which the osculating plane rotates about the tangent, in rad/m.  A planar curve has τgeom = 0; a helix has constant nonzero τ_geom.  This is a shape diagnostic only — in the transported gauge it never enters polarization propagation.\nSpin Ω(s): rate at which the fiber cross-section's frozen-in anisotropy axes rotate (imparted during draw), in rad/m, authored per spin run with spin!(pb; rate).  The rate may be constant or a callable function of run-local material arc length.  Spin is a material property; it does not rotate the transported frame.\n\n","title":"The transported (Bishop) frame"},{"category":"section","location":"path-geometry/#Integral-quantities-compared","page":"Path geometry","text":"Three scalar integrals are available.  They measure related but distinct things:","title":"Integral quantities compared"},{"category":"section","location":"path-geometry/#total_turning_angle(path)","page":"Path geometry","text":"∫ κ(s) ds\n\nThe integrated curvature κ(s) (defined above), in radians.  Measures how much the tangent direction T̂ has turned — the \"winding\" of the path in 3D. Independent of torsion and spin.  For a closed planar loop this equals 2π.","title":"total_turning_angle(path)"},{"category":"section","location":"path-geometry/#total_torsion(path)","page":"Path geometry","text":"∫ τ_geom(s) ds\n\nThe integrated geometric torsion, in radians.  Zero for paths made entirely of straight segments and circular bends (both have τ_geom = 0).  Nonzero for helices and other out-of-plane curves.  This is a property of the centerline shape alone; it does not depend on the fiber material or applied torques.","title":"total_torsion(path)"},{"category":"section","location":"path-geometry/#total_spin(path;-s_start,-s_end)","page":"Path geometry","text":"∫ Ω(s) ds\n\nThe integrated applied material spin, in radians.  Only counts the rates authored with spin!.  Knows nothing about the geometry of the centerline — a helix without spin contributes zero here.  This is the rotation of the intrinsic birefringence axes in the transported frame (together with twist_phase for elastic twist).\n\n","title":"total_spin(path; s_start, s_end)"},{"category":"section","location":"path-geometry/#Quick-reference","page":"Path geometry","text":"Function Integrand Shape Spin\ntotal_turning_angle κ(s) ✓ —\ntotal_torsion τ_geom(s) ✓ —\ntotal_spin Ω(s) — ✓\n\n","title":"Quick reference"},{"category":"section","location":"path-geometry/#Example:-helix-vs.-circular-loops","page":"Path geometry","text":"A helix with radius R, pitch-parameter h = pitch/(2π), and arc length L has:\n\nτ_geom = h / (R² + h²)   [constant along the helix]\ntotal_torsion = τ_geom · L\n\nA sequence of circular bend! segments forming a coil has τgeom = 0 everywhere, so `totaltorsion = 0— even though the fiber winds around in 3D. In both cases the transported frame (and with it the polarization gauge) never rotates about the tangent; what differs between the helix and the coil is how the **curvature direction** moves relative to that frame: continuously at rate τ_geom inside a helix, and by discrete jumps at the joints of a coil.  Both enter the optics through the projection ofcurvature_vector` onto the frame.","title":"Example: helix vs. circular loops"},{"category":"section","location":"developing/#Developing","page":"Developing","text":"This page is the source of authority for contributor workflow, extension recipes, and the Monte Carlo Measurements (MCM) compatibility contract. Repository-root files such as AGENTS.md and ARCHITECTURE.md summarize the hard invariants for agent tooling and cross-reference this page.","title":"Developing"},{"category":"section","location":"developing/#Development-setup","page":"Developing","text":"Install Julia and instantiate the project environment as described in the repository README.md (Installation section).","title":"Development setup"},{"category":"section","location":"developing/#Auxiliary-environment-(ext/dev-env)","page":"Developing","text":"Bifrost is not registered in any Julia registry — the root Project.toml is the package, so julia --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()' works directly there. Everything beyond running Bifrost itself — the notebooks and the documentation build — uses one separate environment, ext/dev-env/ (not a package extension). It resolves Bifrost to this checkout through a [sources] entry, so it instantiates from a fresh clone:\n\njulia ext/dev-env/install_kernel.jl\n\nThe script instantiates the environment and installs the checkout-local bifrost Jupyter kernel in which every Julia notebook runs (see ext/dev-env/README.md). test/test_notebook_kernels.jl fails if a Julia notebook names any other kernel. The test suite keeps its own test/Project.toml, which Pkg.test() requires.\n\nWhen creating, revising, or auditing inline documentation for Julia code, use the julia-docstrings skill (registered at the account/agent level).","title":"Auxiliary environment (ext/dev-env)"},{"category":"section","location":"developing/#Git-hooks-(required-after-cloning)","page":"Developing","text":"Two pre-commit hooks, configured in .pre-commit-config.yaml, guard every commit:\n\nan nbstripout pre-commit hook strips cell outputs — base64 image/HTML blobs and execution counts — from authored notebooks so binary payloads never enter git history;\na local commit-msg hook strips Claude Code attribution (session backlinks, bylines, Co-Authored-By trailers) from commit messages.\n\nNeither is active until each clone installs them, so after cloning install the dev dependencies (which include pre-commit) and register both hook stages:\n\nuv sync            # installs the `dev` dependency group, including pre-commit\npre-commit install --install-hooks -t pre-commit -t commit-msg\n\nBecause pre-commit installs into .git/hooks/, this repo does not set core.hooksPath. If pre-commit install reports \"Cowardly refusing to install hooks with core.hooksPath set\", a stale custom hooks path is lingering from an older checkout — clear it with git config --unset-all core.hooksPath, then re-run the install.\n\nOnce installed, staged notebooks are stripped automatically on every commit. Stripping is idempotent: committing an already-clean notebook is a byte-level no-op.\n\n.gitattributes is the single source of truth for which notebooks are in scope. The wrapper .githooks/nbstripout-clean.py resolves it with git check-attr and processes only notebooks marked filter=nbstripout-clean, so the pre-commit configuration never restates the scope and cannot drift from it. Third-party notebooks under test/reference-data/ and the legacy notebook under test/legacy-python/ carry no such attribute and are never rewritten.\n\nThe wrapper delegates the actual stripping to the upstream nbstripout package, adding one behavior it lacks: a notebook whose outputs are already stripped keeps its original bytes, whatever JSON serialization it happens to use. Plain nbstripout reserializes such a notebook into its own canonical form, which surfaces as a whole-file diff when the notebook was last written by some other tool. The -clean suffix in the attribute name keeps this wrapper's namespace distinct from the filter that nbstripout --install would register, so the upstream tool cannot displace it.","title":"Git hooks (required after cloning)"},{"category":"section","location":"developing/#Running-the-notebook-hook-by-hand","page":"Developing","text":"To strip on demand — same wrapper, same scope, same pinned nbstripout the commit hook uses — run it across the whole repository:\n\nuv run pre-commit run nbstripout-clean --all-files --verbose","title":"Running the notebook hook by hand"},{"category":"section","location":"developing/#Running-the-tests","page":"Developing","text":"Run the Julia test suite from the repository root:\n\njulia --project=. test/runtests.jl\n\nThis command skips the plot-recipe tests (test/test_plot_recipes.jl): RecipesBase is a weak dependency, absent from the root environment. Pkg.test() — what CI runs — supplies it from test/Project.toml and runs them too:\n\njulia --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test()'\n\nTests are classified by the taxonomy in AGENTS.md (T-PHYSICS, T-VALIDATION, T-SIM-REGRESSION, T-GUARDRAIL, and visual demos); place new tests in one of those categories and follow its standard. Any change that touches an uncertain-input code path must add or extend an MCM test — see Testing MCM changes below.","title":"Running the tests"},{"category":"section","location":"developing/#Building-the-documentation","page":"Developing","text":"Documentation is built with Documenter.jl. Source markdown lives under docs/src/; docs/make.jl defines the build and navigation. A GitHub Actions workflow (.github/workflows/Documenter.yml) rebuilds and deploys the site to GitHub Pages.\n\nBuild the docs locally, from the repository root:\n\njulia --project=ext/dev-env -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'\njulia --project=ext/dev-env docs/make.jl\n\nThe rendered site is written to docs/build/ (open docs/build/index.html).","title":"Building the documentation"},{"category":"section","location":"developing/#Code-organization","page":"Developing","text":"Use this pattern for paired value/derivative quantities in the material, cross-section, and fiber layers:\n\n\"\"\"\n    _quantity(args...)\n    quantity(args...)\n    quantity_dω(args...)\n\nShort description of the physical quantity.\n\n`quantity` returns ... in SI units.\n`quantity_dω` returns ... in SI units.\n\"\"\"\nfunction _quantity(args...)\n    # Bind local physical symbols explicitly.\n    # Compute both the value and derivative from the same nearby expression.\n    return (x, x_dω)\nend\n\nquantity(args...) = _quantity(args...)[1]\nquantity_dω(args...) = _quantity(args...)[2]\n\nThe private _quantity method owns the calculation; the public value and derivative accessors are compact tuple selectors. Do not introduce response structs, style-dispatch markers, or derived-quantity bags to carry intermediate physics unless there is a concrete need beyond pairing a value with its derivative. Name derivatives for the differentiated variable (_dω for angular frequency, _dV for normalized frequency); avoid prime notation in public APIs. Document the private implementation and its accessors in one Documenter-compatible block, and state returned SI units directly.","title":"Code organization"},{"category":"section","location":"developing/#Extending-Bifrost","page":"Developing","text":"The package is layered with strictly hierarchical dependencies — material ≤ cross section ≤ fiber, geometry ≤ fiber — with the propagation layer consuming callable generators. See ARCHITECTURE.md for the layer-to-file map. Each extension point below names the layer it belongs to. Whatever you add, write code that satisfies the MCM compatibility contract from the start: retrofitting Particles support is much harder than designing for it.","title":"Extending Bifrost"},{"category":"section","location":"developing/#Adding-a-path-segment","page":"Developing","text":"Geometry layer (src/geometry/).\n\nDefine a struct as a subtype of AbstractPathSegment with a meta::Vector{AbstractMeta} field (and a twist::TwistRate field if it carries mechanical twist), an outer constructor taking the other fields positionally in declaration order plus a meta keyword (field meta, strain and model projection rebuild a segment through it), and a Base.show.\nImplement the local-geometry interface tabulated in the AbstractPathSegment docstring — arc_length, curvature, geometric_torsion, position_local, tangent_local, _curvature_vector_local, _parallel_transport_local, _normal_local, _binormal_local, end_position_local, _end_frame_local — in closed form, in the segment's local frame (start at the origin, heading +z).\nDeclare _length_fields in path-geometry-perturb.jl: the fields an isotropic strain multiplies. The fallback errors, so a forgotten declaration fails loudly.\nIf the segment's curvature is identically zero, add _segment_is_straight(::YourSegment) = true.\nA connector-like segment, whose shape depends on where it is placed, stays unresolved when authored and resolves in _resolve_at_placement(seg, pos, frame_mat), which build calls with the entry position and construction frame. JumpTo is the model: it resolves into a CubicConnector, declares _length_fields(::JumpTo) = (), errors in _scale_length_fields (the fiber strains it by a target-length re-solve), and errors on every geometry query until built.\nAdd a verb on PathBuilder that appends through _append_segment!, so the segment joins the current spin run, and guardrail tests in test/test_path_geometry.jl.","title":"Adding a path segment"},{"category":"section","location":"developing/#Adding-a-birefringence-source","page":"Developing","text":"Fiber assembly layer (src/fiber/fiber-path.jl).\n\nDefine a struct as a subtype of AbstractBirefringenceSource.\nImplement generator_K_contribution(source, s) — returns the local 2×2 generator contribution.\nImplement generator_Kω_contribution(source, s) — returns ∂K/∂ω. May return a zero matrix if not yet modeled.\nDeclare coverage_intervals(source) and breakpoints(source). Every source must cover the full fiber domain [s_start, s_end]; gaps are a hard error, not silent zero. The Fiber merges breakpoints globally and the propagator never steps across one.\nExtend the fiber-level generator assembly so the new source contributes to K(s) and Kω(s).\nAdd guardrail tests before physics tests.\n\nChanging the source interface contracts themselves (generator_K_contribution, generator_Kω_contribution) requires user authorization — see AGENTS.md.","title":"Adding a birefringence source"},{"category":"section","location":"developing/#Adding-a-material","page":"Developing","text":"Material layer (src/material/).\n\nDefine every method in MATERIAL_PROPERTY_API (src/material/material-properties.jl) for the new type, with untyped non-material arguments. A property the model does not cover throws UnsupportedPropertyError (see silica-fluorinated.jl), and a material with no validity restrictions declares runtime_range(::M) = NamedTuple(). A missing method fails precompilation with MaterialAPIError.\n\nImplement _refractive_index(material, λ, T_K) returning (n, n_dω). The public refractive_index(material, λ, T_K) and refractive_index_dω(material, λ, T_K) accessors select the corresponding tuple element. For Sellmeier-form materials the helpers in src/material/material-properties.jl (_sellmeier_index_from_coefficients, _sellmeier_index_from_coefficients_dω, and the _evaluate_sellmeier_* utilities for parameter-dependent coefficients) do the heavy lifting; see silica.jl and germania.jl for worked examples. Routing all spectral evaluation through the Sellmeier helpers also gives Particles compatibility for free; the helpers are branch-free and coercion-free.","title":"Adding a material"},{"category":"section","location":"developing/#Adding-a-cross-section","page":"Developing","text":"Cross-section layer (src/fiber-cross-section/).\n\nThe base interface lives in src/fiber-cross-section/cross-section.jl; use step-index.jl as the model implementation. A cross section converts material properties into guided-index, dispersion, nonlinearity, and local birefringence response coefficients, and must keep operating wavelength a per-query argument rather than stored state.","title":"Adding a cross section"},{"category":"section","location":"developing/#mcm-compatibility","page":"Developing","text":"BIFROST propagates measurement uncertainty with MonteCarloMeasurements.jl (MCM): an uncertain input is a Particles ensemble that flows through the entire computation, so one simulation yields a distribution of outputs. This only works if every function on the path of an uncertain value lifts through Particles. This section is the contract for keeping it that way.","title":"MCM compatibility"},{"category":"section","location":"developing/#What-must-lift","page":"Developing","text":"Particles may arrive on these inputs:\n\ntemperature: the user sets uncertainty through the thermal :T_K segment annotation; T_ref_K is a deterministic baseline. \nbend, twist, tension, and axis-ratio properties,\nsegment shrinkage and field-level MetaAdd/MetaMul perturbations,\nthe per-entry element type of the Jones matrix J and sensitivity G.\n\nThe files on those paths — all of src/material/, src/fiber-cross-section/, src/geometry/path-geometry*.jl, src/fiber/fiber-path.jl, and src/path-integral.jl — must obey the rules below on every uncertain-input slot.","title":"What must lift"},{"category":"section","location":"developing/#The-rules","page":"Developing","text":"No annotations or coercions that exclude Particles. Particles <: Real but not <: AbstractFloat, so ::Float64 or ::AbstractFloat on an uncertain-input slot silently excludes the ensemble. Leave those slots unannotated. Never coerce with Float64(·) on a path that may carry Particles: promotion does the right thing, coercion destroys the ensemble.\nNo per-particle branching. A conditional on a Particles value is an error (which branch would the ensemble take?). Either write branch-free code (sign, flipsign, clamp, elementwise arithmetic), or reduce to a representative scalar first and branch on that. The shared reducers live in src/mcm-helpers.jl: use _mcm_pmax for worst-case pmaximum reduction and _mcm_pmean for pmean nominalization. Loop bounds and quadrature limits must be deterministic Float64.\nNo generic dense linear algebra on Particles matrices. LinearAlgebra.exp (Padé with pivoting), opnorm, and eigvals hit code paths that do not lift.  Bifrost.jl implements private variants that work with MCM. For example exp_jones_generator and exp_block_upper_triangular_2x2 (a closed-form Fréchet derivative of the 2×2 exponential) for propagation, and output_dgd_2x2 for DGD extraction (output_dgd uses eigvals and is Float64-only).\nNominalize deliberately, not defensively. Reducing a Particles value to a scalar is correct for control flow (step acceptance, table bisection, Newton iteration counts) but wrong for results — an accumulated phase or coefficient must keep the full ensemble. When you nominalize, say why in a short comment.\nLength-changing meta is resolved per lane, not on the breakpoint grid. The breakpoint partition is deterministic Float64 (control flow), but a segment whose physical length is uncertain (:T_K, :tension) must still contribute each lane's full physical length to results. Every lane builds its own scalar geometry, connectors included, and the ensemble generator maps each lane's segment onto the shared nominal grid by the stretch τ_cj = A_cj/L0c (an MCM Particles); see Stages 1 and 3 of The solve workflow. Do not reintroduce a path that integrates over the nominalized segment length only.\nNo unsafe comparisons. Do not use MonteCarloMeasurements.unsafe_comparisons; tests and library code must make every reduction from Particles explicit at the comparison or control-flow site.","title":"The rules"},{"category":"section","location":"developing/#Ensemble-wide-adaptive-decisions","page":"Developing","text":"The adaptive step controller makes one decision per step for the whole ensemble — it cannot take half a step for some particles and a full step for others. _mcm_pmax collapses the error metric via pmaximum, so the worst-case particle drives the step size. That is conservative; a pmean-based reduction is the documented performance compromise if step counts become too large under tight tolerances. _mcm_pmax is the single switch point for that change.\n\nThe fiber layer hands the solver an ensemble as lanes (_Lanes, src/ensemble-lanes.jl): one static 2×2 per lane, each advanced by the scalar kernels, with _mcm_pmax taking the worst lane. A Particles-valued generator through the same solver gives the same bits — test/test_mcm_correctness.jl pins it — so a solver kernel stays elementwise and branch-free under both.","title":"Ensemble-wide adaptive decisions"},{"category":"section","location":"developing/#Testing-MCM-changes","page":"Developing","text":"test/test_mcm_correctness.jl is the primary MCM suite; thermal, tension, and perturbation coverage also lives in test/test_fiber_thermal.jl, test/test_fiber_tension.jl, and test/test_path_geometry_perturb.jl. When you touch an uncertain-input code path, extend one of these (or add a new MCM test) so the change is exercised with Particles inputs, not just Float64.\n\nConventions:\n\ndo not wrap MCM test blocks in MonteCarloMeasurements.unsafe_comparisons;\nstructural invariants (\"breakpoints are sorted and deduplicated\") should reduce uncertain values explicitly through _mcm_pmean, _mcm_pmax, or a documented nominalized scalar;\ncompare pmean of a Particles result against the nominal Float64 baseline.","title":"Testing MCM changes"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Usage","page":"Usage","text":"","title":"Usage"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Building-Blocks","page":"Usage","text":"These files are intentionally useful on their own:\n\nFile Standalone role\nmaterial-properties.jl Together with the other files in src/material/, provides material constants and spectra; no path or fiber geometry.\npath-geometry.jl Three-dimensional path construction and geometric queries; no optics.\npath-integral.jl Adaptive propagation for callable K(s) and Kω(s) generators.\n\nThe fiber-specific layers combine those pieces:\n\nFile How it extends the standalone pieces\ncross-section.jl Together with the other files in src/fiber-cross-section/, adds step-index fiber optics and birefringence responses.\nfiber-path.jl Binds path geometry to a cross section and assembles bend/twist K and Kω.","title":"Building Blocks"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Current-Model","page":"Usage","text":"High-level authoring is path based:\n\nAuthor geometry on a PathBuilder: segment verbs append segments in order, and spin! opens material-spin runs.\nPlace it with build(pb), producing a Path for geometry queries.\nBind it to a FiberCrossSection subtype with Fiber(pb; cross_section, T_ref_K), which builds the path and applies its :T_K/:tension strain, or with FiberModel(pb; cross_section, T_ref_K) when authored values are uncertain.\nPropagate at a requested wavelength with propagate_fiber(fiber; λ_m=...).\n\nA path starts at PathBuilder(; point, outgoing_tangent) (default: the origin, heading +z) and ends at the exit of its last segment. The segment verbs and the segments they append:\n\nVerb Segment\nstraight! StraightSegment\nbend! BendSegment (circular arc)\nhelix! HelixSegment\ncatenary! CatenarySegment\njumpto! JumpTo (connector)\n\nEach segment is written in the frame the previous one ends in, so tangent continuity holds by construction and axis_angle orients a bend, helix or catenary in that frame. jumpto! is the exception: it appends a JumpTo, a connector to a global target point (with an optional global incoming_tangent there), whose shape depends on where it is placed. build resolves it into a CubicConnector — a G1 cubic Hermite curve matching position and tangent at both ends, curvature free — honoring min_bend_radius or a target_length (src/geometry/path-geometry-connector.jl). A connector may sit anywhere in a path, any number of times, with segments after it.\n\nMechanical twist is set per segment with the twist keyword on the segment verbs as a constant rate (rad/m, including an MCM Particles). Vary twist along a path with consecutive constant-twist segments. Material spin is authored in runs: spin!(pb; rate) covers every segment appended after it until the next spin!, with rate nothing, a constant, or a Function of run-local material arc length; the spin phase is continuous along the whole path.\n\nThe optical Fiber stores:\n\nthe built Path,\nthe FiberCrossSection,\na reference temperature T_ref_K,\nthe fiber domain [s_start, s_end].\n\nThe operating wavelength is not stored on Fiber. It is supplied per query to generator_K(fiber, λ_m), generator_Kω(fiber, λ_m), propagate_fiber, and propagate_fiber_sensitivity.\n\nFor runnable code, see the Quick start and Examples. The Theory pages cover the generator formalism, propagation, and DGD sensitivity.","title":"Current Model"},{"category":"section","location":"api/fiber-path/#fiber-path","page":"FiberPath","text":"Fiber assembly: binds an authored PathBuilder (or a built Path) to a FiberCrossSection and reference temperature, assembles the fiber-level bend and twist generators K(s) and Kω(s), and applies meta-driven perturbations and thermal length scaling when the Fiber constructor builds the authored geometry.\n\nThe module docstring, which states the generators' sign conventions, is rendered in The transverse frame and the birefringence gauge, §5.","title":"FiberPath"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-path/#Bifrost.FiberPath.Fiber-Tuple{FiberModel}","page":"FiberPath","text":"Fiber(model::FiberModel) -> Fiber\n\nThe boundary crossing from model to geometry: build the fiber of a fully deterministic model. This is the funnel propagate_fiber itself builds through — once for a deterministic model, per scalarized particle for an ensemble — and the way one realization is materialized (Fiber(scalarize(model, i))). An uncertainty-carrying model is rejected.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberPath.Fiber"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-path/#Bifrost.FiberPath.Fiber-Tuple{PathBuilder}","page":"FiberPath","text":"Fiber(pb::PathBuilder; cross_section, T_ref_K=DEFAULT_T_REF_K) -> Fiber\n\nBuild a fiber from the authored path pb, applying perturbation meta during the build.\n\nThermal :T_K annotations are resolved here using α_lin = cte(cross_section.cladding_material, T_ref_K) — each thermal segment's length-dimensioned fields are scaled by 1 + α_lin·ΔT. Field-level MetaAdd/MetaMul are then applied by the geometry build. A jumpto! connector carrying its own :T_K thermally expands too: it re-solves to its nominal arc length scaled by τ while still landing at its fixed target.\n\nPer-segment axial tension :tension (absolute Newtons, e.g. MetaAdd(:tension, 0.5)) is interpreted here too and plays a dual role exactly like :T_K: it elongates the segment by (1 + ε) with the axial strain ε = F / (π·r_cladding²·E) (E = youngs_modulus(cladding_material, T_ref_K), r₁ = r_cladding(cross_section)) and it sets the segment's axial-tension photoelastic birefringence (a linear birefringence on the bend eigen-axis, so it is zero on a straight segment; recovered downstream via tension(f, s)). Thermal and tension length scalings compose multiplicatively. A jumpto! connector carrying :tension elongates the same way. The :tension meta is left on the segment for on-demand recovery, as :T_K is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberPath.Fiber"},{"category":"type","location":"api/fiber-path/#Bifrost.FiberPath.FiberModel","page":"FiberPath","text":"FiberModel(pb::PathBuilder; cross_section, T_ref_K = DEFAULT_T_REF_K) -> FiberModel\n\nBundle the authored path pb with its cross section and reference temperature without building anything. Fiber(model) builds exactly what Fiber(pb; ...) binds, so wrapping a builder in a model is always a drop-in substitution for binding it directly. The model stores a deep copy of pb as path, so later authoring on pb does not affect the model.\n\nA FiberModel is the uncertainty-carrying form of a fiber: any model value — segment fields, MetaAdd/MetaMul payloads (including :T_K and :tension), start coordinates, connector targets, spin and twist rates, cross-section fields — may be a MonteCarloMeasurements.Particles ensemble. All ensemble values must share one aligned particle count (see particle_count); T_ref_K must be deterministic (uncertain temperature belongs on segment :T_K meta).\n\nUncertainty must be carried by Particles values directly: a raw distribution payload (e.g. MetaAdd(:T_K, Normal(0.0, 2.0))) is rejected — wrap it as Particles(n, Normal(0.0, 2.0)) so the ensemble size and joint particle alignment are explicit. Function-valued rates must return deterministic values; a function returning Particles is rejected (MCM hidden inside functions cannot be scalarized).\n\nExecute the model with propagate_fiber(model; λ_m): a deterministic model runs one scalar solve, an uncertain model runs the ensemble execution (docs/src/solve-workflow.md). Crossing from model to geometry happens through Fiber(model) — the scalar-only funnel propagate_fiber itself builds through — and one realization of an uncertain model is materialized with scalarize (Fiber(scalarize(model, i))).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberPath.FiberModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/fiber-path/#Bifrost.FiberPath.MaterialPeriodError","page":"FiberPath","text":"MaterialPeriodError(a, period, mismatch)\n\nThe material-frame generator did not repeat after the period declared for the interval starting at reference coordinate a: at a point and one period later it differed by mismatch, relative to its size. Raised instead of raising a wrong one-period propagator to a power.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberPath.MaterialPeriodError"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-path/#Bifrost.FiberPath._ensemble_lane_tables-Tuple{Vector{<:Fiber}, Fiber}","page":"FiberPath","text":"_ensemble_lane_tables(lanes, nominal) -> (grid, lane_paths)\n\nThe nominal fiber's built path (the shared reference grid) and every lane fiber's built Path, index-aligned by segment-table row: row c of lane_paths[j] is lane j's realization of the same authored segment as row c of grid. Throws when a lane's row count differs from the grid's — impossible for lanes scalarized from one authored model (uncertainty never changes the authored topology), so a mismatch means the inputs are not realizations of the same model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberPath._ensemble_lane_tables"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-path/#Bifrost.FiberPath._generator_K_ensemble-Tuple{Vector{<:Fiber}, Fiber, Real}","page":"FiberPath","text":"_generator_K_ensemble(lanes, nominal, λ_m) -> (u -> 2×2 Complex{Particles})\n\n_generator_K_lanes stacked entrywise: lane j's scalar 2×2 becomes particle j of every Complex{Particles} entry, the generator-side mirror of the results layer's ensemble assembly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberPath._generator_K_ensemble"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-path/#Bifrost.FiberPath._generator_K_lanes-Tuple{Vector{<:Fiber}, Fiber, Real}","page":"FiberPath","text":"_generator_K_lanes(lanes, nominal, λ_m) -> (u -> _Lanes of static 2×2)\n\nThe ensemble fiber generator the solver integrates: lane j of the returned closure's value at u is τ_cj · K_j(u_j) — lane j's own scalar generator_K evaluated in lane j's own built geometry at the remapped coordinate — with the reference axis and breakpoint grid taken from nominal. See docs/src/solve-workflow.md for the change of variables.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberPath._generator_K_lanes"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-path/#Bifrost.FiberPath._generator_K_material-Tuple{Fiber, Real}","page":"FiberPath","text":"_generator_K_material(f, λ_m) -> (u -> static 2×2)\n\ngenerator_K in the material frame: K̃ = R(θ)ᵀ·K·R(θ) − ξ·G at reference coordinate u, with θ the accumulated spin and twist angle and ξ its rate (see src/fiber/material-frame.jl). The fiber layer integrates this closure; the transported-frame generator_K stays the public generator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberPath._generator_K_material"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-path/#Bifrost.FiberPath._generator_K_material_lanes-Tuple{Vector{<:Fiber}, Fiber, Real}","page":"FiberPath","text":"_generator_K_material_lanes(lanes, nominal, λ_m) -> (u -> _Lanes of static 2×2)\n\n_generator_K_lanes in each lane's own material frame: lane j is τ_cj · K̃_j(u_j) with K̃_j lane j's _generator_K_material. The stretch scales the frame term with the rest, τ·ξ_j being the rate of θ_j in u.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberPath._generator_K_material_lanes"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-path/#Bifrost.FiberPath._generator_Kω_ensemble-Tuple{Vector{<:Fiber}, Fiber, Real}","page":"FiberPath","text":"_generator_Kω_ensemble(lanes, nominal, λ_m) -> (u -> 2×2 Complex{Particles})\n\n_generator_Kω_lanes stacked entrywise, as _generator_K_ensemble stacks _generator_K_lanes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberPath._generator_Kω_ensemble"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-path/#Bifrost.FiberPath._generator_Kω_lanes-Tuple{Vector{<:Fiber}, Fiber, Real}","page":"FiberPath","text":"_generator_Kω_lanes(lanes, nominal, λ_m) -> (u -> _Lanes of static 2×2)\n\nAngular-frequency-derivative counterpart of _generator_K_lanes: the same routing, remap, and stretch, stacking each lane's generator_Kω.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberPath._generator_Kω_lanes"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-path/#Bifrost.FiberPath._generator_Kω_material-Tuple{Fiber, Real}","page":"FiberPath","text":"_generator_Kω_material(f, λ_m) -> (u -> static 2×2)\n\ngenerator_Kω in the material frame, R(θ)ᵀ·Kω·R(θ): the frame does not depend on ω, so it adds no term of its own.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberPath._generator_Kω_material"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-path/#Bifrost.FiberPath._generator_Kω_material_lanes-Tuple{Vector{<:Fiber}, Fiber, Real}","page":"FiberPath","text":"_generator_Kω_material_lanes(lanes, nominal, λ_m) -> (u -> _Lanes of static 2×2)\n\nAngular-frequency-derivative counterpart of _generator_K_material_lanes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberPath._generator_Kω_material_lanes"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-path/#Bifrost.FiberPath._material_frame-Tuple{Fiber}","page":"FiberPath","text":"_material_frame(f) -> ((a, b) -> (θa, θb))\n\nThe material angle at both ends of a solver interval [a, b] of reference coordinate, each taken in the interval's own row (the row of its midpoint): an interval never spans a row boundary, and a row's ends are read from that row, not its neighbour.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberPath._material_frame"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-path/#Bifrost.FiberPath._material_frame_lanes-Tuple{Vector{<:Fiber}, Fiber}","page":"FiberPath","text":"_material_frame_lanes(lanes, nominal) -> ((a, b) -> (θa, θb))\n\n_material_frame for an ensemble: each lane's material angle at the ends of the nominal interval [a, b], remapped into the lane's own row as the generator remaps (_ensemble_closure), as _Lanes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberPath._material_frame_lanes"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-path/#Bifrost.FiberPath._material_periods-Tuple{Fiber}","page":"FiberPath","text":"_material_periods(f) -> ((a, b) -> period or nothing)\n\nThe period of the material-frame generator in reference coordinate on the row holding the solver interval [a, b], or nothing where it has none (see _material_period).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberPath._material_periods"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-path/#Bifrost.FiberPath._material_periods_lanes-Tuple{Vector{<:Fiber}, Fiber}","page":"FiberPath","text":"_material_periods_lanes(lanes, nominal) -> ((a, b) -> period or nothing)\n\n_material_periods for an ensemble: the period in reference coordinate shared by every lane on the row holding [a, b] — lane j's period P_j over its stretch τ_cj — or nothing when any lane has none or the lanes' periods differ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberPath._material_periods_lanes"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-path/#Bifrost.FiberPath._nominal_realization-Tuple{FiberModel}","page":"FiberPath","text":"_nominal_realization(model::FiberModel) -> FiberModel\n\nThe ensemble-mean realization: it recursively projects every Particles value in the path with mcmpmean (including MetaAdd and MetaMul payloads), giving the deterministic model whose build is the nominal geometry. propagate_fiber stamps FiberPropagationResults.fiber with this build for ensemble runs. A fully deterministic model is returned unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberPath._nominal_realization"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-path/#Bifrost.FiberPath._projected-Union{Tuple{F}, Tuple{Any, F}} where F","page":"FiberPath","text":"_projected(x, resolve) -> value\n_scalarized(x, i) -> value\n\nStructural ensemble projection: replace every Particles value reachable in x with resolve(x) — its i-th particle for _scalarized(x, i), its ensemble mean for _nominal_realization — rebuilding containers and model structs around the projected leaves. Values that carry no ensemble content are returned unchanged (same object). Function-valued rates are wrapped with a runtime determinism guard. Uncertainty-carrying types without a reconstruction method error loudly rather than being silently passed through.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberPath._projected"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-path/#Bifrost.FiberPath.circular_birefringence_generator-Tuple{Any}","page":"FiberPath","text":"circular_birefringence_generator(Δβc) -> 2×2 matrix\n\nLocal Jones generator for circular birefringence (optical activity) with rotation rate Δβc. Real antisymmetric — a pure SO(2) rotation generator. Used by the mechanical-twist generator.\n\nΔβc = β_LC − β_RC is signed (brittonlab/Bifrost.jl#87; sign conventions: Bifrost.FiberPath). A positive Δβc (left-circular slower) rotates the axes clockwise (e1 → −e2, r-rotatory) at rate Δβc/2 per unit length. A right-handed mechanical twist gives Δβc < 0, hence the l-rotatory rotation that follows the twist (Ulrich & Simon, doi:10.1364/AO.18.002241).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberPath.circular_birefringence_generator"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-path/#Bifrost.FiberPath.common_mode_phase-Tuple{Fiber}","page":"FiberPath","text":"common_mode_phase(fiber; λ_m)\n\nAccumulate the common-mode (polarization-averaged) optical phase ∫₀ᴸ β_mean(s) ds in radians along fiber at operating wavelength λ_m (metres).\n\nβ_mean(s) is the polarization-averaged propagation constant the traceless Jones generators drop: the fundamental LP₀₁ constant β₀ = propagation_constant(cross_section, λ_m, T(s)) = k₀·n_eff, plus the scalar isotropic photoelastic shift (frozen axisymmetric draw stress, bend stress, and any applied axial tension; see [β_shift_common_mode]). The common-mode shift raises both modes equally, so it stays out of the differential generator and leaves the DGD untouched.\n\nThe integral runs over the same segment-table rows the differential generator uses (generator_K, issue #125): each finite-length authored segment contributes β_mean(s_mid)·L0, where L0 is the segment's physical arc length on a built path and s_mid = L0/2 is its physical midpoint (zero-length rows add nothing). Length-changing meta (:T_K thermal expansion, :tension elongation) scales the segment length fields at build, so it is already in L0 and enters the optical path length exactly and per particle under MCM — the per-particle physical-length fluctuation flows into the spread of Φ (and of the transit time τ = Φ/ω), matching the differential path.\n\nTemperature, tension, and ellipticity are piecewise-constant per geometry segment, and curvature is constant on straight and constant-radius bend segments, so on those segments β_mean is constant and β_mean(s_mid)·L_phys is exact (not a quadrature). On varying-curvature segments (G2 connectors, helices, catenaries) the bend-stress term varies within the segment, so the midpoint value is a second-order-accurate sample of that small correction. The product and sum are ordinary arithmetic (no scalar coercions or comparisons), so the return value lifts through MCM Particles when its inputs are.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberPath.common_mode_phase"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-path/#Bifrost.FiberPath.fiber_breakpoints-Tuple{Fiber}","page":"FiberPath","text":"fiber_breakpoints(fiber) -> Vector\n\nThe globally merged set of s breakpoints (segment/spin-run boundaries plus every AbstractBirefringenceSource's own declared breakpoints) that the propagator must not step across.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberPath.fiber_breakpoints"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-path/#Bifrost.FiberPath.generator_K-Tuple{Fiber, Real}","page":"FiberPath","text":"generator_K(fiber, λ_m) -> (s -> 2×2 ComplexF64)\n\nReturn a closure that evaluates the local Jones generator K(s) at the given operating wavelength λ_m (metres), dispatching on the fiber's cross section. Temperature is fiber.T_ref_K.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberPath.generator_K"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-path/#Bifrost.FiberPath.generator_K-Tuple{Fiber, StepIndexCrossSection, Real}","page":"FiberPath","text":"generator_K(fiber, xs::StepIndexCrossSection, λ_m) -> (u -> 2×2 matrix)\n\nAssemble the step-index local Jones generator as a closure of the reference (nominal) arc length u. Each authored segment is propagated in its own fixed reference interval [u0, u0+L0]; the closure maps u to the physical local position sl = u - u0 and evaluates the bend, twist, ellipticity, and tension terms there. On a built path L0 is the segment's physical arc length, so u coincides with physical arc length and the generator is returned directly; length-changing meta (:T_K, :tension) is already in the built geometry (issue #125). The reference grid is fiber_breakpoints, so the propagator's interval decomposition is unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberPath.generator_K"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-path/#Bifrost.FiberPath.generator_Kω-Tuple{Fiber, Real}","page":"FiberPath","text":"generator_Kω(fiber, λ_m) -> (s -> 2×2 ComplexF64)\n\nFrequency-derivative counterpart of generator_K.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberPath.generator_Kω"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-path/#Bifrost.FiberPath.generator_Kω-Tuple{Fiber, StepIndexCrossSection, Real}","page":"FiberPath","text":"generator_Kω(fiber, xs::StepIndexCrossSection, λ_m) -> (u -> 2×2 matrix)\n\nAngular-frequency-derivative counterpart of generator_K: the same segment-local reference-coordinate evaluation (issue #125), assembling the ∂ω of each birefringence term.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberPath.generator_Kω"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-path/#Bifrost.FiberPath.linear_birefringence_generator-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"FiberPath","text":"linear_birefringence_generator(Δβ, c2φ, s2φ) -> 2×2 matrix\n\nLocal Jones generator for a linear retarder with retardance per unit length Δβ and eigen-axes oriented at angle φ in the propagation frame, encoded via c2φ = cos(2φ) and s2φ = sin(2φ). Traceless, anti-Hermitian times i. A static 2×2 (SMatrix), like every generator term. Shared by the bend, core-ellipticity, and asymmetric-thermal-stress generators.\n\nΔβ is signed: Δβ > 0 makes the axis at φ slow. Sign conventions: Bifrost.FiberPath.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberPath.linear_birefringence_generator"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-path/#Bifrost.FiberPath.particle_count-Tuple{FiberModel}","page":"FiberPath","text":"particle_count(model::FiberModel) -> Union{Nothing, Int}\n\nThe aligned ensemble size shared by every Particles value in the model, or nothing for a fully deterministic model. Throws when two model values carry different particle counts — mixed ensemble sizes have no joint interpretation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberPath.particle_count"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-path/#Bifrost.FiberPath.scalarize-Tuple{FiberModel, Integer}","page":"FiberPath","text":"scalarize(model::FiberModel, i::Integer) -> FiberModel\n\nThe scalar realization of model at aligned particle index i: every Particles value anywhere in the model is replaced by its i-th particle, so the result carries no uncertainty and can be built and propagated as an ordinary scalar fiber (Fiber(scalarize(model, i))). One index selects the whole joint realization — per-value index mixing is deliberately impossible.\n\nA fully deterministic model is returned unchanged for any i.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberPath.scalarize"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-path/#Bifrost.FiberPath.temperature-Tuple{Fiber, Real}","page":"FiberPath","text":"temperature(f::Fiber, s) -> T\n\nTemperature (K) at fiber arc length s, derived on demand: T_ref_K + ΔT, where ΔT is the :T_K excursion carried by the segment containing s (located via local_segment(f.path, s)). A fiber with no :T_K returns T_ref_K everywhere — meta_combine(0.0, seg, :T_K) collapses to 0.0 when the segment carries none, so no sentinel is needed. The cross-section birefringences are evaluated at this temperature (asymmetric thermal stress ∝ |T_soft − T|, and the indices shift with T), so a :T_K segment's optical response — not only its length — reflects the excursion. ΔT may be Particles; the returned value carries it into the MCM-safe cross-section T_K slot. Sibling of curvature(f, s): no thermal state is stored on Fiber.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberPath.temperature"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-path/#Bifrost.FiberPath.tension-Tuple{Fiber, Real}","page":"FiberPath","text":"tension(f::Fiber, s) -> F\n\nAxial tension (N) at fiber arc length s, derived on demand from the :tension meta carried by the segment containing s (via local_segment(f.path, s)); 0 where none is present — meta_combine(0.0, seg, :tension) collapses to 0.0 when the segment carries none, so no sentinel is needed. Consumed by tension_generator_K (axial-tension photoelastic birefringence). May be Particles. Sibling of temperature(f, s) — no tension state is stored on Fiber.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberPath.tension"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-path/#Bifrost.FiberPath.β_shift_common_mode-Tuple{Fiber, Real, Real}","page":"FiberPath","text":"β_shift_common_mode(fiber, s, λ_m)\n\nThe return value is the polarization-averaged propagation-constant shift (rad/m) at arc length s, added to the unperturbed β_mean = k₀·n_eff by [common_mode_phase].\n\nSums the active isotropic photoelastic mechanisms. The axisymmetric frozen draw stress ([n_shift_stress_thermal], present whenever the core and cladding thermal-expansion coefficients differ) and any applied axial tension ([n_shift_tension_axial]) are dimensionless index shifts δn, accumulated and converted to a propagation-constant shift by the single k₀ = 2π/λ_m factor applied here; the bend-stress shift on curved segments ([β_shift_stress_bend]) is already a 1/m shift and is added directly. Temperature is temperature(f, s), the bend curvature is curvature(f.path, s), and tension is tension(f, s); the bend and tension terms are skipped where the segment is straight or untensioned. Pure arithmetic, so it lifts through MCM Particles when its inputs are.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberPath.β_shift_common_mode"},{"category":"section","location":"api/material-properties/#material-properties","page":"MaterialProperties","text":"Intrinsic optical material properties — refractive index, thermo-optic behavior, CTE, and the spectral responses and derivatives used by downstream cross-section and DGD calculations. No path or fiber geometry lives here.","title":"MaterialProperties"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/material-properties/#Bifrost.MaterialProperties.MATERIAL_PROPERTY_API","page":"MaterialProperties","text":"MATERIAL_PROPERTY_API\n\nThe material property API as (function, arity) pairs, material argument included. Every concrete AbstractMaterial defines each method with untyped non-material arguments; a deliberately unmodeled property throws UnsupportedPropertyError. Package load fails with MaterialAPIError for any material missing a method, so adding an entry imposes the property on every material.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.MaterialProperties.MATERIAL_PROPERTY_API"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/material-properties/#Bifrost.MaterialProperties.SILICA_BRILLOUIN_GAIN_PEAK","page":"MaterialProperties","text":"SILICA_BRILLOUIN_GAIN_PEAK\n\nPeak Brillouin gain coefficient g_B of fused silica near SILICA_BRILLOUIN_LAMBDA_REF, in m/W. Source: Kobyakov, Sauer & Chowdhury, Adv. Opt. Photon. 2, 1 (2010), doi:10.1364/AOP.2.000001.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.MaterialProperties.SILICA_BRILLOUIN_GAIN_PEAK"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/material-properties/#Bifrost.MaterialProperties.SILICA_BRILLOUIN_LAMBDA_REF","page":"MaterialProperties","text":"SILICA_BRILLOUIN_LAMBDA_REF\n\nReference vacuum wavelength, in meters, for SILICA_BRILLOUIN_LINEWIDTH_HZ and SILICA_BRILLOUIN_GAIN_PEAK.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.MaterialProperties.SILICA_BRILLOUIN_LAMBDA_REF"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/material-properties/#Bifrost.MaterialProperties.SILICA_BRILLOUIN_LINEWIDTH_HZ","page":"MaterialProperties","text":"SILICA_BRILLOUIN_LINEWIDTH_HZ\n\nBrillouin gain spectrum FWHM linewidth Δν_B of fused silica near SILICA_BRILLOUIN_LAMBDA_REF, in Hz. Source: Kobyakov, Sauer & Chowdhury, Adv. Opt. Photon. 2, 1 (2010), doi:10.1364/AOP.2.000001.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.MaterialProperties.SILICA_BRILLOUIN_LINEWIDTH_HZ"},{"category":"type","location":"api/material-properties/#Bifrost.MaterialProperties.AbstractMaterial","page":"MaterialProperties","text":"Material properties for optical glasses.\n\nThis file defines base types and common algebraic methods that will be available to all specific materials defined in the material folder.\n\nUnits (SI unless noted):\n\nλ                     wavelength in m\nT_K                   temperature in K\nxge, xf             dopant molar fraction (dimensionless; range is per-material)\nrefractive indices, Poisson ratio, photoelastic constants: dimensionless\ncte                   1/K\nsoftening_temperature  K\nyoungs_modulus        Pa\nnonlinearrefractiveindex (n_2)  m²/W\n\nEvery concrete material defines each method in MATERIAL_PROPERTY_API, returning a value or throwing UnsupportedPropertyError; package load fails with MaterialAPIError for a material that does not.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.MaterialProperties.AbstractMaterial"},{"category":"type","location":"api/material-properties/#Bifrost.MaterialProperties.MaterialAPIError","page":"MaterialProperties","text":"MaterialAPIError(material, missing_methods)\n\nThrown at package load when the concrete material type material has no method for the MATERIAL_PROPERTY_API functions named in missing_methods.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.MaterialProperties.MaterialAPIError"},{"category":"method","location":"api/material-properties/#Bifrost.MaterialProperties._refractive_index-Tuple{GeO2, Any, Any}","page":"MaterialProperties","text":"_refractive_index(material::GeO2, λ, T_K)\nrefractive_index(material::GeO2, λ, T_K)\nrefractive_index_dω(material::GeO2, λ, T_K)\n\nPure germania refractive index.\n\nλ is wavelength in m. T_K is temperature in K. refractive_index returns a dimensionless refractive index. refractive_index_dω returns d(n)/dω in s.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.MaterialProperties._refractive_index"},{"category":"method","location":"api/material-properties/#Bifrost.MaterialProperties._refractive_index-Tuple{SiO2, Any, Any}","page":"MaterialProperties","text":"_refractive_index(material::SiO2, λ, T_K)\nrefractive_index(material::SiO2, λ, T_K)\nrefractive_index_dω(material::SiO2, λ, T_K)\n\nPure silica refractive index.\n\nλ is wavelength in m. T_K is temperature in K. refractive_index returns a dimensionless refractive index. refractive_index_dω returns d(n)/dω in s.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.MaterialProperties._refractive_index"},{"category":"method","location":"api/material-properties/#Bifrost.MaterialProperties._refractive_index-Tuple{SilicaFluorinatedGlass, Any, Any}","page":"MaterialProperties","text":"_refractive_index(material::SilicaFluorinatedGlass, λ, T_K)\nrefractive_index(material::SilicaFluorinatedGlass, λ, T_K)\nrefractive_index_dω(material::SilicaFluorinatedGlass, λ, T_K)\n\nFluorinated silica refractive index.\n\nλ is wavelength in m. T_K is temperature in K. refractive_index returns a dimensionless refractive index. refractive_index_dω returns d(n)/dω in s.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.MaterialProperties._refractive_index"},{"category":"method","location":"api/material-properties/#Bifrost.MaterialProperties._refractive_index-Tuple{SilicaGermaniaGlass, Any, Any}","page":"MaterialProperties","text":"_refractive_index(glass::SilicaGermaniaGlass, λ, T_K)\nrefractive_index(glass::SilicaGermaniaGlass, λ, T_K)\nrefractive_index_dω(glass::SilicaGermaniaGlass, λ, T_K)\n\nSilica-germania refractive index.\n\nλ is wavelength in m. T_K is temperature in K. refractive_index returns a dimensionless refractive index. refractive_index_dω returns d(n)/dω in s.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.MaterialProperties._refractive_index"},{"category":"method","location":"api/material-properties/#Bifrost.MaterialProperties.brillouin_gain_peak-Tuple{SilicaGermaniaGlass, Any, Any}","page":"MaterialProperties","text":"brillouin_gain_peak(glass::SilicaGermaniaGlass, λ, T_K) -> m/W\n\nPeak Brillouin gain coefficient, anchored at the pure-silica value SILICA_BRILLOUIN_GAIN_PEAK and scaled by the bulk-mixing doping corrections (p₁₂, ρ, v_A) and the textbook 1/λ² dependence. The opto-acoustic overlap and the mode-index factor are not modelled, so the absolute value is uncertain — pass a measured g_B where it matters. Ge doping shifts the Brillouin response weakly over the supported ≤5 mol% range (Deroh et al., Opt. Lett. 43, 4005 (2018), doi:10.1364/OL.43.004005).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.MaterialProperties.brillouin_gain_peak"},{"category":"method","location":"api/material-properties/#Bifrost.MaterialProperties.brillouin_linewidth-Tuple{SilicaGermaniaGlass, Any}","page":"MaterialProperties","text":"brillouin_linewidth(glass::SilicaGermaniaGlass, T_K) -> Hz\n\nBrillouin gain FWHM linewidth. Returns the fused-silica value SILICA_BRILLOUIN_LINEWIDTH_HZ across the supported ≤5 mol% GeO₂ range, where the doping dependence is within measurement scatter; it grows strongly only at heavy doping (≈28→98 MHz over 3.6→98 mol%, Deroh et al., Opt. Lett. 43, 4005 (2018), doi:10.1364/OL.43.004005), a range the material model rejects at construction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.MaterialProperties.brillouin_linewidth"},{"category":"function","location":"api/material-properties/#Bifrost.MaterialProperties.cte","page":"MaterialProperties","text":"cte(material, T_K)\n\nLinear coefficient of thermal expansion of material, in 1/K. T_K is temperature in K.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.MaterialProperties.cte"},{"category":"function","location":"api/material-properties/#Bifrost.MaterialProperties.density","page":"MaterialProperties","text":"density(material, T_K)\n\nMass density ρ of material, in kg/m³.\n\nOwned by concrete materials (subtypes of AbstractMaterial); not defined on AbstractMaterial itself. T_K is temperature in K — the current models treat density as temperature-independent over their validity range. Materials that do not model density throw UnsupportedPropertyError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.MaterialProperties.density"},{"category":"function","location":"api/material-properties/#Bifrost.MaterialProperties.nonlinear_refractive_index","page":"MaterialProperties","text":"nonlinear_refractive_index(material, λ, T_K)\n\nKerr nonlinear refractive index n₂ of material, in m²/W. λ is wavelength in m. T_K is temperature in K.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.MaterialProperties.nonlinear_refractive_index"},{"category":"function","location":"api/material-properties/#Bifrost.MaterialProperties.photoelastic_constants","page":"MaterialProperties","text":"photoelastic_constants(material, T_K)\n\nDimensionless Pockels photoelastic constants (p₁₁, p₁₂) of material. T_K is temperature in K.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.MaterialProperties.photoelastic_constants"},{"category":"function","location":"api/material-properties/#Bifrost.MaterialProperties.poisson_ratio","page":"MaterialProperties","text":"poisson_ratio(material, T_K)\n\nDimensionless Poisson ratio of material. T_K is temperature in K.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.MaterialProperties.poisson_ratio"},{"category":"function","location":"api/material-properties/#Bifrost.MaterialProperties.refractive_index","page":"MaterialProperties","text":"_refractive_index(material, λ, T_K)\nrefractive_index(material, λ, T_K)\nrefractive_index_dω(material, λ, T_K)\n\nMaterial refractive index.\n\nThese methods are owned by concrete materials (subtypes of AbstractMaterial); they are not defined on AbstractMaterial itself. λ is wavelength in m. T_K is temperature in K. refractive_index returns a dimensionless refractive index. refractive_index_dω returns d(n)/dω in s.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.MaterialProperties.refractive_index"},{"category":"function","location":"api/material-properties/#Bifrost.MaterialProperties.runtime_range","page":"MaterialProperties","text":"runtime_range(material) -> NamedTuple\n\nRuntime validity window of material: a NamedTuple of ValidRanges keyed by quantity, e.g. runtime_range(SiO2()).λ.lo. A material without restrictions declares NamedTuple() explicitly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.MaterialProperties.runtime_range"},{"category":"function","location":"api/material-properties/#Bifrost.MaterialProperties.softening_temperature","page":"MaterialProperties","text":"softening_temperature(material, T_K)\n\nGlass softening temperature of material, in K. T_K is temperature in K.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.MaterialProperties.softening_temperature"},{"category":"method","location":"api/material-properties/#Bifrost.MaterialProperties.stiffness_longitudinal-Tuple{AbstractMaterial, Any}","page":"MaterialProperties","text":"stiffness_longitudinal(material, T_K)\n\nLongitudinal elastic stiffness C₁₁ = ρ·v_A² of material, in Pa.\n\nDerived generically for every AbstractMaterial from density (ρ) and velocity_acoustic (v_A); a material that models neither input propagates its UnsupportedPropertyError. T_K is temperature in K.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.MaterialProperties.stiffness_longitudinal"},{"category":"function","location":"api/material-properties/#Bifrost.MaterialProperties.velocity_acoustic","page":"MaterialProperties","text":"velocity_acoustic(material, T_K)\n\nLongitudinal acoustic velocity v_A of material, in m/s.\n\nOwned by concrete materials; not defined on AbstractMaterial itself. T_K is temperature in K, treated as temperature-independent over the current validity range. Materials that do not model it throw UnsupportedPropertyError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.MaterialProperties.velocity_acoustic"},{"category":"function","location":"api/material-properties/#Bifrost.MaterialProperties.youngs_modulus","page":"MaterialProperties","text":"youngs_modulus(material, T_K)\n\nYoung's modulus of material, in Pa. T_K is temperature in K.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.MaterialProperties.youngs_modulus"},{"category":"section","location":"generators/#Deriving-the-Generators","page":"Deriving the generators","text":"BIFROST tries to solve the differential equation\n\nfracpartial J(z)partial z = K(z) J(z)\n\nwhere J(z) is the Jones matrix for our system (which also depends on e.g. omega, the angular frequency of the light), z is the position coordinate along the fiber, and K(z) is a \"generator.\" The generator contains all of the fundamental physics in the problem, so this document shows how to derive it.\n\nOur formulation is very similar to that of Przhiyalkovsky et al., \"Polarization Dynamics of Light Propagating in Bent Spun Birefringent Fiber,\" Journal of Lightwave Technology 38(24), 6879-6885 (2020), DOI: 10.1109/jlt.2020.3017795.","title":"Deriving the Generators"},{"category":"section","location":"generators/#Formulating-the-Equation","page":"Deriving the generators","text":"Suppose our fiber is laid along the z-axis. Propagation of monochromatic light in the fiber is dictated by the wave equation\n\nfracpartial^2vecEpartial z^2 = -k_0^2 epsilon vecE\n\nHere k_0 = omegac and epsilon is the permittivity tensor. This is what contains all of the interesting physics; we'll come back to it in a moment.\n\nWe approximate the waves as transverse, so vecE = (E_x E_y) (i.e. only two components). Time dependence is e^-iomega t (the convention stated in Bifrost.PolarizationMath), so a forward wave advances as e^+ikz. There is a common phase gained by both polarization modes of the form e^+i k_0 barn z where barn is an average refractive index. Let's transform this away by defining E_xy = mathcalE_xy e^+ik_0 barnz. If the anisotropy is low (epsilon_i epsilon_c ll barepsilon), then this acts like a slowly-varying envelope approximation and, upon substitution into our wave equation, we can ignore the second-order derivatives of mathcalE_xy, which turns our wave equation into a Schrödinger-like equation:\n\n2ibarn fracpartial mathcalEpartial z = -k_0(epsilon - barn^2)mathcalE\n\nSo the \"generator\" we're interested in is\n\nK(z) = +fraci2 frack_0barn (epsilon - barn^2)","title":"Formulating the Equation"},{"category":"section","location":"generators/#The-Permittivity-Tensor","page":"Deriving the generators","text":"The permittivity tensor has contributions from every birefringence mechanism available. It explicitly describes how the two modes gain phase, so we can use our physical intuition to write it down. We must pick a basis for doing so: BIFROST uses the parallel-transported (Bishop) pair (e_1 e_2) — zero twist about the tangent, continuous along the whole path — which is the gauge in which free propagation contributes no frame term at all (see The transverse frame and the birefringence gauge). On a straight fiber, where the distinction is invisible, we can write as a starting point\n\nepsilon = barepsilon + iepsilon_c left( beginarraycc 0  -1  1  0 endarray right) + epsilon_i left( beginarraycc cos 2xi z  sin 2xi z  sin 2xi z  -cos 2xi z endarray right)\n\nHere barepsilon = barn^2 is the average permittivity, epsilon_c is the circular birefringence, and epsilon_i is the intrinsic linear birefringence, which is rotating with the spin of the fiber at spin rate xi. Writing xi = 2piL_s with a single spin pitch L_s is the constant-rate special case used here for clarity. In general the spin rate is an arbitrary function of arc length, xi = xi(z), and the implementation treats it that way: the rate of a spin run, authored with spin!(pb; rate), is nothing, a constant, or a Function of run-local material arc length, and the integrator (_integrate_rate) takes the analytic branch for a constant rate and adaptive Gauss–Kronrod quadrature for a function rate. Replace 2xi z below with 2int_0^z xi(z)dz for the function-valued case.\n\nThe remaining freedom in the basis is a single constant rotation — the anchor e_1(0), fixed by a static lab-frame convention (see the frame page, Section 6). We mostly don't care about this overall rotation because we send in light of all polarizations and the observables (DGD in particular) are gauge-invariant.\n\nWith bending, the linear birefringence direction of the bend is the projection of the curvature vector onto (e_1 e_2): theta_b = operatornameatan2(vec kcdot e_2 vec kcdot e_1), while the intrinsic (ellipticity/stress) axes sit at their frozen angle plus the accumulated spin and twist phases. No geometric quantity ever rotates the basis itself — the bookkeeping that the Frenet–Serret basis would require (and that historically produced the issue #88/#89 family of bugs) does not arise.\n\nIf there were no spin, then we would say \n\nepsilon = barepsilon + iepsilon_c left( beginarraycc 0  -1  1  0 endarray right) + epsilon_i left( beginarraycc 1  0  0  -1 endarray right)\n\nThe gyrotropic term enters K as\n\nK_c = frack_0 epsilon_c2barn left( beginarraycc 0  1  -1  0 endarray right)\n\nwhich is circular_birefringence_generator(Δβc) with Deltabeta_c = k_0epsilon_cbarn. Its eigenstate 1 i, left-circular in this convention, has the higher index when epsilon_c  0, consistent with Deltabeta_c = beta_LC - beta_RC.","title":"The Permittivity Tensor"},{"category":"section","location":"generators/#Assembling-the-Fiber-Generator","page":"Deriving the generators","text":"The implementation assembles the generator from independently-modeled mechanisms. From here on we write the propagation coordinate as the arc length s along the centerline (the same role z plays above).\n\nThe original BIFROST-style sliced approach calculates\n\nJ_mathrmtotal=prod_i J_i\n\nwith matrix order matching the order light encounters along the fiber. That approach is simple, but it is difficult to attach a meaningful error bound when linear birefringence, spinning, and other non-commuting terms vary along the path.\n\nThe Julia implementation instead assembles a local generator as a sum over mechanisms (all axes expressed in the transported frame):\n\nK(somega)=K_mathrmbend+K_mathrmtwist+K_mathrmellipticity\n+K_mathrmtension\n\nThe bending contribution comes from path curvature κ(s), which is also how the cross-section layer's bending birefringence response is parameterized (bend_curvature_per_m): in the simplest stress model the magnitude scales like κ(s)^2, and its axis is the curvature direction projected onto the frame (curvature_in_bishop_frame). Since the curvature vector is transverse to the tangent, its two in-frame components satisfy kx^2 + ky^2 = κ^2, so the κ^2 in the magnitude cancels the one normalizing that axis and the generator is formed directly from (kx, ky) — polynomial, and exactly zero where the path is straight. Tension shares the bend axis but scales like κ(s)·F, one power of curvature, so it keeps an explicit magnitude. The intrinsic contribution's axes co-rotate with the material: spin_phase(path, s) + twist_phase(path, s). Twist additionally contributes photoelastic circular birefringence. Geometric torsion appears in none of these — see the frame page.\n\nEach retardance is signed, not a bare magnitude: positive linear Δβ puts the slow axis at the generator angle, and bend/tension come out negative for silica. The sign conventions are stated once, in Bifrost.FiberPath.\n\nThe same decomposition exists for the frequency derivative:\n\nK_omega(somega)\n=K_mathrmbendomega+K_mathrmtwistomega\n+K_mathrmellipticityomega+K_mathrmtensionomega\n\nThat keeps ordinary Jones propagation and DGD sensitivity propagation aligned: both use the same Fiber, wavelength, breakpoint partition, and adaptive integration strategy.","title":"Assembling the Fiber Generator"},{"category":"section","location":"generators/#Numerical-Propagation","page":"Deriving the generators","text":"The step is a fourth-order commutator-free exponential (exp_cf4_step):\n\nJ_n+1=expleft(h(alpha_1K_1+alpha_2K_2)right)\n        expleft(h(alpha_2K_1+alpha_1K_2)right)J_n\nqquad K_i=K(s_n+c_ih)\n\nwith Gauss–Legendre nodes c_12=tfrac12mptfracsqrt36 and weights alpha_12=tfrac14mptfracsqrt36. It is useful here because the solution of a constant-coefficient matrix ODE is exactly an exponential: for constant K the two factors are exp(hK2) each and the step is exact. Step by step, the method preserves the multiplicative structure of Jones propagation. Under the lossless assumption, after removing common phase, the Jones matrices live in SU(2).\n\nThe adaptive controller uses step doubling:\n\ntake one full step of size h,\ntake two half steps of size h/2,\ncompare the two results using phase_insensitive_error,\naccept or reject the step,\nupdate h with a fifth-root controller because the estimate scales as O(h^5).\n\nPath breakpoints come from the built path:\n\nfiber_breakpoints(fiber) = breakpoints(fiber.path)\n\nThose breakpoints are the path's segment boundaries; a spin run starts at one, so it adds none of its own. propagate_fiber integrates each smooth interval with propagate_interval! in the fiber's material frame: with theta the accumulated spin and twist angle and R(theta) the transverse rotation, J=R(theta)tilde J gives dtilde Jds=bigl(R^mathsf TKR-thetaGbigr)tilde J with G = [0 -1; 1 0]. The core ellipticity, whose axis turns with theta, is constant there on a straight, so the step does not resolve its oscillation (src/fiber/material-frame.jl).\n\nOn a helix with a constant spin rate only the bend and tension terms vary in that frame: their axis turns at Omega=tau_g-theta, with tau_g the geometric torsion, so tilde K repeats with period P=piOmega. An interval holding two periods or more is integrated over one period, that propagator (with its omega sensitivity) is raised to the power of the whole periods, and the remainder is integrated (src/fiber/fiber-propagate.jl). An ensemble takes this shortcut only where every lane shares the period, as lanes strained alike by :T_K do.\n\nThe 2x2 Jones exponential uses a closed form based on Cayley-Hamilton. The implementation also factors out small numerical trace drift before applying the traceless formula:\n\nexp(A)=exp(operatornametr(A)2)\nleftcosh(mu)I+operatornamesinhc(mu)tilde Aright\nqquad mu^2=-det(tilde A)\n\nHere sinhc(mu) = sinh(mu) / mu, with a Taylor branch near zero. The full derivation is given in the appendix.","title":"Numerical Propagation"},{"category":"section","location":"generators/#Uncertain-segment-lengths","page":"Deriving the generators","text":"When length-changing meta (:T_K, :tension) makes segment lengths MCM Particles, every lane builds its own geometry and the ensemble solve maps each lane's segments onto one shared grid. See The solve workflow, Stages 1–3.","title":"Uncertain segment lengths"},{"category":"section","location":"generators/#DGD-Sensitivity-Propagation","page":"Deriving the generators","text":"The finite-difference DGD estimate used by the legacy implementation has the form\n\npartial_omega J\napprox fracJ(omega+Deltaomega)-J(omega)Deltaomega\n\nThe Julia propagator instead integrates the sensitivity matrix G = partial_omega J directly:\n\nfracdJds=KJqquad J(s_0)=I\n\nfracdGds=K_omega J+KGqquad G(s_0)=0\n\nAt the output, the PMD generator is\n\nH_mathrmPMD=-iJ^-1G\n\noutput_dgd(J, G) returns the eigenvalue spread of that generator. For 2x2 MCM-valued matrices, output_dgd_2x2(J, G) computes the same spread using a closed-form Hermitian 2x2 formula, avoiding LinearAlgebra.eigvals.\n\nThe coupled sensitivity step is implemented using a closed-form Frechet derivative of the 2x2 exponential:\n\nexpleft(hbeginbmatrixK  K_omega0  Kendbmatrixright)\n=\nbeginbmatrixE  F0  Eendbmatrix\n\nThis is implemented by exp_block_upper_triangular_2x2. Avoiding generic 4x4 LinearAlgebra.exp is important for MCM compatibility and is also faster for ordinary Float64 cases.","title":"DGD Sensitivity Propagation"},{"category":"section","location":"generators/#cayley-hamilton","page":"Deriving the generators","text":"For any 2x2 matrix A, Cayley-Hamilton gives\n\nA^2-operatornametr(A)A+det(A)I=0\n\nIf A is traceless, then\n\nA^2=-det(A)I\n\nDefine mu^2 = -det(A). Then\n\nA^2=mu^2 Iqquad A^3=mu^2 Aqquad A^4=mu^4 Ildots\n\nThe exponential series splits into even and odd powers:\n\ne^A\n=sum_k=0^inftyfracA^2k(2k)\n +sum_k=0^inftyfracA^2k+1(2k+1)\n\nUsing A^(2k) = mu^(2k) I and A^(2k+1) = mu^(2k) A:\n\ne^A\n=\nleft(sum_k=0^inftyfracmu^2k(2k)right)I\n+\nleft(sum_k=0^inftyfracmu^2k(2k+1)right)A\n\nTherefore\n\ne^A=cosh(mu)I+fracsinh(mu)muA\nqquad mu^2=-det(A)\n\nAt mu = 0, interpret sinh(mu) / mu -> 1, so e^A = I + A.","title":"Appendix: Cayley-Hamilton 2x2 Exponential"},{"category":"section","location":"frame-and-gauge/#The-transverse-frame-and-the-birefringence-gauge","page":"Frame and gauge","text":"The frame convention in which BIFROST expresses polarization is the parallel-transported (Bishop) frame. It fixes the meaning of every angle that enters the generators, explains why geometric torsion appears nowhere in the optics, and states the anchor convention and why the frame is continuous along the whole path. It is the companion to the implementation in src/geometry/path-geometry.jl (frame transport) and src/fiber/fiber-path.jl (generator assembly).","title":"The transverse frame and the birefringence gauge"},{"category":"section","location":"frame-and-gauge/#1.-The-propagation-equation-and-what-\"gauge\"-means-here","page":"Frame and gauge","text":"BIFROST integrates the Jones equation along the fiber arc length s:\n\nfracdJ(s)ds = K(s)J(s) qquad J(0) = mathbb1\n\nwith K a traceless anti-Hermitian-times-i generator (lossless propagation; see Deriving the generators). The two components of the Jones vector are the field amplitudes on some pair of transverse unit vectors (e_1(s) e_2(s)) with e_2 = hatTtimes e_1. That pair is a gauge choice: nothing physical depends on it, but every axis angle inside K — the bend axis, the ellipse axis — is measured in it, so the equations are only as trustworthy as the frame convention is consistent. The solver (src/path-integral.jl) never sees the frame; the gauge lives entirely in how fiber-path.jl builds K(s) from geometry queries.","title":"1. The propagation equation and what \"gauge\" means here"},{"category":"section","location":"frame-and-gauge/#2.-Free-propagation-parallel-transports-the-field","page":"Frame and gauge","text":"For a transparent, locally isotropic medium guiding a transverse wave along a gently curved path (radii large compared to the wavelength and the cladding, which is the regime BIFROST is restricted to), Maxwell's equations force the electric field to stay transverse while exchanging direction with the tangent only at the minimal rate required by E perp hatT:\n\nfracdvecEds = -left(veckcdotvecEright)hatT\nqquad veck equiv fracdhatTds\n\nwhere veck(s) is the curvature vector (magnitude kappa, direction toward the local center of curvature). The field never rotates about the tangent. This is Rytov's law, observed in fiber as the Berry-phase rotation of polarization along helical paths (Tomita and Chiao, doi:10.1103/PhysRevLett.57.937; Berry, doi:10.1038/326277a0). The glass itself obeys the same rule: an unspun, untwisted cross-section is carried along the path without rotating about the tangent, so its frozen-in anisotropy axes are parallel-transported too (Ulrich and Simon, doi:10.1364/AO.18.002241).","title":"2. Free propagation parallel-transports the field"},{"category":"section","location":"frame-and-gauge/#3.-The-Bishop-(relatively-parallel)-frame","page":"Frame and gauge","text":"Define the transverse pair by the same transport law (Bishop, doi:10.2307/2319846):\n\nfracde_1ds = -left(veckcdot e_1right)hatT\nqquad e_2 = hatTtimes e_1 \n\nProperties that matter here:\n\nExistence and smoothness everywhere. The transport needs only hat T(s); it is defined on straights, through inflections, and across joints where the curvature direction jumps. (The Frenet–Serret normal hat N = vec kkappa needs kappa neq 0 and flips at inflections.)\nZero twist: langle de_1ds e_2rangle = 0 by construction — the defining property, enforced as a T-PHYSICS test in test/test_bishop_frame.jl.\nGauge freedom is one constant angle. If (e_1 e_2) is relatively parallel, so is the pair rotated by any constant angle about hat T. The frame is unique once e_1(0) is chosen (Section 6).\nRelation to Frenet–Serret. Where the FS frame exists, e_1 = hat Ncostheta - hat Bsintheta with theta(s) = theta(0) + int_0^s tau_mathrmgeomds: the FS pair rotates about the tangent at the geometric-torsion rate relative to parallel transport. This identity is the closed-form helix transport in _parallel_transport_local(::HelixSegment, …).","title":"3. The Bishop (relatively-parallel) frame"},{"category":"section","location":"frame-and-gauge/#4.-Why-the-generator-contains-no-geometric-torsion-term","page":"Frame and gauge","text":"Expand the field on the transported pair, vec E = a_1 e_1 + a_2 e_2. Substituting the transport law for both vec E (Section 2) and (e_1 e_2) (Section 3) cancels the tangential exchange terms identically, leaving\n\nfracddsbeginpmatrixa_1 a_2endpmatrix = K_mathrmaniso(s)\nbeginpmatrixa_1 a_2endpmatrix\n\nwhere K_mathrmaniso contains only material anisotropy. Free propagation — straight, bent, or helical — contributes nothing. Had we expanded on the FS pair instead, the relative rotation of Section 3 would inject a connection term tau_mathrmgeom(isigma_2) that must then be carried, spliced, and (as issues #88/#62/#24 showed) mis-carried across joints, inflections, and near-straight regions. In the transported gauge tau_mathrmgeom exits the optics entirely; it survives only as the shape diagnostic geometric_torsion/total_torsion, with no optical role.","title":"4. Why the generator contains no geometric-torsion term"},{"category":"section","location":"frame-and-gauge/#5.-The-birefringence-sources-in-this-gauge","page":"Frame and gauge","text":"With the gauge fixed, optical generators come only from material anisotropy. A scalar phase such as spin_phase or twist_phase may rotate the axes used by a linear-retarder generator, but it is not itself a birefringence generator. The two are kept in separate tables below to make the taxonomy explicit.","title":"5. The birefringence sources in this gauge"},{"category":"section","location":"frame-and-gauge/#Sign-convention","page":"Frame and gauge","text":"The generators' forms and signs are stated once, in the FiberPath module docstring:\n\nGenerators (birefringence mechanisms):\n\nMechanism Generator type Axis used in generator Signed magnitude Code\nBend stress from curvature linear retarder theta_b = operatornameatan2(vec kcdot e_2 vec kcdot e_1) Deltabeta_b propto kappa^2; 0 (out-of-plane axis slow) bend_generator_K\nAxial tension on a bend linear retarder theta_b (same bend eigen-axis) propto Fkappa; 0, reinforces the bend tension_generator_K\nCore ellipticity + asymmetric frozen-in stress linear retarder theta_mathrmint(s) — see the phase table for how it moves from cross-section; 0 (major axis slow) ellipticity_generator_K\nMechanical twist photoelasticity circular none (circular about hat T) Deltabeta_c = n^2tfracp_11-p_122tau_m (photoelastic only); sign opposite to tau_m since g0 photoelastic_response_to_twist_generator_K\nGeometric torsion none — — no optical generator\n\nOrientation phases and rates (rotate axes; generate nothing):\n\nPhase / rate What it does Physical origin Code\nphi_mathrmspin(s) = int_0^s xids advects the intrinsic axes: theta_mathrmint(s) = theta_mathrmint(0) + phi_mathrmspin + phi_mathrmtwist frozen-in rotation imparted during draw; no stress spin_phase\nphi_mathrmtwist(s) = int_0^s tau_mds advects the intrinsic axes (same formula) elastic twist of the laid fiber twist_phase\ntau_mathrmgeom(s) nothing optical — shape diagnostic only centerline geometry geometric_torsion\n\nspin_phase is not a birefringence source: it is an authored material rotation phase that advects the intrinsic axes through ellipticity_generator_K's axis argument. Mechanical twist appears twice for different reasons — the accumulated phi_mathrmtwist advects those same axes (the geometric rotation), while the local rate tau_m separately creates the photoelastic circular birefringence through photoelastic_response_to_twist_generator_K. The geometric rotation is carried only by the advected axes, never duplicated in the circular term.\n\nWith Deltabeta_c = beta_LC - beta_RC as defined there, a right-handed twist gives Deltabeta_c  0 — l-rotatory activity whose polarization rotation follows the twist, the sign measured and verified by Ulrich and Simon (doi:10.1364/AO.18.002241, g = -n^2 p_44 simeq +016).\n\nThe differential Jones (N) matrix of a twisted fiber is sometimes written as three additive terms — linear birefringence at 0, linear birefringence at 45, and circular birefringence (Tentori and Garcia-Weidner, doi:10.1364/OE.21.031725, Eq. 6). That is the same physics in a fixed frame: the two linear terms are the (cos 2varphi sin 2varphi) decomposition of a single linear/elliptical retarder whose axis rotates with twist, which here is carried by phi_mathrmtwist advecting _intrinsic_axis_in_bishop_frame, while the circular term is photoelastic_response_to_twist_generator_K. So the rotating-axis retarder plus circular generator already represents that three-term structure.\n\nImplementation notes:\n\nvec kcdot e_12\nare curvature_in_bishop_frame (fiber-path.jl): the projection of curvature_vector(path, s) onto bishop_e1/bishop_e2. The double-angle form (cos 2theta_b sin 2theta_b) = ((k_x^2-k_y^2)k^2 2k_xk_yk^2) is normalization- and branch-free (_bend_axis_in_bishop_frame).\nBend-stress magnitudes follow Ulrich, Rashleigh, and Eickhoff (doi:10.1364/OL.5.000273); the spun-bent formulation parallels Przhiyalkovsky et al. (doi:10.1109/JLT.2020.3017795).\nA bend also produces a polarization-common index shift (β_shift_stress_bend). The conformally transformed index profile of a bent fiber, n(x)(1 + xR_mathrmeff) with effective bend radius R_mathrmeff = R(1+k) and k = -(n^22)p_12-nu(p_11+p_12) (Kiiveri et al., doi:10.1117/1.OE.61.12.126106), displaces the mode toward the higher-index outer side and raises both polarizations equally. Being isotropic it stays out of the traceless generator K and enters only the common-mode phase, leaving the differential phase and DGD untouched.\nBoth material phases are integrals of authored rates and are continuous along the whole path by construction, across segment joints and spin-run boundaries alike.","title":"Sign convention"},{"category":"section","location":"frame-and-gauge/#Two-axes,-one-frame","page":"Frame and gauge","text":"Two helpers in fiber-path.jl deliver a linear-retarder axis in the Bishop frame, and they are not two routes to the same number — they orient two physically distinct birefringence sources that merely share the frame.\n\n_bend_axis_in_bishop_frame orients the bend/tension axis. It is purely geometric: curvature_in_bishop_frame projects vec k = dhat Tds onto (e_1 e_2), and the double-angle pair follows from (k_x k_y) without ever forming the angle itself (next subsection). It is blind to spin and twist, and its direction is undefined where kappa = 0.\n_intrinsic_axis_in_bishop_frame orients the ellipticity axis. It rides on top of the frame: a frozen ellipse angle measured from e_1 plus the accumulated material rotation phi_mathrmspin + phi_mathrmtwist. It is always defined and is independent of the path's curvature direction.\n\nBoth return the same (cos 2φ, sin 2φ) double-angle pair consumed by linear_birefringence_generator; the source they belong to lives in the name, not the type.\n\n _bend_axis_in_bishop_frame _intrinsic_axis_in_bishop_frame\nPhysical origin path shape (curvature direction) material ellipse + spin/twist\nFrame entry project vec k onto (e_1 e_2) constant offset + phi_mathrmspin+phi_mathrmtwist\nDepends on spin/twist no yes\nDefined when kappa = 0 direction no; product yes yes\nConsumers bend_generator_K, tension_generator_K ellipticity_generator_K\n\nThe single shared anchor is e_1(s): theta = 0 means \"aligned with e_1\" for both. ","title":"Two axes, one frame"},{"category":"section","location":"frame-and-gauge/#The-bend-axis:-the-double-angle,-and-the-\\kappa2-that-cancels","page":"Frame and gauge","text":"Why the bend axis is delivered as a ratio of quadratics rather than an angle, and why the bend term needs no kappa neq 0 guard while the tension term does (issue #315).\n\nThe curvature vector is transverse, exactly. Since hat T is a unit vector, hat Tcdothat T = 1, so 2hat Tcdot dhat Tds = 0 and vec k perp hat T identically — not to leading order. So vec k lies wholly in the span of (e_1 e_2) and its projection loses nothing:\n\nk_x = vec kcdot e_1 quad k_y = vec kcdot e_2\nqquad k_x^2 + k_y^2 = lVertvec krVert^2 = kappa^2 \n\nWriting vec k = kappa(cosvarphi e_1 + sinvarphi e_2), the pair is just polar coordinates in the transverse plane: (k_x k_y) = kappa(cosvarphi sinvarphi). That pair is what _bend_axis_vector_at returns (and what curvature_in_bishop_frame returns on the per-s side). Rotating the frame by a constant delta about hat T sends varphi to varphi - delta.\n\nWhy a double angle. A retarder eigen-axis is a line, not an arrow: varphi and varphi + pi are the same physical state, so any coordinate for it must be pi-periodic — which (cosvarphi sinvarphi) is not. Equivalently, K_ell is a traceless symmetric rank-2 object, so a frame rotation by varphi rotates its components by 2varphi; on the Poincaré sphere linear birefringence sits on the S_1–S_2 equator and a real-space rotation by varphi is a rotation by 2varphi about S_3. The factor of two is the spin-2 nature of the retarder, not a convention. One visible consequence: vec k to -vec k leaves the pair below unchanged, since both entries are even in (k_x k_y) — pinned as a T-PHYSICS test in test/test_fiber_path_generators.jl.\n\nThe pair without trigonometry. Applying the double-angle identities to (k_x k_y) = kappa(cosvarphi sinvarphi),\n\ncos 2varphi = frack_x^2 - k_y^2k_x^2 + k_y^2\nqquad\nsin 2varphi = frac2 k_x k_yk_x^2 + k_y^2\n\nwhich is _bend_axis_at (and _bend_axis_in_bishop_frame). Preferred over operatornameatan2 plus cossin on three counts: no transcendentals; no branch cut, so it is smooth wherever kappa neq 0; and conditional-free, which MCM requires — atan2 branches on argument signs and errors on Particles.\n\nWhat happens at kappa = 0. Both expressions become 00. That is honest rather than a numerical wart: a straight segment has no bend plane, so the direction of curvature genuinely does not exist. Note that the frame is perfectly well defined there (Section 3) — only the angle measured in it is not.\n\nWhat does have a limit is the product with the magnitude, and for bending the two singular factors are the same one. Bend stress gives Deltabeta_b = Ckappa^2 (Section 5), so\n\nDeltabeta_bcos 2varphi = Ckappa^2frack_x^2-k_y^2kappa^2 = C(k_x^2 - k_y^2)\nqquad\nDeltabeta_bsin 2varphi = C2k_xk_y \n\nPolynomial in (k_x k_y): no division, finite everywhere, and exactly zero on a straight segment. The kappa^2 that makes the magnitude vanish is the same kappa^2 that makes the direction diverge; the physics was never singular, only the intermediate factorization into magnitude times direction. This is why the generator consumes _bend_axis_vector_at and the bend term carries no kappa neq 0 guard. The cancellation crosses a layer boundary — the magnitude is a cross-section quantity and the normalization a geometry one — so the cross-section layer exposes C on its own as _birefringence_bending_coefficient.\n\nAxial tension is the instructive contrast. It shares the bend eigen-axis but scales as Deltabeta_t propto kappa F — one power of curvature — so only half the kappa^2 cancels and a lvertkapparvert = sqrtk_x^2+k_y^2 survives. Its slope is unbounded at zero, and that kink is real: tension birefringence is linear in curvature magnitude. So the tension term keeps a kappa = 0 boundary guard, where the bend term needs none.","title":"The bend axis: the double angle, and the kappa^2 that cancels"},{"category":"section","location":"frame-and-gauge/#6.-The-anchor-e_1(0)-and-continuity","page":"Frame and gauge","text":"Parallel transport determines the frame up to one constant: e_1(0). BIFROST uses a static lab-frame rule (_initial_frame_from_tangent): take the world axis least aligned with the launch tangent and Gram–Schmidt it,\n\ne_1(0) = frachat a - (hat acdothat T)hat TlVert cdots rVert\nqquad hat a = operatorname*argmin_hat xhat yhat z hat Tcdot hat a\n\nFor the common hat T(0) = hat z launch this is simply e_1(0) = hat x. The rule is deliberately blind to curvature: it depends only on the launch direction, is reproducible by hand, never moves when downstream geometry is edited, and has no special cases.\n\nFrom the anchor, build places the whole path in one loop and advances e_1 through every segment — connectors included — by that segment's closed-form parallel transport (_parallel_transport_local), so each segment's entry e_1 is its predecessor's exit e_1. The frame is therefore continuous along the whole path by construction (a T-GUARDRAIL test in test/test_bishop_frame.jl, issue #89).","title":"6. The anchor e_1(0) and continuity"},{"category":"module","location":"frame-and-gauge/#Bifrost.FiberPath","page":"Frame and gauge","text":"FiberPath\n\nFiber assembly: binds an authored path to a cross section and assembles the local Jones generators K(s) and Kω(s) of dJ/ds = K(s) J.\n\nSign conventions\n\nThis is the single statement of the fiber generators' sign conventions. The time sign and the circular handedness they rest on are those of Bifrost.PolarizationMath: e^{−iωt}, so propagation multiplies an amplitude by e^{+iβs}; handedness judged looking toward the source.\n\nJones basis. The first component lies along the transported Bishop leg e1(s), the second along e2(s). An axis angle φ is measured from e1 toward e2.\nLinear birefringence is signed, Δβ ≡ β(axis at φ) − β(axis at φ + π/2), so Δβ > 0 makes the axis at φ the slow one. Its generator (linear_birefringence_generator) is\nK_ℓ = (iΔβ/2) [cos 2φ   sin 2φ\n               sin 2φ  −cos 2φ]\nand advances the axis at φ with phase +Δβ/2 per unit length.\nCircular birefringence is signed, Δβc ≡ β_LC − β_RC. Its generator (circular_birefringence_generator) is the real rotation generator\nK_c = (Δβc/2) [ 0  1\n               −1  0]\nwhose left-circular eigenstate [1, i] advances with phase +Δβc/2 per unit length. A linear state's azimuth (from e1 toward e2) evolves as ψ(s) = ψ(0) − Δβc·s/2.\n\nEnforced by test/test_birefringence_signs.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberPath"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Examples","page":"Examples","text":"","title":"Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Julia","page":"Examples","text":"Start Julia from the repository root with julia --project=. and run the Quick start example; the blocks below continue from its xs, pb and fiber.","title":"Julia"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#DGD","page":"Examples","text":"J, G, stats = propagate_fiber_sensitivity(fiber; λ_m = 1550e-9, verbose = false)\nτ = output_dgd(J, G)    # seconds\n\nFor Monte Carlo Measurements (MCM) values, prefer output_dgd_2x2(J, G) over output_dgd(J, G) because it avoids eigvals. See MCM compatibility for the surrounding conventions.","title":"DGD"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Uncertain-models-and-sweeps","page":"Examples","text":"A FiberModel binds the same authored path without building it, and propagate_fiber on a model returns every observable eagerly:\n\nmodel   = FiberModel(pb; cross_section = xs, T_ref_K = 297.15)\nresults = propagate_fiber(model; λ_m = [1540e-9, 1550e-9, 1560e-9], verbose = false)\nresults.dgd\n\nAny authored value may be a MonteCarloMeasurements.Particles ensemble — for example MetaAdd(:T_K, Particles(200, Normal(-20.0, 2.0))) on the connector — and the observables in results are then Particles-valued, every lane exactly its own scalar solve; see The solve workflow.","title":"Uncertain models and sweeps"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Python","page":"Examples","text":"The wrapper.py shim boots juliacall against this project and returns a tab-completable view of any Julia module. Names listed in dir(Bf) are exactly what that module exports — none of the ~1000 names a juliacall module otherwise inherits from Base. Julia names ending in ! use the _b suffix.\n\nimport math\nimport wrapper\n\nBf = wrapper.wrap(\"Bifrost\")\n\npb = Bf.PathBuilder()\nBf.straight_b(pb, length=0.1)\nBf.bend_b(pb, radius=0.05, angle=math.pi / 2)\nfiber = Bf.Fiber(pb, cross_section=Bf.SMF28())\n\nJ, stats = Bf.propagate_fiber(fiber, λ_m=1550e-9, verbose=False)\n\nFor a fuller juliacall example, see docs/juliacall-demo.py and its Julia-side module docs/juliacall-demo.jl. To run python code in the uv environment use uv run python docs/juliacall-demo.py.","title":"Python"},{"category":"section","location":"api/path-integral/#path-integral","page":"PathIntegral","text":"Adaptive propagation of the Jones equation dJ/ds = K(s)J and the coupled sensitivity system for G = ∂ωJ, with breakpoint-aware interval decomposition, phase-insensitive error control, and MCM-friendly closed-form matrix exponentials and DGD extraction. The Theory section derives the underlying generators.","title":"PathIntegral"},{"category":"type","location":"api/path-integral/#Bifrost.PathIntegral.DGDSweep","page":"PathIntegral","text":"DGDSweep\n\nWraps a FiberPropagationResults for plotting its differential group delay vs the sweep's driving parameter. Build with dgd; throws a MissingSensitivityError for results propagated with sensitivity = false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathIntegral.DGDSweep"},{"category":"type","location":"api/path-integral/#Bifrost.PathIntegral.FiberPropagationResults","page":"PathIntegral","text":"FiberPropagationResults\n\nEagerly stored observables of a fiber propagation run, the return type of propagate_fiber(model::FiberModel; ...) and of the wavelength-sweep form propagate_fiber(fiber; λ_m::AbstractVector).\n\nPer deterministic case i (case value driving_values[i], one per queried wavelength):\n\nJ[i], J_dw[i]: output Jones matrix and its angular-frequency sensitivity.\ndgd[i]: differential group delay in seconds (output_dgd_2x2). J_dw and dgd are nothing for a run with sensitivity = false, and dgd on such results throws a MissingSensitivityError.\nΦ[i]: common-mode phase in radians (common_mode_phase).\noutput_state[i]: output polarization as a StokesVector, from J[i] applied to input_state.\nstats[i]: per-interval PropagatorStats (for an ensemble run, one such vector per particle).\n\nThe ensemble descriptor n_particles records the aligned ensemble size, or nothing for a scalar run. A deterministic model runs one scalar solve; an uncertain model runs per-lane scalar geometry plus one ensemble solve (docs/src/solve-workflow.md).\n\nFor an ensemble run every observable entry is Particles-valued with joint particle alignment preserved across all observables and cases. The deterministic case axis and the ensemble axis are never merged: a wavelength sweep of an uncertain model yields length(driving_values) cases, each carrying the full ensemble.\n\nfiber holds one built fiber at the run's nominal geometry, so results carry the resolved geometry (path, frames, breakpoints, cross section, T_ref_K) without retaining per-particle paths: the propagated fiber itself for a scalar run or wavelength sweep, and the built ensemble-mean realization (every Particles model value collapsed to its mean) for an ensemble run.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathIntegral.FiberPropagationResults"},{"category":"type","location":"api/path-integral/#Bifrost.PathIntegral.FiberSweep","page":"PathIntegral","text":"FiberSweep\n\nCommon supertype of the typed plotting wrappers around a FiberPropagationResults: DGDSweep, PhaseSweep, OutputStateSweep, and JonesSweep (built by dgd, phase, output, and PolarizationMath.jones respectively). Each is plain data — constructing or inspecting one requires no plotting package; only rendering it with plot needs RecipesBase/Plots loaded alongside Bifrost.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathIntegral.FiberSweep"},{"category":"type","location":"api/path-integral/#Bifrost.PathIntegral.JonesSweep","page":"PathIntegral","text":"JonesSweep\n\nWraps a FiberPropagationResults for plotting its output Jones matrices. Build with PolarizationMath.jones(results), extending the existing jones conversion generic rather than introducing a new name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathIntegral.JonesSweep"},{"category":"type","location":"api/path-integral/#Bifrost.PathIntegral.MissingSensitivityError","page":"PathIntegral","text":"MissingSensitivityError(observable)\n\nobservable (:dgd) was requested from a FiberPropagationResults propagated with sensitivity = false, which skips the ∂ω solve it needs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathIntegral.MissingSensitivityError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/path-integral/#Bifrost.PathIntegral.OutputStateSweep","page":"PathIntegral","text":"OutputStateSweep\n\nWraps a FiberPropagationResults for plotting its output-polarization observables (degree of polarization, azimuth, ellipticity, Poincaré-sphere trajectory, polarization ellipse) vs the sweep's driving parameter. Build with output.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathIntegral.OutputStateSweep"},{"category":"type","location":"api/path-integral/#Bifrost.PathIntegral.ParticleSolveError","page":"PathIntegral","text":"ParticleSolveError(particle_index, cause)\n\nRealizing one lane of an uncertain model failed — canonically a jumpto! connector that cannot satisfy min_bend_radius, or a strained connector target length shorter than its chord. particle_index is the aligned particle index and cause the underlying scalar exception: a BuildSiteError naming the segment when building one segment failed. Lane feasibility is not checked in advance: lanes build concurrently, and the lowest-index infeasible lane is reported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathIntegral.ParticleSolveError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/path-integral/#Bifrost.PathIntegral.PhaseSweep","page":"PathIntegral","text":"PhaseSweep\n\nWraps a FiberPropagationResults for plotting its common-mode phase vs the sweep's driving parameter. Build with phase.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathIntegral.PhaseSweep"},{"category":"type","location":"api/path-integral/#Bifrost.PathIntegral.PropagatorStats","page":"PathIntegral","text":"PropagatorStats(accepted_steps, rejected_steps)\n\nRecord adaptive step counts for one smooth propagation interval.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathIntegral.PropagatorStats"},{"category":"type","location":"api/path-integral/#Bifrost.PathIntegral.SolverParams","page":"PathIntegral","text":"SolverParams(; rtol, atol, h_init, h_max, h_min, safety, growth_max, shrink_min)\n\nAdaptive step-doubling controls for the path-integral.jl propagators.\n\nPass a SolverParams value through the params keyword to set the tolerances, initial and maximum step sizes, minimum-step guard, and step-size update limits used by interval, piecewise, and fiber propagation.\n\nh_init and h_max default to nothing, meaning \"derive from the interval\": the initial step becomes (s1 - s0) / 100 and the maximum step s1 - s0. Pass a Float64 to override either one.\n\nAll fields are Float64 solver controls and never carry uncertainty, so this struct sits outside the MCM contract; MonteCarloMeasurements.Particles live only on the physical inputs.\n\nDefault values:\n\nField Default Meaning\nrtol 1e-9 relative tolerance\natol 1e-12 absolute tolerance\nh_init nothing initial step (nothing → (s1-s0)/100)\nh_max nothing maximum step (nothing → s1-s0)\nh_min 1e-12 minimum step (underflow guard)\nsafety 0.9 step-update safety factor\ngrowth_max 2.0 maximum step growth on accept\nshrink_min 0.2 minimum step shrink on reject\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathIntegral.SolverParams"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-integral/#Bifrost.PathIntegral._fiber_state_trace-Tuple{Fiber, Real, AbstractVector{<:Real}}","page":"PathIntegral","text":"_fiber_state_trace(f, λ_m, s_samples; input_state = Canonical.H(),\n                   params = SolverParams()) -> Vector{<:StokesVector}\n\nOutput polarization state S(s) = stokes(J(s) * input_state) at each s in sorted s_samples ⊆ [f.s_start, f.s_end], where J(s) is the end-to-end Jones matrix from f.s_start to s.\n\nDrives the same local generator (generator_K) and primitive step (propagate_interval!) as propagate_fiber, merging s_samples into the fiber's own breakpoint grid (fiber_breakpoints) so the adaptive step controller only ever integrates the smooth sub-intervals delimited by breakpoints — the same invariant propagate_piecewise itself respects, just with the intermediate J recorded at the extra points instead of discarded. Backs the Poincaré-sphere trajectory in inspect(fiber; ...); it is not part of the propagate_fiber result surface.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathIntegral._fiber_state_trace"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-integral/#Bifrost.PathIntegral._propagate_fiber_frame-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"PathIntegral","text":"_propagate_fiber_frame(K, frame, breaks; J0, verbose, params,\n                       period = (a, b) -> nothing) -> (J, stats)\n\nIntegrate dJ/du = K(u)·J across the intervals of breaks in the frame whose angle at an interval's ends frame(a, b) returns, turning J into the frame at each start and out of it at each end. K is the generator in that frame, and period(a, b) its period on the interval, or nothing. Returns the final J (a Matrix for a matrix J0) and one PropagatorStats per interval.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathIntegral._propagate_fiber_frame"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-integral/#Bifrost.PathIntegral._propagate_fiber_frame_sensitivity-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"PathIntegral","text":"_propagate_fiber_frame_sensitivity(K, Kω, frame, breaks; J0, verbose, params,\n                                   period = (a, b) -> nothing) -> (J, G, stats)\n\n_propagate_fiber_frame for the coupled Jones and sensitivity system: G starts at zero and turns with J, the frame being independent of ω.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathIntegral._propagate_fiber_frame_sensitivity"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-integral/#Bifrost.PathIntegral.align_global_phase-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix}","page":"PathIntegral","text":"align_global_phase(A, B)\n\nReturn the unit complex phase that best aligns B to A in Frobenius inner product.\n\nMinimizing ‖A - ϕB‖_F over unit ϕ gives ϕ = tr(B' * A) / abs(tr(B' * A)); note the argument order in the trace, B' * A rather than A' * B.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathIntegral.align_global_phase"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-integral/#Bifrost.PathIntegral.exp_block_upper_triangular_2x2-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix}","page":"PathIntegral","text":"exp_block_upper_triangular_2x2(hM, hMω)\n\nReturn the closed-form exponential blocks for a coupled sensitivity step.\n\nThis computes the diagonal block E and off-diagonal block F of the block-upper-triangular 4×4 matrix\n\nA = [ M   Mω ]\n    [ 0   M  ]\n\nscaled by h.  The result is\n\nexp(h·A) = [ E   F ]\n           [ 0   E ]\n\nwhere E = exp(hM) and F = h · φ(hM, Mω) with φ the Frechet derivative of exp along Mω:\n\nφ(A, B) = ∫₀¹ exp((1-τ)A) · B · exp(τA) dτ\n\nFor a 2×2 M = c·I + M̃ with M̃ traceless and μ² = -det(M̃):\n\nexp(A) = exp(c) · [cosh(μ) I + sinhc(μ) M̃]\n\nThe Frechet derivative admits a closed form in the 2×2 case (see Al-Mohy & Higham, \"Computing the Fréchet derivative of the matrix exponential\", 2009, §5).  For A = c·I + Ã with Ã traceless and eigenvalues ±μ, one can write\n\nφ(A, B) = α₀·B + α₁·(ÃB + BÃ) + α₂·ÃBÃ\n\nwith scalar coefficients that depend only on c and μ.\n\nWe derive the coefficients directly: because the trace part commutes, it factors as exp(c) on both E and F, leaving a traceless problem.  For a traceless 2×2 Ã with eigenvalues ±μ, the identities Ã² = μ² I and the representation exp(Ã) = cosh(μ) I + sinhc(μ) Ã let us evaluate the integral in closed form using the basis {B, ÃB + BÃ, ÃBÃ}:\n\nexp(Ã) − I = (cosh(μ) − 1) I + sinhc(μ) Ã\nφ(Ã, B)   = β₀ B + β₁ (ÃB + BÃ) + β₂ ÃBÃ\n\nDirect computation (expanding exp((1-τ)Ã) = cosh((1-τ)μ)I + sinhc((1-τ)μ)(1-τ)Ã and the mirror factor, then integrating over τ ∈ [0,1]) gives:\n\nβ₀ = sinhc(μ)                                    (coefficient of B)\nβ₁ = (cosh(μ) − sinhc(μ)) / (2 μ²)                (coefficient of ÃB + BÃ)\nβ₂ = (sinhc(μ) − cosh(μ) + μ² sinhc_int(μ)) / μ⁴  (coefficient of ÃBÃ)\n\nwhere sinhc_int(μ) = ∫₀¹ τ·sinhc((1-τ)μ)·sinhc(τμ) dτ.  Rather than carry that integral in closed form, we use the alternative decomposition based on Ã² = μ² I: any polynomial in Ã on a 2×2 matrix reduces to αI + βÃ, so the full φ(Ã, B) collapses to terms in {B, ÃB, BÃ, ÃBÃ} whose scalar weights integrate to elementary functions of μ.\n\nBecause this derivation is fiddly, the MCM use case motivates it (LinearAlgebra's generic exp relies on pivoting and bounds-style operations that do not lift through Particles).  Instead of carrying the Frechet closed form symbolically, we fall back to a numerically exact construction: compute E = exp(hM) via exp_jones_generator, then compute F via Van Loan's identity\n\nexp([ hM   hMω ]) = [ exp(hM)   F       ]\n    [ 0    hM  ]   [ 0         exp(hM) ]\n\nand extract F by forming the 4×4 exponential through the 2×2 closed form applied to hM and hM' plus an explicit integral representation.\n\nImplementation: use Higham's scaling & squaring of the 2×2 closed form, combined with the identity that for A block-upper-triangular with equal diagonal blocks,\n\nexp(s·A)^2 = [ exp(2sM)    2·exp(sM)·F_s ]\n             [ 0           exp(2sM)      ]\n\nwhere F_s is the off-diagonal of exp(s·A).  We use s = 1 directly:\n\nF = ∑_{k=1}^∞ (hM)^(k-1)/k! · hMω · something...\n\nwhich is equivalent to the series\n\nF = h · ∑_{k=0}^∞ (hM)^k · hMω · (hM)^j ... /(j+k+1)! ...\n\nThis is what Al-Mohy & Higham eq. (10.15) in Higham's \"Functions of Matrices\" computes via scaling & squaring.  For 2×2 blocks the series closes in finite form because of M̃² = μ² I for the traceless part.  Rather than re-derive, we compute F by the Kronecker-product identity:\n\nvec(F) = (I ⊗ I) · L_A(hMω) · vec(I)\n       ≈ h·sinhc(hM/2)  applied bilinearly\n\nConcretely: factor M = c I + M̃ with M̃ traceless and μ² = −det(M̃). Then exp(hM) = exp(hc) · [cosh(μh)·I + sinhc(μh)·M̃·h] (note: we fold h into the coefficient so μh is dimensionless).  The Frechet derivative along Mω of the scalar-function part is then elementary, and we get:\n\nF = h · exp(hc) · (\n        φ₀(hc, μh) · Mω\n      + φ₁(hc, μh) · (M̃·Mω + Mω·M̃) · h\n      + φ₂(hc, μh) · M̃·Mω·M̃ · h²\n    )\n\nwith\n\nφ₀(a, b) = sinhc(b)\nφ₁(a, b) = (cosh(b) - sinhc(b)) / (2 b²)\nφ₂(a, b) = (b·sinh(b) - 3·cosh(b) + 3·sinhc(b)) / (2 b⁴)\n\nevaluated at a = hc (trace part absorbed into exp(hc) prefactor) and b = μh.  Small-b branches use Taylor expansions for numerical stability.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathIntegral.exp_block_upper_triangular_2x2"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-integral/#Bifrost.PathIntegral.exp_cf4_step-Tuple{Any, Float64, Float64, Any}","page":"PathIntegral","text":"exp_cf4_step(K, s, h, J)\n\nAdvance J one fourth-order commutator-free exponential step over [s, s + h]:\n\nJ ← exp(h·(α₁K₁ + α₂K₂)) · exp(h·(α₂K₁ + α₁K₂)) · J,\n\nwith Kᵢ = K(s + cᵢh) at the Gauss–Legendre nodes c = 1/2 ∓ √3/6 and weights α = 1/4 ∓ √3/6: a two-exponential commutator-free Magnus method of fourth order (test/testsolverconvergence.jl measures the order). Each factor is the closed-form exp_jones_generator, so the step stays unitary and lifts through MCM Particles; for a constant K both factors are exp(hK/2) and the step is exact.\n\nK must return a 2×2 local Jones generator. The return value is a static 2×2; for lanes (_Lanes) of K and J it is the step taken lane by lane.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathIntegral.exp_cf4_step"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-integral/#Bifrost.PathIntegral.exp_jones_generator-Tuple{AbstractMatrix}","page":"PathIntegral","text":"exp_jones_generator(A)\n\nReturn the closed-form exponential of a 2×2 Jones generator.\n\nFor an exactly traceless matrix A, Cayley-Hamilton gives A^2 = -det(A) I, so\n\nexp(A) = cosh(μ) I + sinh(μ)/μ * A, where μ^2 = -det(A).\n\nThis implementation also removes any tiny numerical trace drift by factoring out exp(tr(A)/2) and applying the closed form to the centered traceless part.\n\nAccepts any 2×2 AbstractMatrix with a Complex eltype and returns a static 2×2 (SMatrix); lifts elementwise through MCM Particles entries.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathIntegral.exp_jones_generator"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-integral/#Bifrost.PathIntegral.exp_sensitivity_cf4_step-Tuple{Any, Any, Float64, Float64, Any, Any}","page":"PathIntegral","text":"exp_sensitivity_cf4_step(K, Kω, s, h, J, G)\n\nAdvance (J, G) one fourth-order commutator-free step over [s, s + h]: the step of exp_cf4_step applied to the coupled system d/ds [G; J] = A·[G; J] with the 4×4 block-upper-triangular generator\n\nA = [ K   Kω ]\n    [ 0   K  ]\n\neach factor exponentiated in closed form by exp_block_upper_triangular_2x2. Lanes (_Lanes) step lane by lane. The motivation for avoiding LinearAlgebra.exp on the 4×4 matrix is MCM compatibility: the generic Padé approximation relies on operations (typemax, isfinite on matrix norms, pivoting) that do not lift through Particles.  The closed form is also faster and more accurate than the generic Padé even for pure Float64 inputs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathIntegral.exp_sensitivity_cf4_step"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-integral/#Bifrost.PathIntegral.output_dgd-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix}","page":"PathIntegral","text":"output_dgd(J, G; hermitianize = true)\n\nReturn differential group delay as the real eigenvalue spread of the PMD generator.\n\nH = -i·J⁻¹·G.\n\nNote: under MCM, eigvals on a Matrix{<:Particles} hits LinearAlgebra routines that do not lift elementwise (typemax, pivoting).  For uncertain propagations, compute the mean DGD per-sample via bymap at the call site, or extract the Hermitian eigenvalues by hand for the 2×2 case (closed form: eigenvalues of a 2×2 Hermitian matrix are (tr/2) ± √((tr/2)² − det)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathIntegral.output_dgd"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-integral/#Bifrost.PathIntegral.output_dgd_2x2-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix}","page":"PathIntegral","text":"output_dgd_2x2(J, G; hermitianize = true)\n\nReturn closed-form DGD for a 2×2 PMD generator.\n\nThis is equivalent to output_dgd for the 2×2 case but avoids eigvals, so it lifts through MCM Particles.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathIntegral.output_dgd_2x2"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-integral/#Bifrost.PathIntegral.phase_insensitive_error-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix}","page":"PathIntegral","text":"phase_insensitive_error(A, B)\n\nReturn the Frobenius error between two matrices after removing global phase.\n\nThe best common phase is chosen by align_global_phase.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathIntegral.phase_insensitive_error"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-integral/#Bifrost.PathIntegral.pmd_generator-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix}","page":"PathIntegral","text":"pmd_generator(J, G; hermitianize = true)\n\nCompute the PMD generator H = -im * (J \\ G).\n\nSet hermitianize = true to replace H by (H + H') / 2, which suppresses small numerical anti-Hermitian drift before DGD extraction.\n\nFor the 2×2 case the J \\ G solve uses a comparison-free closed form (_solve_2x2), so the generator lifts through MCM Particles; larger matrices fall back to the generic \\ (Float64 paths only).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathIntegral.pmd_generator"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-integral/#Bifrost.PathIntegral.propagate_fiber-Tuple{FiberModel}","page":"PathIntegral","text":"propagate_fiber(model::FiberModel; λ_m, input_state = Canonical.H(),\n                sensitivity = true, verbose = true, params = SolverParams())\n    -> FiberPropagationResults\n\nExecute a fiber model. λ_m is the operating wavelength in metres: a scalar (one deterministic case) or an AbstractVector (a deterministic wavelength sweep — the case axis).\n\nsensitivity = true integrates G = ∂ωJ alongside J, for J_dw and DGD. sensitivity = false integrates J alone, for the PTF without the ∂ω solve, and leaves J_dw and dgd as nothing. J sets the step size in both modes (G, in seconds, is far smaller), so J is the same either way.\n\nA fully deterministic model runs a single ordinary scalar solve (docs/src/solve-workflow.md). An uncertain model realizes every aligned particle index as its own scalar fiber (scalarize → Fiber: its own trajectory, connector solutions, frames, and phases), then integrates the optics once, for all particles, through a single Particles-valued solve over the stacked per-lane generators on the nominal reference grid. Every observable is exact per particle to solver tolerance, and every entry comes back Particles-valued with joint particle alignment preserved.\n\nA failure while realizing one lane's geometry is rethrown as a ParticleSolveError carrying the particle index; lane feasibility is not checked in advance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathIntegral.propagate_fiber"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-integral/#Bifrost.PathIntegral.propagate_fiber-Tuple{Fiber}","page":"PathIntegral","text":"propagate_fiber(fiber; λ_m, verbose = true, params = SolverParams())\npropagate_fiber(fiber; λ_m::AbstractVector, input_state = Canonical.H(),\n                sensitivity = true, ...)\n\nPropagate a path-backed Fiber at operating wavelength λ_m.\n\nWith a scalar λ_m the return is (J_final, stats), one PropagatorStats per interval of fiber_breakpoints(fiber). The fiber is integrated in its material frame, which turns with the spin and mechanical twist (src/fiber/material-frame.jl): there the generator is generator_K(fiber, λ_m) with its linear-birefringence axes turned back by the accumulated angle, plus the frame's own circular term, so the ellipticity of a spun straight is constant and its oscillation costs no steps; on a helix with a constant spin rate it repeats with a period, and an interval holding several periods is integrated over one and raised to their number. J_final is the lab-frame (transported-frame) Jones matrix.\n\nWith a vector λ_m the fiber is swept over the given wavelengths (a deterministic case axis) and the return is a FiberPropagationResults holding every observable (Jones matrix, sensitivity, DGD, common-mode phase, and the output polarization state of input_state) eagerly. Each sample runs the coupled sensitivity solve, or with sensitivity = false the Jones solve alone, leaving J_dw and dgd as nothing. input_state and sensitivity participate only in sweep mode; a scalar-λ_m call ignores them and solves J alone. Julia keywords do not dispatch, so both modes share this method and branch on the runtime type of λ_m.\n\nλ_m is always deterministic; a Particles wavelength is rejected (uncertainty belongs on FiberModel, and first-order wavelength sensitivity is carried by G = ∂ωJ).\n\nLumped jump matrices are a primitive-solver concept: the fiber layer derives its breakpoint grid internally, so there is no coherent key a caller could supply here. Callers who need lumped elements drive propagate_piecewise directly with an explicit grid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathIntegral.propagate_fiber"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-integral/#Bifrost.PathIntegral.propagate_fiber_sensitivity-Tuple{Fiber}","page":"PathIntegral","text":"propagate_fiber_sensitivity(fiber; λ_m, verbose = true,\n                            params = SolverParams())\n\nPropagate a path-backed Fiber and its angular-frequency sensitivity.\n\nThe generators are generator_K(fiber, λ_m) and generator_Kω(fiber, λ_m), integrated in the material frame as propagate_fiber integrates K, over the intervals of fiber_breakpoints(fiber); the return is (J_final, G_final, stats), both in the lab (transported) frame. As with propagate_fiber, lumped jump matrices live on the primitive solver, not on this surface.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathIntegral.propagate_fiber_sensitivity"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-integral/#Bifrost.PathIntegral.propagate_interval!-Tuple{Any, Float64, Float64, Any}","page":"PathIntegral","text":"propagate_interval!(K, s0, s1, J0; params = SolverParams())\n\nPropagate dJ/ds = K(s) * J over one smooth interval.\n\nThe method is fourth-order commutator-free exponential step doubling (exp_cf4_step) with phase_insensitive_error. Step controls and tolerances are carried by params::SolverParams. It returns (J_final, stats), where stats is a PropagatorStats for the interval; J0 is left unchanged. J0 is a 2×2 matrix (J_final is then a Matrix), or lanes (_Lanes) of static 2×2s advanced through one shared step sequence, for a K whose value is lanes too.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathIntegral.propagate_interval!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-integral/#Bifrost.PathIntegral.propagate_interval_sensitivity!-Tuple{Any, Any, Float64, Float64, Any}","page":"PathIntegral","text":"propagate_interval_sensitivity!(K, Kω, s0, s1, J0; G0, params = SolverParams())\n\nPropagate the coupled Jones and angular-frequency sensitivity system over one interval.\n\nThe equations are dJ/ds = K(s) * J and dG/ds = Kω(s) * J + K(s) * G. Step controls and tolerances are carried by params::SolverParams. The function returns (J_final, G_final, stats) and leaves both initial values unchanged; they are 2×2 matrices or lanes, as for propagate_interval!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathIntegral.propagate_interval_sensitivity!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-integral/#Bifrost.PathIntegral.propagate_piecewise-Tuple{Any, AbstractVector{<:Real}}","page":"PathIntegral","text":"propagate_piecewise(K, breaks; jumps = Dict(), J0 = I, verbose = true,\n                    params = SolverParams())\n\nPropagate dJ/ds = K(s) * J across breakpoint-delimited smooth intervals.\n\nbreaks must be sorted and defines intervals [breaks[i], breaks[i + 1]]. jumps may contain lumped Jones matrices applied immediately after reaching a breakpoint. params::SolverParams is forwarded to propagate_interval!.\n\nReturn (J_final, stats_per_interval).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathIntegral.propagate_piecewise"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-integral/#Bifrost.PathIntegral.propagate_piecewise_sensitivity-Tuple{Any, Any, AbstractVector{<:Real}}","page":"PathIntegral","text":"propagate_piecewise_sensitivity(K, Kω, breaks; jumps = Dict(),\n                                jump_omegas = Dict(), J0 = I, G0 = 0,\n                                verbose = true, params = SolverParams())\n\nPropagate the coupled Jones and angular-frequency sensitivity system across intervals.\n\nThe equations are dJ/ds = K(s) * J and dG/ds = Kω(s) * J + K(s) * G. jumps are lumped Jones matrices applied after breakpoints, and jump_omegas are their angular-frequency derivatives. params::SolverParams is forwarded to propagate_interval_sensitivity!.\n\nReturn (J_final, G_final, stats_per_interval).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathIntegral.propagate_piecewise_sensitivity"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-integral/#Bifrost.PathIntegral.sensitivity_phase_insensitive_error-NTuple{4, AbstractMatrix}","page":"PathIntegral","text":"sensitivity_phase_insensitive_error(J_ref, G_ref, J_cmp, G_cmp)\n\nReturn one scalar error for coupled Jones and sensitivity matrices.\n\nThe comparison removes the global phase determined by J_ref and J_cmp, then returns the maximum of the reduced Frobenius errors for J and G.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathIntegral.sensitivity_phase_insensitive_error"},{"category":"method","location":"api/path-integral/#Bifrost.PathIntegral.sinhc-Tuple{Any}","page":"PathIntegral","text":"sinhc(μ)\n\nReturn sinh(μ) / μ using a Taylor expansion near zero.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.PathIntegral.sinhc"},{"category":"section","location":"#BIFROST","page":"Home","text":"BIFROST (Birefringence In Fiber: Research and Optical Simulation Toolkit) is a Julia package for simulating polarization mode dispersion in step-index, silica-based, germania-doped optical fibers.\n\nRather than representing a fiber as a pre-sliced list of Jones matrices, BIFROST builds a continuous centerline path, binds it to a transverse fiber cross section, and integrates a local Jones generator\n\nfracdJds=K(somega)Jqquad J(s_0)=I\n\nwith an adaptive, phase-insensitive, fourth-order commutator-free exponential (Lie-group) integrator that never steps across path breakpoints. A fiber is integrated in its material frame, which turns with the spin and twist.","title":"BIFROST"},{"category":"section","location":"#Architecture","page":"Home","text":"The package is one umbrella module, Bifrost, composed of layered submodules:\n\nModule Role\nMaterialProperties Intrinsic optical material properties and spectral responses.\nPathGeometry 3D path authoring, differential geometry, and twist resolution.\nFiberCS Step-index cross-section optics and local birefringence responses.\nFiberPath Binds a path to a cross section and assembles K(s) / Kω(s).\nPathIntegral Adaptive Jones and DGD-sensitivity propagation.\nPlots Opt-in visualization helpers (using Bifrost.Plots).\n\nThe Theory pages derive the generators and explain the integral geometric quantities. The API pages list the documented, exported symbols of each module.","title":"Architecture"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-start","page":"Home","text":"using Bifrost\n\nxs = SMF28(; ellipticity_axis_ratio = 1.02)    # slightly elliptical core\n\npb = PathBuilder()                              # starts at the origin, heading +z\nstraight!(pb; length = 0.5, meta = [Nickname(\"lead-in\")])\nspin!(pb; rate = 2π)                            # material spin (rad/m) from here on\nbend!(pb; radius = 0.05, angle = π / 2, axis_angle = π / 6)\njumpto!(pb; point = (0.3, 0.0, 1.0), incoming_tangent = (1.0, 0.0, 0.0),\n        min_bend_radius = 0.02, meta = [MetaAdd(:T_K, -20.0)])\nspin!(pb; rate = nothing)                       # end the spin run\nstraight!(pb; length = 0.2, twist = 3.0)\n\npath  = build(pb)                               # placed geometry, for queries\nfiber = Fiber(pb; cross_section = xs, T_ref_K = 297.15)\n\nJ, stats = propagate_fiber(fiber; λ_m = 1550e-9, verbose = false)\n\npb is the authored path: a lead-in; a bend and a jumpto! connector to a global target, both spun, the connector 20 K below T_ref_K; and a twisted lead-out. build(pb) places it into a Path for geometry queries (position(path, s), bishop_frame(path, s), …); Fiber builds it again, applying the connector's :T_K strain, and binds the cross section. See Usage for the authoring model, Examples for DGD, uncertain models and Python, and §1 of test/human/test_polarization.ipynb for a plotted walkthrough. Installation instructions live in the project README.md.","title":"Quick start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Regime-of-Operation","page":"Home","text":"This library models step-index silica-based germania-doped optical fibers. It includes chromatic dispersion effects. At this time, the library does not model other possible dopants, specially engineered materials such as dispersion-compensating fiber, or other index profiles such as graded-index fibers.\n\nAt present, BIFROST models birefringence from four mechanisms:\n\nCore noncircularity\nAsymmetric thermal stress due to differing coefficients of thermal expansion between core and cladding when the core is noncircular\nBending\nTwisting\n\nIt does not model birefringence due to:\n\nCladding noncircularity\nNon-concentric cladding and core\nExternal asymmetric stress, such as pushing on the fiber in one direction\nTransverse electric fields\nAxial magnetic fields\n\nBased on validation work, as well as the limits of the approximations made and the validity range of the data used in BIFROST, we believe the codebase correctly computes supported contributions to birefringence in the following regime:\n\nSingle-mode operation, V2405\nThe weakly guiding regime, n_textco-n_textcl ll 1, which implicitly requires weak germanium doping\nThe nearly circular-core regime, e^2 ll 1\nBend radii much larger than the cladding radius, R gg r_textcl\nTemperatures 200 K lesssim T lesssim 300 K, limited by the model for the thermo-optic coefficient dndT of bulk germania glass\nTelecom wavelengths 1 mum lesssim lambda lesssim 2 mum\n\nWe do not model the temperature dependence of the coefficients of thermal expansion or the photoelastic constants p_11 and p_12 in fused silica and germania, as the variation is small within the above parameter regime. Polarization-dependent loss and nonlinear scattering effects are also outside the current model.","title":"Regime of Operation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Known-limitations","page":"Home","text":"src/path-integral.jl assumes lossless Jones propagation. Do not introduce gain, loss, or polarization-dependent loss there without a separate design.\nThe Fiber constructor handles geometry and metadata perturbations, including thermal length scaling, by building the authored geometry once; twist/spin remapping under length-changing meta remains a known caveat area.\nThe Julia code has substantial internal tests, but it is not yet a validated replacement for the legacy Python model or for published fiber data.","title":"Known limitations"},{"category":"section","location":"api/bifrost/#bifrost","page":"Bifrost","text":"Top-level plotting-wrapper entry points (dgd, phase, output) and the sweep types they return, declared at the Bifrost module level ahead of the layers that implement them.","title":"Bifrost"},{"category":"type","location":"api/bifrost/#Bifrost._BifrostMetaRegistry","page":"Bifrost","text":"_BifrostMetaRegistry\n\nDedup table for per-segment meta (excluding Nickname, which is always shown inline) collected while a container (e.g. PathBuilder) itemizes many segments in its full display. Segments whose rendered meta text is identical share one symbolic key (meta1, meta2, ...) instead of repeating the same text once per segment; _bifrost_show_meta_registry prints the accumulated key => text lookup table once, at the end of the display.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost._BifrostMetaRegistry"},{"category":"type","location":"api/bifrost/#Bifrost._Lanes","page":"Bifrost","text":"Lane-major ensemble values for the solver.\n\nAn ensemble integration advances every lane — one scalar realization of an uncertain model — through one shared adaptive step sequence (docs/src/solve-workflow.md). While the solver runs, _Lanes holds the lanes' values side by side, one plain value per lane (a static 2×2 Jones matrix, or a Float64 error), so each lane's arithmetic is the scalar solver's own and an operation allocates one vector rather than one per matrix entry. The solver's kernels map over the lanes, and the step controller reduces across them with _mcm_pmax, as it reduces an MCM Particles value. The results layer turns the final lanes into Particles entries (_lanes_particles).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost._Lanes"},{"category":"method","location":"api/bifrost/#Bifrost._bifrost_describe_matrix-Tuple{AbstractMatrix}","page":"Bifrost","text":"_bifrost_describe_matrix(m) -> String\n\nOne-line description of a matrix field, e.g. \"6×3 Matrix{Float64}\", in place of dumping its entries.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost._bifrost_describe_matrix"},{"category":"method","location":"api/bifrost/#Bifrost._bifrost_describe_vector-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"Bifrost","text":"_bifrost_describe_vector(v) -> String\n\nOne-line description of a vector field too long to print element-by-element, e.g. \"256-element Vector{Float64}\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost._bifrost_describe_vector"},{"category":"method","location":"api/bifrost/#Bifrost._bifrost_list_compact-Tuple{IO, AbstractVector}","page":"Bifrost","text":"_bifrost_list_compact([io,] v; limit = 8) -> String\n\nRender up to limit elements of v via their own compact show, comma-separated; beyond limit, append \"… (+K more)\" instead of continuing to enumerate. Used for segments-style fields so a normal-sized build prints in full while a pathologically large one stays bounded.\n\nThe io form propagates any IOContext properties on io (e.g. a meta-dedup registry, see _BifrostMetaRegistry) into each element's own show call; the no-io form is a context-free convenience for use outside a Base.show method body.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost._bifrost_list_compact"},{"category":"method","location":"api/bifrost/#Bifrost._bifrost_meta_key!-Tuple{Bifrost._BifrostMetaRegistry, AbstractString}","page":"Bifrost","text":"_bifrost_meta_key!(reg, rendered) -> Symbol\n\nLook up (or assign, on first sight) the symbolic key for the already-rendered meta text rendered in reg.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost._bifrost_meta_key!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/bifrost/#Bifrost._bifrost_plural-Tuple{Integer, AbstractString}","page":"Bifrost","text":"_bifrost_plural(n, noun) -> String\n\n\"1 segment\" / \"3 segments\" — pluralize noun for a compact element count.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost._bifrost_plural"},{"category":"method","location":"api/bifrost/#Bifrost._bifrost_round-Tuple{Any}","page":"Bifrost","text":"_bifrost_round(x)\n\nEnsemble mean of a possibly-Particles value, rounded to 6 significant digits for display. Display-only: never affects stored precision.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost._bifrost_round"},{"category":"method","location":"api/bifrost/#Bifrost._bifrost_show_compact-Tuple{IO, AbstractString, Any}","page":"Bifrost","text":"_bifrost_show_compact(io, typename, parts)\n\nPrint typename(parts[1], parts[2], ...). parts are already-formatted strings or values with a reasonable print method.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost._bifrost_show_compact"},{"category":"method","location":"api/bifrost/#Bifrost._bifrost_show_full-Tuple{IO, AbstractString, Any}","page":"Bifrost","text":"_bifrost_show_full(io, typename, rows)\n\nPrint typename: followed by one label = value line per (label, value) pair in rows.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost._bifrost_show_full"},{"category":"method","location":"api/bifrost/#Bifrost._bifrost_show_meta_registry-Tuple{IO, Bifrost._BifrostMetaRegistry}","page":"Bifrost","text":"_bifrost_show_meta_registry(io, reg)\n\nPrint the accumulated key => text lookup table, if reg collected any entries; prints nothing when reg is empty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost._bifrost_show_meta_registry"},{"category":"method","location":"api/bifrost/#Bifrost._lanes_particles-Tuple{Bifrost._Lanes{<:StaticArraysCore.SMatrix{2, 2}}}","page":"Bifrost","text":"_lanes_particles(x::_Lanes{<:SMatrix{2,2}}) -> Matrix{Complex{Particles}}\n\nThe 2×2 matrix whose entry k carries lane j's entry k as particle j, in lane order, so joint particle alignment is the lane order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost._lanes_particles"},{"category":"method","location":"api/bifrost/#Bifrost._mcm_allfinite-Tuple{Union{MonteCarloMeasurements.Particles, MonteCarloMeasurements.StaticParticles}}","page":"Bifrost","text":"_mcm_allfinite(x) -> Bool\n\ntrue only when x is finite for every particle (simply isfinite for plain reals). Used by input validation so an uncertain quantity with any non-finite realization is rejected explicitly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost._mcm_allfinite"},{"category":"method","location":"api/bifrost/#Bifrost._mcm_is_particles-Tuple{Union{MonteCarloMeasurements.Particles, MonteCarloMeasurements.StaticParticles}}","page":"Bifrost","text":"_mcm_is_particles(x) -> Bool\n\ntrue for Particles/StaticParticles ensemble values, false otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost._mcm_is_particles"},{"category":"method","location":"api/bifrost/#Bifrost._mcm_iszero_all-Tuple{Union{MonteCarloMeasurements.Particles, MonteCarloMeasurements.StaticParticles}}","page":"Bifrost","text":"_mcm_iszero_all(x) -> Bool\n\ntrue only when x vanishes for every particle (simply iszero for plain reals). Birefringence term guards use this instead of == zero so a zero-mean but nonzero-variance input (e.g. a signed twist rate centered on zero) is never silently collapsed to a scalar zero; a term is skipped only when it truly vanishes across the whole ensemble.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost._mcm_iszero_all"},{"category":"method","location":"api/bifrost/#Bifrost._mcm_nparticles-Tuple{Union{MonteCarloMeasurements.Particles, MonteCarloMeasurements.StaticParticles}}","page":"Bifrost","text":"_mcm_nparticles(x) -> Int\n\nNumber of particles carried by an ensemble value. Companion of _mcm_is_particles; callers guard with that predicate first.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost._mcm_nparticles"},{"category":"method","location":"api/bifrost/#Bifrost._mcm_particle_sites!-Tuple{Vector{Pair{String, Int64}}, Any, String}","page":"Bifrost","text":"_mcm_particle_sites(x, label) -> Vector{Pair{String,Int}}\n_mcm_particle_sites!(sites, x, label)\n\nRecursively locate every Particles/StaticParticles value reachable inside x, returning label-path => particle-count pairs (e.g. \"segments[2].radius\" => 200).\n\nFunction values are opaque leaves: this structural walk cannot see uncertainty a function might be hiding. Two runtime checks catch that instead — _validate_model_rate_functions (an eager probe, run once at FiberModel construction) and _guarded_rate (a wrapper installed by _scalarized whenever it hits a Function during scalarize(model, i)). The eager probe catches the obvious case at authoring time; the guarded wrapper catches at solve time anything that slips past a single-point probe. The double layer is deliberate, specifically because functions are opaque to this walk.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost._mcm_particle_sites!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/bifrost/#Bifrost._mcm_pmax-Tuple{Union{MonteCarloMeasurements.Particles, MonteCarloMeasurements.StaticParticles}}","page":"Bifrost","text":"_mcm_pmax(x) -> Float64\n\nLargest particle of a possibly-Particles value, as a Float64 (plain reals are converted directly). Used for adaptive step control and uniform branch selection, where one conservative decision must bound the whole ensemble, and for hard upper bounds that must hold for every realization.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost._mcm_pmax"},{"category":"method","location":"api/bifrost/#Bifrost._mcm_pmean-Tuple{Union{MonteCarloMeasurements.Particles, MonteCarloMeasurements.StaticParticles}}","page":"Bifrost","text":"_mcm_pmean(x)\n\nEnsemble mean of a possibly-Particles value (the value itself for plain reals). Used where a single nominal value must represent the ensemble — geometric routing (segment lookup, breakpoint grid) and centered-domain summaries.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost._mcm_pmean"},{"category":"method","location":"api/bifrost/#Bifrost._mcm_pmin-Tuple{Union{MonteCarloMeasurements.Particles, MonteCarloMeasurements.StaticParticles}}","page":"Bifrost","text":"_mcm_pmin(x)\n\nSmallest particle of a possibly-Particles value (the value itself for plain reals). Used for hard lower bounds that must hold for every realization, e.g. a positive length or bend radius.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost._mcm_pmin"},{"category":"function","location":"api/bifrost/#Bifrost.dgd","page":"Bifrost","text":"dgd(results::FiberPropagationResults) -> DGDSweep\n\nWrap propagation results for plotting: differential group delay vs the sweep's driving parameter (plot(dgd(results))). Plain data — a FiberSweep holds only the wrapped results and carries no plotting dependency of its own.\n\nThese wrapper constructors are entry points for the PlotsRecipesExt package extension (load RecipesBase or Plots alongside Bifrost to render them). Declared here, at the Bifrost top level, ahead of PathIntegral (whose results.jl supplies the real methods via import ..Bifrost: dgd), so the presentation-facing surface (this function plus phase, output, and PolarizationMath.jones) reads as one group, independent of where each wrapper type's real implementation lives.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.dgd"},{"category":"function","location":"api/bifrost/#Bifrost.inspect","page":"Bifrost","text":"inspect(path; title = \"\", fidelity = 1.0, nsteps = 50) -> PlotlyBase.Plot\ninspect(fiber::Fiber; λ_m, input_state = Canonical.H(), title = \"\",\n        fidelity = 1.0, nsteps = 50) -> PlotlyBase.Plot\n\nInteractive 3D inspector. inspect(path) takes a built Path: an arc-length slider scrubs a cursor along the centerline, carrying a transported T̂/N̂/B̂ frame, a normal–binormal plane, and a spin-phase arrow, alongside a live readout of s, position, curvature, geometric torsion, spin rate, and accumulated spin phase.\n\ninspect(fiber) takes a path-backed Fiber plus λ_m and additionally shows a Poincaré-sphere inset tracking the output polarization state S(s) = stokes(J(s) * input_state) along the same slider, color-graded by arc length like the centerline.\n\nImplemented by the PathInspectorExt package extension (load PlotlyBase alongside Bifrost to render it). Unlike dgd/phase/ output, this bypasses the plot() recipe pipeline entirely: the slider and per-step readout are Plotly-native layout/frame constructs with no RecipesBase equivalent, so plot(path) (PlotsRecipesExt) stays the static, backend-agnostic centerline view, and inspect(path) is the Plotly-only interactive diagnostic.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.inspect"},{"category":"function","location":"api/bifrost/#Bifrost.output","page":"Bifrost","text":"output(results::FiberPropagationResults) -> OutputStateSweep\n\nWrap propagation results for plotting: output-polarization observables vs the sweep's driving parameter or trajectory — degree of polarization, azimuth, ellipticity, a Poincaré-sphere trajectory, or a polarization ellipse (plot(output(results)), plot(output(results), :poincare), plot(output(results), :ellipse)). See dgd.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.output"},{"category":"function","location":"api/bifrost/#Bifrost.phase","page":"Bifrost","text":"phase(results::FiberPropagationResults) -> PhaseSweep\n\nWrap propagation results for plotting: common-mode phase vs the sweep's driving parameter (plot(phase(results))). See dgd.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.phase"},{"category":"section","location":"solve-workflow/#solve-workflow","page":"The solve workflow","text":"How propagate_fiber turns an uncertain FiberModel into Particles-valued optical observables. One algorithm serves every model: geometry is realized per particle with ordinary scalar code, and the optics — the expensive part — is integrated once, for all particles at once, by a single sensitivity solve. There are no execution modes and no approximation knobs. A model carrying MonteCarloMeasurements.Particles anywhere follows exactly this path; a fully deterministic model is its M = 1 degenerate case.\n\nThis page describes the orchestration. The physics inside the generator — what the bend, twist, ellipticity, and tension terms are and why — is derived in Deriving the Generators; the transported frame the generator is written in is defined in The transverse frame and the birefringence gauge; the path-authoring and differential-geometry layer is described in Path geometry.","title":"The solve workflow"},{"category":"section","location":"solve-workflow/#The-problem","page":"The solve workflow","text":"An uncertain model carries Particles values — M index-aligned joint samples, called particles — anywhere geometry or response inputs are authored: segment fields (radius, angle, pitch, turns, a, length, axis_angle), field-level MetaAdd/MetaMul modifiers, foreign meta payloads (:T_K, :tension), mechanical twist and spin rates, start coordinates, jumpto! connector targets, and cross-section fields. Index alignment means lane j — the j-th sample of every Particles in the model — is one joint realization of the fiber.\n\nThe observables (Jones matrix J, its frequency sensitivity G = partial_omega J, DGD, common-mode phase) must be exact per particle: lane j of every output equals what an ordinary scalar solve of lane j's realized model produces, to solver tolerance. In particular a jumpto! connector — whose shape depends on the entire trajectory before it, which differs per particle — must be resolved from each particle's own trajectory, never from a shared or averaged one.\n\nTwo structural facts shape the solution:\n\nUncertainty never changes the authored topology. Every particle has the same segments, in the same order and the same spin runs. Particles disagree about lengths, curvatures, frames, phases, and connector shapes — never about what exists.\nGeometry is cheap; propagation is expensive. Realizing a path is frame chaining plus at most a few one-dimensional connector searches. The adaptive Jones + sensitivity integration dominates the run time.\n\nConsequence: pay the cheap part M times, so that every geometric quantity is exactly its own particle's, and pay the expensive part once, by integrating all M propagation problems in lockstep through one solver call.","title":"The problem"},{"category":"section","location":"solve-workflow/#Objects-and-notation","page":"The solve workflow","text":"Two indices run through this page, and they are independent:\n\nj = 1 ldots M\n— the particle (lane) index. M is particle_count of the model, the number of aligned samples every Particles in it carries.\nc = 1 ldots C\n— the segment index: row c of the per-segment table that build puts on every Path (columns seg, u0, L0, twist_entry, spin_run, spin_rate, spin_phi0, spin_material0, spin_dmds), one row per authored segment in authored order, zero-length segments included, never reordered or filtered. C is the same for every particle, because uncertainty never changes the authored topology — row c is the same authored segment in every lane, so the row index is the cross-lane alignment key.\n\nThree arc-length coordinates also appear. They are coordinates, not further indices — in particular q below is a position, not something that ranges over a set:\n\ns\n— physical arc length along a realized fiber, measured from its start. Every lane has its own s, since lanes differ in total length.\nu\n— the reference coordinate: arc length along the nominal build, u in 0 L_textref. Shared by all lanes, and the variable the solver actually advances along.\nq\n— segment-local physical arc length, measured from the entry of whichever segment is in play. Written q_j for lane j's, since a segment's physical length differs by lane.\n\nSymbol / object Meaning\nFiberModel The authored, unbuilt, uncertainty-carrying spec: a PathBuilder + cross section + T_ref_K.\nscalarize(model, j) The fully deterministic model at lane j: every Particles replaced by its j-th sample.\nNominal realization Every Particles replaced by its mean — one deterministic model defining the reference build.\nu_0c, L_0c Row c's u0 and L0 in the nominal build: segment c occupies u_0c u_0c + L_0c of the reference axis.\nA_cj Physical arc length of segment c as built for particle j (row c's L0 in lane j's build).\ntau_cj Reference-to-physical stretch of segment c for particle j: A_cjL_0c.\n\nDeterministic by contract, never Particles: the wavelength lambda (a deterministic case axis — sensitivity in omega is computed analytically through G), the reference temperature T_ref_K, solver tolerances (SolverParams), and function-valued rate profiles (a rate Function must return plain reals).","title":"Objects and notation"},{"category":"section","location":"solve-workflow/#Stage-1-—-realize-the-geometry,-per-particle","page":"The solve workflow","text":"For each j = 1 ldots M, build lane j's fiber with the ordinary scalar machinery:\n\nfiber_j = Fiber(scalarize(model, j))   # placement, connectors, strain — no ODE\n\nThis is a complete, independent scalar build: :T_K/:tension strain is applied once, here (including the target-length re-solve of a connector carrying its own strain), field-level meta is applied to segment fields, and one placement loop chains the construction frame, transports the Bishop frame, solves every jumpto! connector by its scalar search with min_bend_radius honored, and fills the table's spin columns. No differential equation is touched.\n\nBecause each particle's geometry is its own complete scalar build, everything downstream is per-particle correct by construction: placement frames, the parallel-transported Bishop frame (including its holonomy on non-planar paths), twist and spin phase offsets, arc lengths, and connector shapes. Nothing geometric is ever shared, averaged, or approximated across particles. The geometry layer itself stays strictly scalar — Particles never enter build (see MCM compatibility).\n\nFailure contract. Lane feasibility is not checked in advance. Any exception raised while realizing lane j — canonically a jumpto! connector that cannot satisfy min_bend_radius, or a strained connector target length shorter than its chord — is rethrown as a ParticleSolveError carrying the particle index j, and the calculation ends there. Lanes build concurrently, one task each, so when several lanes fail the lowest index is reported. When building one segment failed, its cause is a BuildSiteError naming that segment.\n\nOne extra build, of the nominal realization, provides the reference grid of Stage 2 and is stamped on the results for reporting and plotting.","title":"Stage 1 — realize the geometry, per particle"},{"category":"section","location":"solve-workflow/#Stage-2-—-one-grid-for-M-domains","page":"The solve workflow","text":"Each lane obeys its own ordinary propagation problem along its own physical arc length s:\n\nfracdJ_jds = K_j(s)J_j  qquad s in 0 L_j \n\nThe M problems live on M different domains: a thermally strained segment is 0.98 m long for one particle and 1.02 m for another, so total lengths L_j and every interior segment boundary sit at different s per lane. A single solver call needs one domain and one set of breakpoints.\n\nThe nominal build provides both. Segment c occupies u_0c u_0c + L_0c of the reference coordinate u, where L_0c is the nominal segment length, and the segments tile 0 L_textref end to end. Every particle shares this tiling, because every particle has the same C segments in the same order — lanes disagree only about how physically long each segment is. Three consequences:\n\nBreakpoints are shared and deterministic. Segment boundaries — the points the adaptive solver must never step across — sit at the same u for all lanes and stay plain Float64.\nRows align by index. Row c of lane j's table is that particle's realization of the same authored segment as row c of the nominal table.\nA zero-length segment keeps its row, so the indices stay aligned, but its interval is empty: it adds no breakpoint and routing never lands on it.","title":"Stage 2 — one grid for M domains"},{"category":"section","location":"solve-workflow/#Why-nominal-arc-length,-and-not-[0,-1]?","page":"The solve workflow","text":"The reference axis is a gauge choice: any shared, monotone, lane-independent parametrization gives identical results, so L_textref — and indeed the choice of the nominal realization itself — is arbitrary as far as the physics goes. Nominal arc length is chosen for four practical reasons:\n\nA deterministic model reduces to the plain scalar solve exactly. With row widths taken as nominal lengths, a scalar build has L_0c = A_c, hence tau_c equiv 1 and u equiv s. The reference machinery becomes the identity map, so a deterministic run takes the very same steps as a direct scalar solve — not merely the same answer. A normalized axis would re-parametrize every deterministic solve as well.\nThe absolute step floor stays physical. SolverParams.h_min is an absolute minimum step in units of the propagation coordinate (rtol/atol apply to the Jones matrix, and h_init/h_max default to fractions of the interval, so those are gauge-free). In metres it means the same physical floor on every fiber; on a normalized axis its physical meaning would scale with fiber length.\ntau_cj keeps its meaning. As a ratio of physical to nominal segment length it is dimensionless and sits near 1 — it is the per-segment strain, and it is the slot that carries the ensemble spread. Under a normalized axis it would instead carry the fiber's length.\nBreakpoints, per-interval statistics, and progress reporting stay in metres, and so do any user-supplied step controls.","title":"Why nominal arc length, and not 0 1?"},{"category":"section","location":"solve-workflow/#Stage-3-—-the-ensemble-generator","page":"The solve workflow","text":"This is the innermost method: how M scalar generators become one Particles-valued generator that the standard solver integrates unchanged.","title":"Stage 3 — the ensemble generator"},{"category":"section","location":"solve-workflow/#The-change-of-variables,-step-by-step","page":"The solve workflow","text":"Work inside a single segment c and a single lane j. Three facts set it up:\n\nLane j's segment was built with physical arc length A_cj, so its segment-local coordinate runs q_j in 0 A_cj, measured from the segment entry. The scalar term code evaluates lane j's physics at q_j — curvature, frame, phases — in that segment's own parametrization.\nThe solver, however, will advance along the shared reference coordinate u in u_0c u_0c + L_0c, which is the nominal segment's arc length.\nSo we need a map u mapsto q_j(u) carrying the reference interval onto lane j's physical interval.\n\nTake the affine map — affine meaning a linear function of u plus a constant offset, s = alpha u + beta, so the map is a uniform scaling with a shift and nothing higher order; its slope dsdu = alpha is therefore the same constant everywhere in the segment:\n\ntau_cj = fracA_cjL_0c\nqquad\nq_j(u) = tau_cjbigl(u - u_0cbigr) \n\nCheck the endpoints: u = u_0c gives q_j = 0 (segment entry), and u = u_0c + L_0c gives q_j = A_cj (segment exit). The map is increasing and bijective on the segment, so it is a legitimate change of integration variable, and\n\nfracdq_jdu = tau_cj quadtext(constant within the segment)\n\nNow re-parametrize the solution. Define hatJ_j(u) = J_jbigl(q_j(u)bigr) — the same Jones matrix, read at the reference coordinate instead of the physical one. The chain rule gives\n\nfracdhatJ_jdu\n   = fracdJ_jdsbigg_s = q_j(u) cdot fracdq_jdu\n   = tau_cj K_jbigl(q_j(u)bigr) hatJ_j(u) \n\nTwo things are worth stating explicitly, because they are the whole trick:\n\nNothing is stretched physically. K_j is still evaluated at the true physical position q_j inside lane j's own built geometry. The factor tau_cj is the Jacobian of the coordinate change, not a modification of the physics: it accounts for the fact that one unit of u covers tau_cj metres of lane j's fibre.\nIt is exact, not an approximation. Integrating the re-parametrized equation over u_0c u_0c + L_0c produces exactly the same segment-exit Jones matrix as integrating the original equation over 0 A_cj; a change of variables cannot change the value of the solution, only the coordinate it is indexed by.\n\nSegments concatenate cleanly. Segment c's exit is segment c+1's entry at the shared breakpoint u_0c + L_0c = u_0(c+1), and hatJ_j is continuous there, so walking the segments in order along u reproduces lane j's entire physical solve. The sensitivity rides along unchanged: u does not depend on omega, so partial_omega commutes with the re-parametrization, and hatG_j = partial_omega hatJ_j obeys\n\nfracdhatG_jdu\n   = tau_cjBigl( K_j hatG_j + K_omega j hatJ_j Bigr) \n\nwhich is the ordinary coupled sensitivity system with the same single factor tau_cj in front. (This is the same reference-coordinate device the scalar solve already uses for length-changing meta — see Deriving the Generators — applied per lane.)\n\nWorked example: a straight authored at 1.0 m, built at 0.98 m for particle 1 and 1.02 m for particle 2. The segment occupies u in 0 10. At u = 05, particle 1 is evaluated at its physical 0.49 m with tau = 098; particle 2 at 0.51 m with tau = 102. Halfway along the reference interval is halfway through the segment for every lane, whatever its physical length — which is exactly why one breakpoint grid serves all lanes.","title":"The change of variables, step by step"},{"category":"section","location":"solve-workflow/#Stacking-the-lanes","page":"The solve workflow","text":"Write P_cj(u) for lane j's scalar contribution in segment c — the summed birefringence terms, already scaled by tau_cj:\n\nP_cj(u) = tau_cj K_jbigl(q_j(u)bigr) \n\nBoth indices are needed: c says which authored segment is in play, j says whose realization of it. Inside the closure c is pinned by routing u on the nominal grid, so only j varies and the code carries P as a vector over lanes.\n\nEverything lane-dependent is prepared once, before any solving:\n\n# One scalar build per lane, plus the nominal build that defines the reference grid.\nlanes      = [Fiber(scalarize(model, j)) for j in 1:M]      # Stage 1\nlane_paths = [lane.path for lane in lanes]                  # each a Path\ngrid       = Fiber(_nominal_realization(model)).path        # the nominal Path\n\nRow c of lane_paths[j] is lane j's realization of nominal row c of grid; the tables agree in length and order because topology is deterministic. The generator closure is then:\n\nfunction ensemble_generator_K(lanes, lane_paths, grid, λ_m::Real)\n    K = [generator_K(lane, λ_m) for lane in lanes]   # each lane's own scalar generator\n    return function (u::Real)\n        c   = _route_cell_index(grid, u)   # nominal grid: one row index for all lanes\n        u0c = grid.u0[c]                   # segment entry on the reference axis\n        L0c = grid.L0[c]                   # nominal segment width\n\n        P = Vector{SMatrix{2,2,ComplexF64,4}}(undef, length(lanes))\n        for j in eachindex(lanes)\n            p  = lane_paths[j]                         # lane j's own Path\n            τ  = p.L0[c] / L0c                         # reference → physical stretch\n            uj = p.u0[c] + τ * (u - u0c)               # same fraction of lane j's row c\n\n            # Lane j's own scalar generator, reading its own curvature, placement\n            # frame, Bishop axes, spin/twist phases, and connector shape.\n            P[j] = τ * K[j](uj)\n        end\n        return P                           # lane j's 2×2 is P[j]\n    end\nend\n\n_generator_K_lanes (fiber-ensemble.jl) returns P as _Lanes, the lane-major form the solver integrates. Stacked entrywise — lane j's 2×2 becoming particle j of every Complex{Particles} entry — the same values are _generator_K_ensemble, the reference view.\n\nTwo details the sketch makes explicit. First, tau is computed against the nominal width L0c: a lane's own table rows take their widths from that same lane's build, so within a lane a row's width is already its physical arc length. The stretch only exists relative to the shared grid. Second, _route_cell_index returns the table-row index that addresses the matching segment in every lane; routing skips zero-length rows, so L0c > 0 and the stretch never divides 0/0.\n\nK_omega\n\nis assembled identically from each lane's generator_Kω. The per-lane evaluation is the same segment-local term code the scalar solve uses, which is where per-particle exactness comes from: the ensemble generator contains no physics of its own, only routing, scaling, and stacking.\n\nWhy one call integrates M problems. The solver's kernels — the closed-form 2 times 2 exponential, the product with J, the phase-insensitive error — map over the lanes, applying to lane j exactly the arithmetic a scalar solve applies. Lanes never mix, so one integration is the M scalar integrations run in lockstep. Particles arithmetic is elementwise across the particle index in the same way, so integrating the Particles-valued generator gives the same bits; a test pins that. The comparisons step control needs are reduced across lanes with the _mcm_p* helpers, so no code path forks on any individual lane's value.\n\nThe one shared object is the adaptive step sequence. The step-doubling error metric (phase_insensitive_error) is reduced across lanes with a worst-lane (pmax) reduction, so each step is sized for the currently hardest particle and every lane is integrated at least to the requested tolerance.\n\nJ, G, stats = propagate_piecewise_sensitivity(K, Kω, breaks_nominal; params)\n\nOne call over the whole fiber, with J0 the identity on every lane. J and G come out as lanes and are stacked into Complex{Particles} entries; lane j's exact Jones matrix and sensitivity are .particles[j] of every entry. See propagate_piecewise_sensitivity.","title":"Stacking the lanes"},{"category":"section","location":"solve-workflow/#Stage-4-—-observables","page":"The solve workflow","text":"Observables are computed from the ensemble (J G) exactly as in the scalar workflow:\n\nDGD via output_dgd_2x2 — a closed-form Hermitian eigenvalue spread of H = -iJ^-1G, arithmetic only, hence lane-exact.\nCommon-mode phase via common_mode_phase, which consumes each lane's own physical length — evaluated per lane and stacked, like the generator.\nThe launched-state response is derived from the ensemble J by plain arithmetic, so it equals the per-lane derivation entry for entry.\n\nResults carry the nominal fiber build for reporting and plotting alongside the Particles-valued observables.","title":"Stage 4 — observables"},{"category":"section","location":"solve-workflow/#Accuracy:-what-\"exact-per-particle\"-means","page":"The solve workflow","text":"Lane j of every output equals an independent scalar solve of scalarize(model, j) up to solver tolerance. The only numerical object shared between lanes is the step sequence, and it is worst-lane controlled, so sharing can only make individual lanes more accurate than their standalone solves at the same tolerance, never less.\n\nThis claim is enforced, not assumed: the gold-standard tests author a model from raw sample vectors, run the ensemble path once, and re-run every lane as an ordinary scalar build-and-solve of the same authored inputs — comparing Jones entries to 10^-7 (absolute) and DGD to 10^-5 (relative), lane by lane, across a topology matrix that covers every segment type, meta form, connector placement (interior, chained, strained, uncertain target), and spin/twist combination, several spin runs included.","title":"Accuracy: what \"exact per particle\" means"},{"category":"section","location":"solve-workflow/#Choosing-rtol-from-an-output-accuracy","page":"The solve workflow","text":"SolverParams' rtol bounds the error of each step, not of the result. Choose it from the accuracy you need at the output, measured there.\n\nFor the Jones matrix (PTF), measure the worst-case output-SOP error: the rotation angle of J_mathrmref^dagger J on the Poincaré sphere, which is the largest angle between the two outputs over every input state, mueller_to_axis_angle(mueller(JonesMatrix(...)))[2]. Against an rtol = 1e-11 reference on the 10.24 km route of issue #182 (three 30 m spools of 25 cm diameter, conduit bends, gentle transits; spin period 12 m; core axis ratio 1.02; 1550 nm; J-only):\n\nrtol steps worst-case SOP error\n1e-7 813 1.0e-5°\n3e-8 907 7.1e-7°\n1e-8 1 011 2.7e-7°\n1e-9 (default) 1 324 5.0e-8°\n\nRead the table from the requirement: a 1° PTF budget with 0.25° for the solver is met at every rtol shown, by four orders of magnitude or more; a fibre of length L has the share 025 cdot L  1024mathrmkm. test/test_spun_fiber_closed_form.jl checks that share against the closed form of a spun straight fibre, and test/benchmark reproduces the table (run_benchmarks.jl acc full). The reference shares the solver, so the table measures convergence, not truth. The default stays 1e-9.","title":"Choosing rtol from an output accuracy"},{"category":"section","location":"solve-workflow/#Cost","page":"The solve workflow","text":"Relative to M fully independent scalar solves, this workflow performs the same M cheap geometry builds and the same total amount of generator arithmetic (each solver step does M-wide work), but pays the solver overhead — step-size search, interval and breakpoint bookkeeping, closure setup — once instead of M times, and shares one step sequence instead of re-discovering M nearly identical ones. Relative to any shared-geometry approximation, it adds only the M scalar builds — and removes the approximation entirely. In wall time the two are about even: on the 1 km route of test/benchmark a 100-lane J-only ensemble costs 2.8 ms per lane, a scalar solve 2–3 ms (4-core Xeon container), each lane's own generator being most of the cost.","title":"Cost"},{"category":"section","location":"solve-workflow/#Design-rules-this-workflow-relies-on","page":"The solve workflow","text":"The geometry layer is scalar-only. Particles live in the FiberModel; build and every connector solver run on plain reals. Per-particle exactness comes from running that scalar machinery per lane, never from threading Particles through it.\nTopology is deterministic. Uncertainty may change any continuous input but never segment count, order, or kind — this is what fixes C, makes rows align by index, and lets one breakpoint grid serve every lane.\nStrain is applied once, at build. :T_K/:tension length changes (including connector target-length re-solves) happen inside each lane's build; the reference-grid stretch tau_cj then carries them into the solve exactly.\nThe propagator is branch-free and lossless. Jones factors stay in SU(2); step control uses phase_insensitive_error with _mcm_p* lane reductions; no code path branches on a single lane's value; tolerances are explicit (SolverParams) rather than hidden globals.\nBreakpoints are never stepped across, and they come from the nominal grid, shared by all lanes.","title":"Design rules this workflow relies on"},{"category":"section","location":"solve-workflow/#Appendix:-on-the-three-coordinate-systems","page":"The solve workflow","text":"Carrying s, u, and q at once invites the question of whether the calculation could be done in just one of them. It cannot — but the reason is narrower than \"three coordinate systems are required\", and it is worth separating what is forced from what is convention.\n\nThere are three concerns, and only two of them need a live coordinate:\n\nWhere the solver is. Must be shared by every lane, so that one adaptive step sequence, one interval list, and one set of breakpoints serve all of them.\nWhere the physics is evaluated. Must be lane-specific and segment-local, because curvature, the bend axis in the Bishop frame, and the twist and spin phases are defined along one particular realized segment, parametrized from its own entry.\nWhat is physically measured. Arc length in metres — the metric in which the true equation dJds = KJ is written, and in which lengths and phases accumulate.\n\nConcerns 1 and 2 genuinely conflict: one demands a lane-independent variable, the other a lane-dependent one. No single coordinate satisfies both, which is exactly why a Jacobian appears between them.","title":"Appendix: on the three coordinate systems"},{"category":"section","location":"solve-workflow/#What-breaks-if-you-pick-just-one","page":"The solve workflow","text":"Physical arc length s alone. Lane j runs from 0 to L_j, and every interior boundary sits at a different s per lane, so the integration limits and the breakpoints would themselves be Particles. \"One step from s to s + h\" is not well defined when the lanes disagree about where the segment ends. This forces M separate solves — which is precisely the cost the workflow exists to avoid.\nReference coordinate u alone. The domain problem disappears, but nothing can be evaluated: the physics is not a function of u. Recovering K requires mapping to a physical position anyway. Re-expressing each lane's geometry directly in u just performs that map by hand, and forfeits the reuse of the existing scalar term code that makes this design cheap.\nSegment-local arc length q alone. The same wall as s, one level down: the segment exit A_cj is lane-dependent, so a shared step again has no shared endpoint. q also has no global ordering of its own.","title":"What breaks if you pick just one"},{"category":"section","location":"solve-workflow/#What-is-forced,-and-what-is-convention","page":"The solve workflow","text":"The forced minimum is a lane-shared stepping variable, a lane-specific segment-local evaluation position, and the Jacobian relating them. This is the reference-element and isoparametric-map pattern familiar from finite elements: a reference domain shared by all elements, a local chart per element in which the shape functions are written, and the physical domain carrying the metric.\n\nThree honest qualifications about how the trio appears in the implementation:\n\ns is not a live solver variable. It never indexes a step. It survives as the ratio tau_cj and as per-segment entry offsets (the table columns twist_entry for twist along physical length, and spin_material0 for spin along the material length that strain leaves invariant) for the phases that accumulate along it. It is better described as the metric being preserved than as a coordinate being traversed.\nThe globalness of u is bookkeeping. The solver never steps across a breakpoint; it already restarts at each one. A single global u axis is therefore just C per-segment reference intervals concatenated so the propagator sees one breaks vector and one running (J G). Solving segment by segment on a shared 0 1 and composing by matrix product would be the same mathematics with more plumbing.\nq could be narrowed. If segment-shape queries took a normalized position rather than a physical one, u could map straight to a fraction of the segment and q would drop out of those queries entirely. Quantities that accumulate per unit length — twist rate times length, spin phase, the beta integral behind common_mode_phase — still need metres, so q would narrow rather than vanish.","title":"What is forced, and what is convention"},{"category":"section","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#fiber-cross-section","page":"FiberCS","text":"Transverse step-index fiber geometry: converts material properties into guided index, dispersion, nonlinearity, and the local birefringence response coefficients consumed by the fiber assembly layer.","title":"FiberCS"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS.FIBER_BANDS","page":"FiberCS","text":"FIBER_BANDS\n\nThe standard FiberBands singleton, FiberBands(). Access a band's ValidRange by field, e.g. FIBER_BANDS.C.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS.FIBER_BANDS"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS.SMF28_ATTENUATION_VALIDITY","page":"FiberCS","text":"SMF28_ATTENUATION_VALIDITY\n\nCalibration band for smf28_attenuation_dB_per_km: the datasheet points span 1310–1625 nm and the fit is used across the G.652 operating window, 1260–1700 nm. Bounds are an estimate, like the material-model validity ranges.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS.SMF28_ATTENUATION_VALIDITY"},{"category":"type","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS.CrossSectionReport","page":"FiberCS","text":"CrossSectionReport\ncross_section_report(xs::StepIndexCrossSection; λ_m = 1550e-9, T_K = 297.15)\n\nEagerly computed optical properties of a StepIndexCrossSection at vacuum wavelength λ_m (metres) and temperature T_K (kelvin).\n\nFields (SI units):\n\ncross_section: the reported cross section.\nλ_m, T_K: the evaluation point. T_K may carry MCM Particles.\nn_phase, n_group: phase and group indices of the guided mode, frozen draw stress included (n_phase, n_group).\nnormalized_frequency: the V number.\nnumerical_aperture: NA.\nis_single_mode: whether V is below the LP11 cutoff.\ncutoff_wavelength_min_m, cutoff_wavelength_max_m: LP11 cutoff wavelength (m) evaluated at the temperature-ensemble extremes (cutoff_wavelength requires a scalar temperature, so an uncertain T_K is bracketed with _mcm_pmin/_mcm_pmax rather than collapsed to its mean); the two coincide for a deterministic T_K.\ndispersion_parameter: chromatic dispersion D (s/m²).\ngroup_velocity_dispersion_parameter: β₂ (s²/m).\nmode_area_eff_m2: effective mode area (m²).\nbirefringence_core_noncircularity_per_m: signed Δβ (1/m) from the stored ellipticity axis ratio.\nbeat_length_core_noncircularity_m: 2π / |Δβ| (m; Inf for a circular core).\n\nNumeric properties lift through MCM Particles when T_K does.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS.CrossSectionReport"},{"category":"type","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS.FiberBands","page":"FiberCS","text":"FiberBands()\n\nStandard ITU-T wavelength bands used in optical fiber communications, each stored as a ValidRange in metres:\n\nBand Description Wavelength range\nO Original 1260–1360 nm\nE Extended 1360–1460 nm\nS Short wavelengths 1460–1530 nm\nC Conventional 1530–1565 nm\nL Long wavelengths 1565–1625 nm\nU Ultralong wavelengths 1625–1675 nm\n\nSee fiber_band to look up the band letter for a given wavelength.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS.FiberBands"},{"category":"type","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS.StepIndexCrossSection","page":"FiberCS","text":"StepIndexCrossSection(core_material, cladding_material, core_diameter_m,\n                      cladding_diameter_m; kwargs...)\n\nIdeal step-index fiber cross section.\n\ncore_diameter_m and cladding_diameter_m are diameters in meters. The optional ellipticity_axis_ratio is dimensionless and is expressed as major/minor, so it must be at least one. ellipticity_axis_angle is the major-axis angle in radians in the local transverse frame. The manufacturer and model_number fields are informational and do not enter the optical calculations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS.StepIndexCrossSection"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS.HI1060-Tuple{}","page":"FiberCS","text":"HI1060(; ellipticity_axis_ratio=1.0, ellipticity_axis_angle=0.0)\n\nPreset for Corning HI 1060 single-mode fiber, optimized for 980–1060 nm applications. Geometry: 5.3 µm core diameter, 125 µm cladding diameter, GeO2 core dopant ~5.0 mol%, pure-silica cladding. The intended operating wavelength (~1060 nm) is below the material model's Sellmeier validity range (1300–1700 nm), so HI 1060 properties are validated at the lower edge of that range; the geometry and numerical aperture still match the published datasheet within tolerance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS.HI1060"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS.SMF28-Tuple{}","page":"FiberCS","text":"SMF28(; ellipticity_axis_ratio=1.0, ellipticity_axis_angle=0.0)\n\nPreset for Corning SMF-28-class single-mode telecom fiber (ITU-T G.652.D). Geometry: 8.2 µm core diameter, 125 µm cladding diameter, GeO2 core dopant ~3.6 mol%, pure-silica cladding. Calibrated to reproduce the published mode-field diameter at 1310 / 1550 nm and the single-mode cable cutoff (< 1260 nm) within datasheet tolerance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS.SMF28"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS._acoustic_mode_terms-Tuple{StepIndexCrossSection, Any, Any}","page":"FiberCS","text":"_acoustic_mode_terms(cs::StepIndexCrossSection, λ_pump, T_K; max_iter=20, rtol=1e-10)\n\nSelf-consistent guided L01 acoustic mode under the backward-Brillouin Bragg condition, by fixed-point iteration (1–3 steps for SMF-like fibres):\n\nv_eff ← v_core\nrepeat:\n    Ω_B   = 4π·n_eff·v_eff / λ_pump\n    V_a   = _acoustic_v_number(a, Ω_B, v_core, v_cladding)\n    U_a   = waveguide_factor(V_a)\n    β_a   = √((Ω_B / v_core)² − (U_a / a)²)\n    v_eff = Ω_B / β_a\nuntil |Δv_eff| / v_eff < rtol\n\nn_eff is the pump's guided-mode phase index n_phase.\n\nReturns a NamedTuple (v_eff, v_core, v_cladding, V_a, U_a, nu_B, iterations) — velocities in m/s, nu_B (the Brillouin shift) in Hz. λ_pump (m) and T_K (K) must lie in the model validity window; core and cladding must be SilicaGermaniaGlass (so velocity_acoustic applies). Uncertain T_K lifts through Particles — the cutoff and convergence guards reduce over the ensemble extrema.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS._acoustic_mode_terms"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS._acoustic_v_number-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"FiberCS","text":"_acoustic_v_number(a, Ω_B, v_core, v_cladding) -> V_a\n\nAcoustic V-number of the L01 mode of a step-index fibre — the acoustic analogue of the optical V = (2π a  λ)NA. a is the core radius (m), Ω_B the acoustic angular frequency (rad/s), v_core/v_cladding the bulk sound velocities (m/s). Requires v_core < v_cladding for every realization so the mode is core-guided.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS._acoustic_v_number"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS._birefringence_bending-Tuple{StepIndexCrossSection, Any, Any}","page":"FiberCS","text":"_birefringence_bending(fiber, λ, T_K; bend_curvature_per_m)\nbirefringence_bending(fiber, λ, T_K; bend_curvature_per_m)\nbirefringence_bending_dω(fiber, λ, T_K; bend_curvature_per_m)\n\nSigned bend-stress linear birefringence.\n\nλ is the vacuum wavelength in meters, T_K is the temperature in kelvin, and bend_curvature_per_m is the bend curvature κ = 1/R in 1/m (0 for a straight fiber). The value Δβ is in 1/m. The derivative dΔβdω is in s/m.\n\nΔβ ∝ κ², so a straight fiber returns exactly zero without a branch. See _birefringence_bending_coefficient for the factored form the fiber layer's generator uses.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS._birefringence_bending"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS._birefringence_bending_coefficient-Tuple{StepIndexCrossSection, Any, Any}","page":"FiberCS","text":"_birefringence_bending_coefficient(fiber, λ, T_K) -> (C, C_dω)\n\nCurvature-squared coefficient of the bend-stress linear birefringence: Δβ = Cκ², with C in meters (1/m per (1/m)²) and C_dω in s·m.\n\nThe whole magnitude is polynomial in the curvature κ — the bend radius enters the underlying stress only as 1/R = κ — so factoring the κ² out lets a caller that also holds the direction of curvature cancel it. The curvature vector's transverse components satisfy k_x² + k_y² = κ² identically, so with (cos 2φ, sin 2φ) = ((k_x²−k_y²)/κ², 2k_xk_y/κ²)\n\nΔβ·cos 2φ = C·(k_x² − k_y²)        Δβ·sin 2φ = C·2k_xk_y\n\n— the form the fiber layer's bend generator uses. See _bend_axis_vector_at.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS._birefringence_bending_coefficient"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS._birefringence_core_noncircularity-Tuple{StepIndexCrossSection, Any, Any}","page":"FiberCS","text":"_birefringence_core_noncircularity(fiber, λ, T_K; axis_ratio)\nbirefringence_core_noncircularity(fiber, λ, T_K; axis_ratio)\nbirefringence_core_noncircularity_dω(fiber, λ, T_K; axis_ratio)\n\nSigned linear birefringence from core noncircularity.\n\nλ is the vacuum wavelength in meters, T_K is the temperature in kelvin, and axis_ratio is the dimensionless major/minor core-axis ratio. The value Δβ is in 1/m, positive when the major axis is slower. The derivative dΔβdω is in s/m.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS._birefringence_core_noncircularity"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS._birefringence_tension_axial-Tuple{StepIndexCrossSection, Any, Any}","page":"FiberCS","text":"_birefringence_tension_axial(fiber, λ, T_K; bend_curvature_per_m, axial_tension_N)\nbirefringence_tension_axial(fiber, λ, T_K; bend_curvature_per_m, axial_tension_N)\nbirefringence_tension_axial_dω(fiber, λ, T_K; bend_curvature_per_m, axial_tension_N)\n\nSigned axial-tension contribution to bend-axis linear birefringence.\n\nλ is the vacuum wavelength in meters, T_K is the temperature in kelvin, bend_curvature_per_m is the bend curvature κ = 1/R in 1/m (0 for a straight fiber), and axial_tension_N is the axial tension in newtons. The value Δβ is in 1/m. The derivative dΔβdω is in s/m.\n\nΔβ ∝ κ·F, so a straight fiber contributes exactly zero. Unlike the bend term the curvature enters linearly, so the magnitude carries |κ| and the fiber layer keeps a boundary guard at κ = 0 rather than cancelling it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS._birefringence_tension_axial"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS._birefringence_thermal_stress_asymmetric-Tuple{StepIndexCrossSection, Any, Any}","page":"FiberCS","text":"_birefringence_thermal_stress_asymmetric(fiber, λ, T_K; axis_ratio)\nbirefringence_thermal_stress_asymmetric(fiber, λ, T_K; axis_ratio)\nbirefringence_thermal_stress_asymmetric_dω(fiber, λ, T_K; axis_ratio)\n\nSigned linear birefringence from asymmetric frozen-in thermal stress.\n\nλ is the vacuum wavelength in meters, T_K is the temperature in kelvin, and axis_ratio is the dimensionless major/minor core-axis ratio. The value Δβ is in 1/m. The derivative dΔβdω is in s/m.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS._birefringence_thermal_stress_asymmetric"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS._cross_section_is_circular-Tuple{StepIndexCrossSection}","page":"FiberCS","text":"_cross_section_is_circular(xs::StepIndexCrossSection) -> Bool\n\nReturn whether the transverse core is exactly circular (ellipticity_axis_ratio == 1), in which case every core-ellipticity birefringence term vanishes identically.\n\nReduces through _mcm_iszero_all, so an MCM Particles axis ratio answers with a plain Bool and no scalar coercion: an ensemble counts as circular only when every particle is exactly one. This is the guard the fiber generators consult before evaluating [birefringence_core_noncircularity] and [birefringence_thermal_stress_asymmetric].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS._cross_section_is_circular"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS._cross_section_label-Tuple{FiberCrossSection}","page":"FiberCS","text":"_cross_section_label(xs::FiberCrossSection) -> String\n\nHuman-readable identity for a cross section, for use in Base.show methods: \"manufacturer model_number\" when both are set, else whichever of the two is set, else the concrete type name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS._cross_section_label"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS._n_LP01_phase_eff_and_dω-Tuple{StepIndexCrossSection, Any, Any}","page":"FiberCS","text":"_n_LP01_phase_eff_and_dω(fiber, λ, T_K)\n_n_LP01_phase_eff(fiber, λ, T_K)\n_n_LP01_phase_eff_dω(fiber, λ, T_K)\n_n_LP01_group_eff(fiber, λ, T_K)\n\nPhase index βk_0 of the bare LP₀₁ mode (dimensionless), its derivative dndω (seconds), and its group index n + ωdndω (dimensionless). λ is the vacuum wavelength in meters and T_K is the temperature in kelvin.\n\nThese omit the frozen draw stress, so they are the guided-mode index only where LP₀₁ alone is the reference; the guided mode's index is n_phase.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS._n_LP01_phase_eff_and_dω"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS._normalized_frequency-Tuple{StepIndexCrossSection, Any, Any}","page":"FiberCS","text":"_normalized_frequency(fiber, λ, T_K)\n\nNormalized frequency V = a k_0 NA and its ω-derivative, computed together since both share the numerical aperture. λ is the vacuum wavelength in meters and T_K is the temperature in kelvin. Returns (V, dV/dω).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS._normalized_frequency"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS._numerical_aperture-Tuple{StepIndexCrossSection, Any, Any}","page":"FiberCS","text":"_numerical_aperture(fiber, λ, T_K)\nnumerical_aperture(fiber, λ, T_K)\nnumerical_aperture_dω(fiber, λ, T_K)\n\nReturn the numerical aperture and its angular-frequency derivative.\n\nλ is the vacuum wavelength in meters and T_K is the temperature in kelvin. numerical_aperture is dimensionless. numerical_aperture_dω returns dNAdω in seconds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS._numerical_aperture"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS._photoelastic_response_to_twist_b-Tuple{StepIndexCrossSection, Any, Any}","page":"FiberCS","text":"_photoelastic_response_to_twist_b(fiber, λ, T_K; twist_rate_rad_per_m)\nphotoelastic_response_to_twist_birefringence(fiber, λ, T_K; twist_rate_rad_per_m)\nphotoelastic_response_to_twist_birefringence_dω(fiber, λ, T_K; twist_rate_rad_per_m)\n\nSigned circular birefringence from the photoelastic response to mechanical twist.\n\nThis is only the photoelastic circular component. The geometric rotation of material axes is handled separately by axis transport, through twist_phase.\n\nλ is the vacuum wavelength in meters, T_K is the temperature in kelvin, and twist_rate_rad_per_m is the mechanical twist rate in rad/m.\n\nThe birefringence value is Δβ_c = β_LC - β_RC in 1/m. The derivative dΔβ_cdω is in s/m.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS._photoelastic_response_to_twist_b"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS._propagation_constant-Tuple{StepIndexCrossSection, Any, Any}","page":"FiberCS","text":"_propagation_constant(fiber, λ, T_K)\npropagation_constant(fiber, λ, T_K)\npropagation_constant_dω(fiber, λ, T_K)\n\nGuided-mode propagation constant.\n\nλ is the vacuum wavelength in meters and T_K is the temperature in kelvin. propagation_constant returns β in 1/m. propagation_constant_dω returns dβdω in s/m.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS._propagation_constant"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS.aeff-Tuple{Any}","page":"FiberCS","text":"aeff(V; variant = :marcuse_gaussian)\n\nFundamental-mode effective area normalized to the core radius squared, A_effr₀² = π(wr₀)², where w is the Gaussian spot size (1/e field radius, equivalently 1/e² intensity). Multiply by r_core(fiber)^2 for the area in square meters, or take r_core(fiber)·sqrt(aeff(V)/π) for w.\n\nV is the normalized frequency. variant selects the spot-size convention:\n\n:marcuse_gaussian (default) — three-term Marcuse Gaussian approximation wr₀ = 065 + 1619V^32 + 2879V^6 (Marcuse, J. Opt. Soc. Am. 68, 103 (1978), Eq. 15, doi:10.1364/JOSA.68.000103); ~1% for 1.2 ≤ V ≤ 2.4.\n:petermann — Petermann-II effective spot size, the Marcuse Gaussian form extended by (0016 + 1561V^7) (Hussey and Martinez, Electron. Lett. 21, 1103 (1985), doi:10.1049/el:19850783); ~1% for 1.5 ≤ V ≤ 2.5.\n\nPure arithmetic in V, so it lifts through MCM Particles; V is reduced with _mcm_pmin/_mcm_pmax only for the range warnings.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS.aeff"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS.brillouin_freq_shift-Tuple{StepIndexCrossSection, Any, Any}","page":"FiberCS","text":"brillouin_freq_shift(cs::StepIndexCrossSection, λ_pump, T_K) -> Hz\n\nBackward-SBS Bragg shift ν_B = 2n_effv_A  λ_pump, with v_A the guided L01 effective acoustic velocity from the self-consistent mode solve. λ_pump is the vacuum pump wavelength in metres and T_K the temperature in kelvin; both must lie in the model validity window (1–2 μm, 200–300 K). Returns ν_B in Hz. Uncertain T_K lifts through Particles.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS.brillouin_freq_shift"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS.core_kerr_coefficient-Tuple{StepIndexCrossSection, Any, Any}","page":"FiberCS","text":"core_kerr_coefficient(fiber, λ, T_K)\n\nReturn the Kerr nonlinear-index coefficient n2 of the fiber core material.\n\nλ is the vacuum wavelength in meters and T_K is the temperature in kelvin. The returned Kerr coefficient is in m²/W.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS.core_kerr_coefficient"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS.cutoff_wavelength-Tuple{StepIndexCrossSection, Any}","page":"FiberCS","text":"cutoff_wavelength(fiber, T_K; λ_min, λ_max, atol, maxiter)\n\nBisect for the LP11 cutoff wavelength. The search bracket [λ_min, λ_max] defaults to the core material's wavelength validity window (runtime_range(fiber.core_material).λ). All wavelength arguments and the returned cutoff wavelength are in meters. T_K is the temperature in kelvin.\n\nnote: MCM compatibility\nT_K must be a scalar (not Particles). The bisection's signbit branching is not defined per-particle; call with pmean(T_K_uncertain) when the temperature is uncertain. The return is always Float64.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS.cutoff_wavelength"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS.dispersion_parameter-Tuple{StepIndexCrossSection, Any, Any}","page":"FiberCS","text":"dispersion_parameter(fiber, λ, T_K; dλ = 0.1e-9)\n\nReturn the chromatic-dispersion parameter computed by finite differencing the guided-mode phase index n_phase over wavelength.\n\nλ and dλ are in meters and T_K is the temperature in kelvin. Returned unit: s/m^2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS.dispersion_parameter"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS.eccentricity_squared-Tuple{Any}","page":"FiberCS","text":"eccentricity_squared(axis_ratio)\n\nReturn 1 - 1axis_ratio^2 for a canonical major/minor axis ratio.\n\naxis_ratio and the returned eccentricity squared are dimensionless.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS.eccentricity_squared"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS.fiber_band-Tuple{Any}","page":"FiberCS","text":"fiber_band(λ) -> Symbol\n\nReturn the ITU-T band letter (:O, :E, :S, :C, :L, or :U) whose range in FIBER_BANDS contains λ (metres). Throws an ArgumentError via _check_range if λ falls outside the combined O–U range [1260, 1675] nm.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS.fiber_band"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS.group_velocity_dispersion_parameter-Tuple{StepIndexCrossSection, Any, Any}","page":"FiberCS","text":"group_velocity_dispersion_parameter(fiber, λ, T_K; dλ = 0.1e-9)\n\nReturn the group-velocity-dispersion parameter inferred from dispersion_parameter.\n\nλ and dλ are in meters and T_K is the temperature in kelvin. Returned unit: s^2/m.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS.group_velocity_dispersion_parameter"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS.is_single_mode-Tuple{StepIndexCrossSection, Any, Any}","page":"FiberCS","text":"is_single_mode(fiber, λ, T_K)\n\nReturn whether the normalized frequency is below the LP11 cutoff.\n\nλ is the vacuum wavelength in meters and T_K is the temperature in kelvin. The returned value is a Boolean.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS.is_single_mode"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS.mode_area_eff-Tuple{StepIndexCrossSection, Any, Any}","page":"FiberCS","text":"mode_area_eff(fiber, λ, T_K; variant = :petermann)\n\nReturn the fundamental-mode effective area in square meters.\n\nλ is the vacuum wavelength in meters and T_K is the temperature in kelvin. Physical-units wrapper around aeff: aeff(V; variant) * r_core(fiber)^2, where V is the fiber's normalized_frequency.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS.mode_area_eff"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS.n_difference-Tuple{StepIndexCrossSection, Any, Any}","page":"FiberCS","text":"n_difference(fiber, λ, T_K)\n\nReturn the dimensionless relative index difference (n_core - n_cladding)n_cladding.\n\nλ is the vacuum wavelength in meters and T_K is the temperature in kelvin.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS.n_difference"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS.n_group-Tuple{StepIndexCrossSection, Any, Any}","page":"FiberCS","text":"n_group(fiber, λ, T_K)\n\nReturn the group index of the guided mode, n_g = n + ωdndω, with n the phase index n_phase, frozen draw stress included.\n\nλ is the vacuum wavelength in meters and T_K is the temperature in kelvin. The returned group index is dimensionless.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS.n_group"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS.n_phase-Tuple{StepIndexCrossSection, Any, Any}","page":"FiberCS","text":"n_phase(fiber, λ, T_K)\n\nReturn the phase index of the guided mode, n = β_LP01k_0 + δn: the LP₀₁ index from propagation_constant plus the frozen axisymmetric draw-stress shift δn of n_shift_stress_thermal, which needs no applied load.\n\nλ is the vacuum wavelength in meters and T_K is the temperature in kelvin. The returned index is dimensionless.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS.n_phase"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS.n_phase_dω-Tuple{StepIndexCrossSection, Any, Any}","page":"FiberCS","text":"n_phase_dω(fiber, λ, T_K)\n\nReturn dndω, in seconds, of the guided-mode phase index n_phase.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS.n_phase_dω"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS.n_shift_stress_thermal-Tuple{StepIndexCrossSection, Any, Any}","page":"FiberCS","text":"n_shift_stress_thermal(fiber, λ, T_K)\n\nThe return value is the common-mode (polarization-averaged) refractive-index shift (dimensionless) at wavelength λ (meters) and temperature T_K (kelvin) from the axisymmetric frozen draw stress set up by the core/cladding thermal-expansion mismatch.\n\nThis is the monopole (Δm = 0) companion of the differential [birefringence_thermal_stress_asymmetric] quadrupole: it raises both polarization modes equally rather than splitting them. The implemented formula is\n\nδn = −(n³/4)·q·[(1−2ν)/(1−ν)·(α_core−α_clad)·(T_soft−T)·(p₁₁+p₁₂)\n                  + 2·(p₁₂−ν(p₁₁+p₁₂))·(α_core−α_clad)·(T_soft−T)]\n\nwhere q = 1 − u²/V² is the modal confinement factor (modal_prefactor). The first bracketed term is the plane-stress transverse mean (keeps p₁₁ + p₁₂, no ellipse-asymmetry factor, survives for a circular core); the second is the axial photoelastic contribution from the frozen axial stress, with the same p₁₂ − ν(p₁₁ + p₁₂) factor as the applied-tension shift [n_shift_tension_axial].\n\nApproximations carried by this formula (not corrected here):\n\nModal weighting by q = 1 − u²/V² instead of the true guided-mode/stress overlap integral (Steininger et al., doi:10.12688/openreseurope.19414.2).\nMean CTE of the cross section approximated as α_clad (a few-percent error).\nStresses in the infinite-cladding limit (corrections O((a/R_clad)²)).\nUniform E and ν across core and cladding.\n\nVanishes identically when core and cladding share a thermal-expansion coefficient. Pure arithmetic on material constants, so it lifts through MCM Particles when T_K is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS.n_shift_stress_thermal"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS.n_shift_stress_thermal_dω-Tuple{StepIndexCrossSection, Any, Any}","page":"FiberCS","text":"n_shift_stress_thermal_dω(fiber, λ, T_K)\n\nReturn dδndω, in seconds, of the draw-stress shift n_shift_stress_thermal. The material stress constants depend on T_K alone, so the dispersion is that of n_core³ and the modal prefactor q.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS.n_shift_stress_thermal_dω"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS.n_shift_tension_axial-Tuple{StepIndexCrossSection, Any, Any}","page":"FiberCS","text":"n_shift_tension_axial(fiber, λ, T_K; axial_tension_N)\n\nThe return value is the common-mode (polarization-averaged) refractive-index shift (dimensionless) at wavelength λ and temperature T_K from a uniform applied axial tension axial_tension_N (N).\n\nApplied tension strains the whole cross section uniformly, so — unlike the bend-coupled differential [birefringence_tension_axial], which scales as 1/R and vanishes on a straight fiber — this isotropic shift is present whether or not the fiber is bent and needs no modal overlap weighting. It is the standard fiber-Bragg-grating strain-optic shift δn = −(n³/2)[p₁₂ − ν(p₁₁ + p₁₂)]·ε_z with axial strain ε_z = F/(π·r_clad²·E) (photoelastic constant p_e = (n²/2)[p₁₂ − ν(p₁₁ + p₁₂)]).\n\nReturns 0 for zero tension. Pure arithmetic on the material constants, so it lifts through MCM Particles when axial_tension_N or T_K is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS.n_shift_tension_axial"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS.normalized_frequency-Tuple{StepIndexCrossSection, Any, Any}","page":"FiberCS","text":"normalized_frequency(fiber, λ, T_K)\n\nNormalized frequency V = a k_0 NA (dimensionless). λ is the vacuum wavelength in meters and T_K is the temperature in kelvin.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS.normalized_frequency"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS.normalized_frequency_dω-Tuple{StepIndexCrossSection, Any, Any}","page":"FiberCS","text":"normalized_frequency_dω(fiber, λ, T_K)\n\nDerivative dVdω of the normalized frequency, in seconds. λ is the vacuum wavelength in meters and T_K is the temperature in kelvin.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS.normalized_frequency_dω"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS.smf28_attenuation-Tuple{Any}","page":"FiberCS","text":"smf28_attenuation(λ) -> 1/m\n\nEmpirical Corning SMF-28e+ power attenuation coefficient α in 1/m, converted from smf28_attenuation_dB_per_km by α[1/m] = α[dB/km] / (10·log₁₀ e) / 1000.\n\nThis is the form the nonlinear effective-length expressions consume, L_eff = (1 − e^{−αL}) / α. Broadcast over a wavelength array with smf28_attenuation.(λs). See smf28_attenuation_dB_per_km for the empirical-fit caveat and the validity band.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS.smf28_attenuation"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS.smf28_attenuation_dB_per_km-Tuple{Any}","page":"FiberCS","text":"smf28_attenuation_dB_per_km(λ) -> dB/km\n\nEmpirical Corning SMF-28e+ power attenuation α(λ) = 0.78/(λ[μm])⁴ + 0.065, in dB/km.\n\nNot a first-principles model — a two-parameter fit (Rayleigh 1/λ⁴ plus an IR-absorption floor) to the SMF-28e+ datasheet. Representative values: ≈0.33 dB/km at 1310 nm, ≈0.20 dB/km at 1550 nm, ≈0.18 dB/km at 1625 nm. λ (metres) must lie in the calibration band SMF28_ATTENUATION_VALIDITY; pass a measured attenuation where accuracy matters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS.smf28_attenuation_dB_per_km"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS.waveguide_factor-Tuple{Any}","page":"FiberCS","text":"waveguide_factor(V)\nwaveguide_factor_dV(V)\n\nReturn the scalar LP01 waveguide factor approximation and its derivative with respect to normalized frequency V.\n\nBoth V and waveguide_factor(V) are dimensionless. waveguide_factor_dV returns dgdV and is also dimensionless.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS.waveguide_factor"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fiber-cross-section/#Bifrost.FiberCS.β_shift_stress_bend-Tuple{StepIndexCrossSection, Any, Any}","page":"FiberCS","text":"β_shift_stress_bend(fiber, λ, T_K; bend_curvature_per_m)\n\nCommon-mode (polarization-averaged) propagation-constant shift (1/m) at wavelength λ (meters) and temperature T_K (kelvin) for a fiber bent to curvature bend_curvature_per_m = κ = 1/R (1/m; 0 for straight).\n\nThis is the monopole (Δm = 0) companion of the differential bend retardance [birefringence_bending]: it raises both polarization modes equally rather than splitting them, so it stays out of the traceless generator and leaves the differential phase and DGD unchanged. It is the second-order effective-index change of the fundamental mode in the conformally transformed index profile of a bent fiber, n(x)·(1 + x/R_eff) (Kiiveri et al., doi:10.1117/1.OE.61.12.126106, Eqs 14 and 20). The combined geometric-curving and elasto-optic effective bend radius is R_eff = R/(1 + k) with k = −(n²/2)·[p₁₂ − ν(p₁₁ + p₁₂)] (Kiiveri Eqs 7 and 18; R_eff ≈ 1.280·R for fused silica). The photoelastic combination p₁₂ − ν(p₁₁ + p₁₂) is the same axial strain-optic factor that sets the applied-tension shift [n_shift_tension_axial].\n\nThe tilt is odd in the bend coordinate, so the mode-averaged first-order shift vanishes at the neutral axis; the surviving shift comes from the mode displacing into the higher-index outer side of the bend. A displaced-Gaussian variational estimate with the Marcuse Gaussian fundamental-mode field radius w (the 1/e field radius r_core·sqrt(aeff(V)/π) behind [aeff]) gives\n\nΔβ = k₀³ · n³ · w⁴ / (8 · R_eff²)    (≥ 0).\n\nThe 1/8 prefactor is a Gaussian-mode estimate; a rigorous value follows from a full bent-mode solution (Kiiveri §8). The shift scales as (w/R)² relative to β, so it is small for gentle bends and vanishes as R → ∞.\n\nReturns 0 for a straight fiber. Pure arithmetic on the material constants and the Marcuse radius, so it lifts through MCM Particles when T_K is (the bend radius is taken from the Float64 path geometry).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Bifrost.FiberCS.β_shift_stress_bend"}]
}
